    <node id="&quot;POLYETHER-POLYURETHANE FOAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyether-Polyurethane Foam is a type of polyurethane foam, which is a mass-produced plastic with high environmental resistance and low biodegradability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyether-polyurethane foam is a type of plastic that Tenebrio molitor larvae can degrade."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyether-polyurethane foam is degraded by yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), affecting their gut microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyether-polyurethane foam is a material degraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, with significant impacts on their gut microbiome."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;YELLOW MEALWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">YELLOW MEALWORMS, the larval stage of Tenebrio molitor beetles, are renowned for their ability to biodegrade various types of plastics. These larvae play a critical role in addressing plastic pollution by converting some plastics into carbon dioxide and excreting the rest as feces, commonly referred to as frass, which can be converted into biochar for various applications. Their digestive systems enable them to break down materials such as polystyrene (PS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polypropylene (PP), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polylactic acid (PLA), polyether-polyurethane foam (PU), and other polymers through depolymerization processes facilitated by their intestinal flora.

Research has demonstrated that YELLOW MEALWORMS can ingest and degrade plastics, including disposable surgical masks made from polypropylene materials. They are capable of completing their life cycle even on plastic-based diets, though their survival rates and pupation rates may be lower compared to traditional food sources like bran. Despite this, their gut microbial communities remain resilient to plastic-based diets, showing greater adaptability than those of other insect species, such as superworms.

The gut microbiota of YELLOW MEALWORMS contribute significantly to the depolymerization of plastics like PS and PU foams, and experiments have isolated previously unreported PS-degrading bacterial strains from their digestive systems. Studies also investigate the effects of different mask layer diets on the growth, survival, and gut bacterial and fungal communities of the larvae, focusing not only on their plastic degradation abilities but also on changes in their gut microbiomes during these processes.

Overall, YELLOW MEALWORMS represent a promising biological solution for managing plastic waste. While they exhibit significant weight changes and mortality rates depending on the type of plastic consumed, highlighting both their potential and limitations in plastic waste management, their ability to consume and degrade plastics underscores their importance in efforts to address environmental plastic pollution. This process involves both microbe-dependent and independent mechanisms in the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39598f698094d1663ca3438bc143bc90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81b6237c84f002117fbfe7575d9cdd6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff228c7711f94fb27152b187b1fa3eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FTIR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FTIR) is a versatile analytical technique used to obtain an infrared spectrum of absorption or emission. FTIR plays a critical role in studying and analyzing chemical changes in various materials, particularly polymers and plastics, during processes such as biodegradation, hydrocarbon degradation, and the impact of dietary factors. It identifies major functional groups in materials like polystyrene (PS) feedstock, frass samples, polypropylene (PP), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and other materials, characterizing their structural changes.

FTIR is widely employed to detect the scission of specific bonds, such as ether and urethane bonds, providing qualitative evidence of degradation and oxidation in materials like polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polyether-polyurethane foam, polystyrene (PS), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and microplastics. The technique confirms the biodegradation of plastics, including PET and PS, by analyzing changes in their chemical composition and functional groups after ingestion by organisms like mealworms (*T. molitor* and *Z. atratus* larvae) or during other degradation processes. FTIR also detects new functional groups, such as C-O stretching and alcohol groups, indicating degradation and partial oxidation of polymers.

FTIR serves as a critical tool for characterizing plastic degradation, confirming oxidation processes, and providing detailed information about the chemical structure and changes occurring in various materials during degradation. Its applications span multiple fields, including environmental science, polymer chemistry, and biotechnology, making it indispensable for studying the interactions between materials and their environments. By analyzing the infrared spectrum of absorption, FTIR offers insights into the chemical bonds and functional groups present in samples, enabling researchers to understand the mechanisms of degradation and the impact of biological or environmental factors on material properties. 

Additionally, FTIR is used to confirm the breakdown of benzoic rings in polystyrene and reveals different degradation pathways of PP occurring in the guts of *T. molitor* and *Z. atratus* larvae, further demonstrating its utility in understanding complex degradation processes in biological systems. Overall, FTIR is a powerful technique for analyzing chemical bonds, structures, and functional groups in a wide range of materials, contributing significantly to advancements in material science and environmental studies. It is instrumental in analyzing the physiochemical properties of materials, detecting changes such as the formation of new functional groups, and confirming chemical modifications in egest</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;XPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Extruded Polystyrene (XPS) is another type of PS product, often denser than EPS, used in coffee cups and packaging."&lt;SEP&gt;"Extruded Polystyrene Foam (XPS) is another type of polystyrene foam used for insulation and packaging."&lt;SEP&gt;"X-ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy (XPS) is used to analyze changes in the surface chemical composition and functional groups of HDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"X-ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy (XPS) using Thermo Scientific K-Alpha equipment for surface chemical component investigation."&lt;SEP&gt;"X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy, a technique used to characterize the elemental content and chemical composition of sample surfaces."&lt;SEP&gt;"XPS (X-ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy) is a process that helps identify changes in the chemical composition of polyether-polyurethane foam after ingestion by yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"XPS (X-ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy) is a surface analysis technique used to examine the chemical composition of polyether-PU foam before and after biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"XPS (X-ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy) is a technique used to analyze the surface chemistry of PS films after incubation with bacterial strains."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TGA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"TGA (thermogravimetric analysis) confirms oxidation and biodegradation of PP in both insect larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA) is a versatile analytical technique used to study thermal modifications, stability, and degradation of materials, particularly plastics. TGA measures weight changes in materials as a function of temperature, providing insights into their composition, thermal properties, and decomposition characteristics. This method has been widely applied to confirm plastic degradation, such as polyether-polyurethane foam and polystyrene (PS), both before and after biodegradation processes, including their transformation into frass through larval digestion.

TGA is instrumental in analyzing the thermal stability of various materials, including polyether-polyurethane foam processed by yellow mealworms, surgical mask layers, and residues. It detects weight loss during thermal decomposition, supporting studies on polymer modification and degradation under different atmospheres, such as nitrogen and air. Additionally, TGA is often used alongside techniques like FTIR to confirm biodegradation processes. The method characterizes thermal changes from polystyrene feedstock to frass samples, comparing pristine Styrofoam to its degraded form after larval digestion. Overall, TGA provides critical evidence for understanding the thermal behavior and breakdown mechanisms of polymers and plastics in diverse applications.&lt;SEP&gt;"Thermogravimetric analysis employed to study thermal stability and degradation behavior of LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thermogravimetric analysis, a technique used to study thermal stability and degradation of materials."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GPC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"GPC (Gel Permeation Chromatography) is a technique used to analyze molecular weight changes during polystyrene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"GPC (gel permeation chromatography) is a technique used to analyze molecular weight distributions of polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;GPC (Gel Permeation Chromatography) is a versatile analytical technique primarily used to separate and analyze molecules based on their size. It plays a critical role in polymer science by determining molecular weights, molecular weight distributions, and changes in these properties due to degradation processes. GPC has been widely employed to study various polymers, including polyurethane (PU), expanded polystyrene (EPS), high-impact polystyrene (HIPS), polystyrene (PS), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), and Styrofoam.

This technique is instrumental in confirming the decreased molecular weight of polymers such as polyether-polyurethane foam, indicating depolymerization. However, it cannot directly analyze pristine polyether-polyurethane foam due to its insolubility. Instruments like the Agilent 1260 have been utilized to measure molecular weights across different polymers. GPC is also applied to assess biodegradation processes, analyzing molecular weight changes in plastics ingested by organisms such as worms and superworms, including those treated with antibiotics. It studies the molecular weight distribution of fecula and frass produced during degradation.

GPC analyzes molecular weight changes in polymers extracted from frass and feedstock, and it evaluates the impact of molecular weight changes in polyethylene during biodegradation. The method is used to study molecular weight changes in foam plastics after they pass through larval guts and measures molecular weight distributions of polymers such as PS during degradation. Additionally, GPC analyzes molecular weight changes in Styrofoam and its degradation products in frass, as well as molecular weight changes in plastics before and after digestion by yellow mealworms.

Despite some limitations, such as potential interference from metabolites, GPC remains an essential tool for evaluating depolymerization patterns in materials like surgical mask layers and understanding the breakdown mechanisms of polymers under various conditions. Overall, GPC provides valuable insights into the behavior and transformation of polymers, making it indispensable in both laboratory and biological contexts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIODEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biodegradation is the process by which organic substances are broken down into simpler compounds by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;**Biodegradation** is the natural biological process by which organic substances, including both naturally occurring and synthetic materials such as plastics, are broken down into simpler compounds by living organisms. This process plays a critical role in environmental sustainability by reducing waste accumulation and facilitating the recycling of nutrients back into ecosystems.

The breakdown primarily involves microorganisms, such as bacteria, fungi, and other biological agents like mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae) and superworms (*Zophobas atratus* larvae). These organisms produce enzymes capable of degrading complex organic materials, ranging from natural substances to synthetic ones like low-density polyethylene (LDPE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), polystyrene (PS), polypropylene (PP), polyether-polyurethane foam, and polyethylene (PE) microplastics. For example, HDPE can be broken down by specific microbial strains, resulting in weight loss and structural changes. Additionally, certain organisms like mealworms have demonstrated the ability to break down challenging materials such as expanded-polystyrene or polyether-polyurethane foam, achieving this through enzymatic actions or microbial interactions within their digestive systems.

Factors influencing biodegradation include polymer characteristics, storage conditions, and the presence of suitable microorganisms or enzymes. Microbial strains such as WJ2020 and WJ2025 have been identified as effective in breaking down PS films, further demonstrating the wide applicability of biodegradation processes. Biodegradation encompasses not only the breakdown of organic materials by microorganisms but also enzymatic depolymerization or microbial attack within the larvae's guts.

Overall, **biodegradation** is a comprehensive term that includes the breakdown of organic materials by microorganisms or other biological agents, contributing significantly to nutrient cycling and waste management. It ensures the continuous cycling of nutrients and contributes to the health and balance of ecosystems. Bacteria play a crucial role in breaking down plastics, making biodegradation essential for maintaining ecological balance and mitigating environmental pollution caused by synthetic materials. Biodegradation remains a focus of study regarding how plastics can be broken down by living organisms, offering promising solutions for environmental challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation refers to the breakdown of organic substances into simpler forms by biological agents such as bacteria or fungi."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation refers to the process by which microorganisms break down complex organic substances into simpler forms."</data>
