    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ETHER BONDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chemical bonds in polyether PU foam assumed to be resistant to microbial attack but may undergo oxidative reactions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ether Bonds are part of the chemical structure of polyether-polyurethane foam and are cleaved during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ether bonds are specific types of chemical bonds (-C-O-C-) found in polyether-PU foam, which are hydrolyzed during the biodegradation process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;URETHANE BONDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Urethane Bonds are another type of bond in polyether-polyurethane foam that can be broken down during biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STAPHYLOCOCCUS EPIDERMIDIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial species shown to degrade synthetic polystyrenes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Staphylococcus epidermidis is a bacterium strain that can modify the surface properties of polyether-polyurethane polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Staphylococcus epidermidis strain KH can grow on polyether-PU foam, causing its surface degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ALTERNARIA SP. PURDK2&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Alternaria sp. PURDK2 is a fungus capable of degrading polyether-polyurethane polymer, resulting in weight loss by cleaving urethane bonds."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLADOSPORIUM TENUISSIMUM A2.PP.5 AND A3.1.1&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cladosporium tenuissimum A2.PP.5 and A3.1.1 are fungi with high polyether-polyurethane degradation abilities, causing significant weight loss."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLADOSPORIUM PSEUDOCLADOSPORIOIDES T1.PL.1&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Another fungal strain with significant polyether-PU degradation capabilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cladosporium pseudocladosporioides T1.PL.1 is a fungus with high polyether-polyurethane degradation abilities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYESTER-POLYURETHANE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyester-Polyurethane is another form of polyurethane, known for higher biodegradability due to ester bonds hydrolyzable by microorganisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOLOGICAL MARKER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biological markers indicate the presence or extent of biodegradation processes in organisms exposed to plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Changes in the gut microbiome composition serve as biological markers indicating the impact of polyether-polyurethane foam on the digestive system of yellow mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLEAVING THE URETHANE-BONDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A chemical reaction involving the breaking of urethane bonds, contributing to the degradation of polyether-PU."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLADOSPORIUM TENUISSIMUM A2.PP.5&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A fungal strain with high polyether-PU degradation abilities, capable of causing up to 65% weight loss in plastics after 21 days of incubation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SUPERWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Superworms (Zophobas aratus) have gut microbes involved in the biodegradation of polypropylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms (Zophobas atrinus) are involved in the biodegradation of polypropylene through gut-microbe-dependent depolymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms (Zophobas morio) contribute to plastic biodegradation via enzymatic activities in their gut."&lt;SEP&gt;**Superworms (Zophobas atratus)** are the larval stage of the beetle species *Zophobas atratus*, which belongs to the family Tenebrionidae. These insects are renowned for their ability to consume and biodegrade recalcitrant plastics, including polypropylene, polystyrene, low-density polyethylene, and even polyurethane foams. Superworms are larger and heavier than mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*) and can be bred across multiple generations, making them a unique subject in scientific research.

Their plastic-degrading capabilities are attributed to both their own enzymatic activity and the presence of gut microbiota, such as *Pseudomonas aeruginosa*. Studies have demonstrated that superworms can survive and thrive on diets composed solely of Styrofoam, converting it into carbon dioxide and frass—a byproduct that supports plant growth. When fed plastic, superworms exhibit distinct gut microbial responses compared to other larvae, such as yellow mealworms, and demonstrate higher survival rates under such conditions.

Superworms show a preference for polystyrene foam over other types of plastic and are capable of ingesting and degrading various forms of plastic through gut microbe-dependent depolymerization. This makes them an intriguing subject for research into plastic waste management and potential solutions for environmental challenges. Additionally, superworms are studied not only for their environmental impact but also for their nutritional value, highlighting their versatility in scientific exploration.&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms are the larvae of Zophobas atratus, studied for their ability to degrade various plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms, the larvae of Zophobas atratus, exhibit the ability to degrade polypropylene with the assistance of their gut microbes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WAX MOTH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Larvae of Galleria mellonella, involved in plastic consumption and degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Larvae of Galleria mellonella, shown to prefer pristine plastics over waste electrical and electronic equipment (WEEE) plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wax moth is an insect from which Aspergillus flavus was isolated, contributing to the study of plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The wax moth Galleria mellonella contributes to polyethylene degradation through its larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wax Moth (WM) is another insect larva species utilized in the experiment to explore the effects of plastic ingestion."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SNAILS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SPECIES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Achatina fulica, a species involved in plastic consumption and degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica, contributing to plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Snails are invertebrates capable of biofragmenting ingested plastics into smaller particles."&lt;SEP&gt;"Snails are another group of invertebrates shown to ingest microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HONEYBEES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Insects identified for their role in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insects that have been identified as contributing to plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYETHYLENE (PE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyethylene is the most produced polymer globally, making up a large portion of plastic waste."&lt;SEP&gt;**Polyethylene (PE)** is a widely produced synthetic polymer derived from petroleum, known for its durability, high molecular weight, hydrophobic nature, and resistance to degradation. It constitutes approximately 29.6% of global plastic production and finds extensive use in various applications, including packaging materials, plastic bags, mulching films, flame retardants, and other products. Polyethylene exists in different variations such as High-Density Polyethylene (HDPE), Linear Low-Density Polyethylene (LLDPE), and Low-Density Polyethylene (LDPE), each characterized by differences in molecular weights and degrees of crystallinity.

Despite being generally considered non-biodegradable due to its hydrophobic nature and stability, certain microorganisms, insects, and enzymes have demonstrated the ability to degrade polyethylene. Mealworm larvae (*Tenebrio molitor*), particularly yellow mealworms, can consume and partially digest polyethylene, converting up to 49.0 ± 1.4% into CO₂ through microbial action within their gut microbiome. Specific bacteria such as *Kosakonia*, *Bacillus sp.* YP1, and *Enterobacter asburiae* VT1, along with fungi like *Aspergillus flavus* PEDX3, contribute to the degradation process. The structural similarity of polyethylene to lignin makes it potentially degradable by certain enzymes, although it remains non-hydrolyzable and poses challenges due to its inherent stability.

Polyethylene undergoes molecular weight changes during biodegradation and produces degradation products such as long-chain alkanes. Aged forms of polyethylene are more susceptible to degradation by *Tenebrio molitor* larvae. Experiments involving the feeding of polyethylene to mealworms have revealed significant effects on their protein content and growth, highlighting the complex interactions between this plastic and biological systems. Compared to polypropylene (PP), polyethylene is considered more easily degraded, though it still presents challenges due to its durability. Residual particles remain after biodegradation, indicating that the process is not fully complete.

Polyethylene remains suitable for mechanical recycling, enhancing its lifecycle management potential. In research contexts, polyethylene has been studied alongside other plastics, such as expanded polystyrene (EPS), in the mealworm diet to explore its degradation capabilities and understand insect-plastic interactions. Ongoing studies aim to reduce its environmental impact, particularly its contribution to microplastics and pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf662c8e784d14a3a4dba2e89041aa4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af5eb6a8326b4977801e2f7694a2c750&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18