    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE (PS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene (PS) is a widely used synthetic aromatic hydrocarbon polymer derived from petroleum, with styrene as its monomer unit. It has the molecular formula [-CH(C6H5)CH2-]n and is characterized by its rigidity, versatility, resistance to microbial degradation, and the presence of a benzene ring in its structure. Polystyrene typically appears white but can be produced in various forms, such as solid or expanded polystyrene (EPS), and exhibits a density of approximately 0.016 g/cm³. Its defined molecular weight characteristics allow it to be processed using techniques like extrusion and injection molding, making it highly applicable in modern industries and daily life products.

Polystyrene finds widespread use in products such as glass frames, plastic cups, building insulation, packaging materials, containers, disposable plastics, food packaging, cutlery, CD cases, household appliances, takeout boxes, and various other industrial commodities. Its properties of good thermal insulation and radiation resistance make it particularly valuable for these uses. Globally, polystyrene accounts for 7% of total plastic consumption, making it a major plastic pollutant widely used in packing, building, and food processing industries.

Despite its general resistance to microbial breakdown, polystyrene can serve as a carbon source for certain microorganisms under specific conditions. Mealworm larvae (*Tenebrio molitor*), superworm larvae (*Zophobas atratus*), and darkling beetle larvae (*Tenebrio obscurus*) have demonstrated the ability to consume and degrade polystyrene, especially when it is in the form of Styrofoam or expanded polystyrene (EPS). Scientific studies involving polystyrene have utilized insect larvae to test its degradation, showing that these larvae can use polystyrene as their sole carbon source through depolymerization within their gut systems. This process leads to the mineralization of polystyrene into carbon dioxide, generating metabolic by-products that are analyzed to understand decomposition mechanisms. The ingestion of polystyrene also influences the gut microflora of *T. molitor* larvae, highlighting its significance in biodegradation research.

However, while mealworm larvae can metabolize polystyrene to some extent, it is insufficient as a primary source of mass and energy for their growth and development. Studies indicate that polystyrene affects protein and nitrogen levels in the insects and</data>
