    <node id="&quot;ACINETOBACTER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial genus implicated in the biodegradation of polyethylene in the gut of wax moth larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter is a genus of bacteria that includes species capable of degrading polystyrene, as demonstrated by an isolated strain from Tribolium castaneum larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Isolated from red flour beetle, contributing to PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter is a bacterium significantly associated with factors influencing gut microbiome assembly."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter is a genus of bacteria, one species of which has been isolated for its ability to degrade polystyrene from Tribolium castaneum larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter is a genus of bacteria capable of degrading polystyrene, isolated from the larvae of Tribolium castaneum."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter, isolated from red flour beetles, participates in polystyrene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter is a genus of bacteria, one species of which has been isolated from Tribolium castaneum larvae for its ability to degrade polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter is a genus of bacteria present in the gut microbiome of PP-fed larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter rises to 9.65% in PP-fed groups, suggesting its role in PP degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter was one of the dominant genera in polyolefin-fed groups, suggesting its involvement in polyolefin degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"A genus of Gram-negative bacteria capable of adhering to abiotic surfaces."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter is a genus of bacteria that includes strains capable of degrading polystyrene and other plastics."</data>
