    <node id="&quot;MINERALIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mineralization is the conversion of organic matter into inorganic compounds through microbial activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mineralization is the first phase in the Kjeldahl method where samples are broken down at high temperatures."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mineralization refers to the biological process by which polystyrene is broken down in the larval intestine, contributing to lipid formation."&lt;SEP&gt;**MINERALIZATION**

Mineralization is a biological and chemical process where complex organic materials are converted into simpler inorganic compounds through biodegradation. This transformation involves the breakdown of organic substances into basic inorganic forms, such as carbon dioxide (CO₂), water (H₂O), and other mineral components like chloride ions (Cl⁻). The process plays a critical role in the degradation of various organic materials, including plastics like polystyrene (PS) and PVC.

Microbial activity is central to mineralization, with microorganisms facilitating the conversion of ingested organic matter into inorganic compounds. This process can occur in environments such as the gut of organisms like mealworms and superworms, where microbial communities break down complex organic materials, such as Styrofoam, into CO₂. Studies have shown that up to 36.7% of ingested Styrofoam carbon can be mineralized into CO₂ during this process.

Mineralization represents the final step in biodegradation, ensuring the complete decomposition of organic materials by transforming intermediate degradation products into inorganic compounds. It is an essential process for environmental sustainability, enabling the breakdown of complex organic substances into harmless inorganic forms. Overall, mineralization refers to the complete degradation of organic materials into simple inorganic compounds, primarily facilitated by microbial action. This process is observed in various contexts, including the biodegradation of plastics and the natural cycling of organic matter in ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;"The process where organic materials are converted into their mineral components, often observed in the breakdown of plastics by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mineralization is the process where organic matter is converted into inorganic compounds, often involving microbial activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mineralization is the process where organic materials are converted into their mineral components."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mineralization is the process where complex organic materials are converted into simpler inorganic compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process of converting organic materials into simpler inorganic compounds, relevant to plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mineralization refers to the conversion of organic matter into inorganic compounds, often involving microbial activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mineralization is the process by which organic matter is converted into inorganic compounds through microbial activity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-412d934721b214c2cd0565531172171c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81b6237c84f002117fbfe7575d9cdd6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b8cc3aa81b03290b589ccf0a1548ca0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba22196a59b67cbf67af7d76a039af1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a0e22d97687eaef3d31a60f7da6e668&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a6288e1109e