    <node id="&quot;PVC MICROPLASTIC POWDERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PLASTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Type of plastic whose molecular weights decrease significantly due to mealworm feeding."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PLA (Polylactic Acid) is a biodegradable material fed to mealworms. It resulted in a 15% mass loss and had the highest mass loss among materials in Table 2."&lt;SEP&gt;"PLA (polylactic acid) is a biodegradable polyester used in experiments to study its effects on lipid storage and decomposition by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;PLA, or polylactic acid, is a biodegradable thermoplastic polymer derived from renewable resources. It is considered a bio-based and degradable plastic that has been extensively studied for its interactions with various organisms, particularly mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae). PLA shows higher degradation rates compared to non-biodegradable plastics like polyethylene (PE) and other polymers such as PET when processed by T. molitor larvae. These larvae preferentially consume PLA over non-degradable plastics, demonstrating varied consumption rates depending on the conditions.

In studies involving PLA, it was found that the material can accelerate pupation in mealworms when used as their sole diet; however, it prevents adult development. Additionally, PLA has been shown to increase metabolic activity in mealworms and other model insects, but it may also cause a reduction in larval weight under certain conditions, such as when supplemented with specific diets (e.g., resulting in a 28.7% reduction in mean larval weight for black soldier fly larvae).

PLA undergoes enzymatic hydrolysis facilitated by enzymes like polyester hydrolases, lipases, and carboxylesterases, contributing to its degradability. While PLA is fragmented by organisms such as earthworms, there is limited evidence of complete biodegradation in some contexts. Molecular weight measurements have been used to characterize PLA in studies assessing its degradation by plastic-degrading macroinvertebrates, highlighting its potential role in environmental cleanup efforts.

Overall, PLA's ability to be consumed and degraded by organisms like mealworms underscores its significance in research exploring sustainable solutions for plastic waste management. However, its effects on insect growth and physiological function remain complex and context-dependent.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HEAVY METALS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TOXIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Components in plastics affecting mealworm decomposition performance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Heavy metals are toxic elements that can associate with microplastics, causing bioaccumulation and ecological damage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Heavy metals are analyzed in plastics to understand their impact on biodegradation efficiency."&lt;SEP&gt;"Heavy metals are toxic substances often found in marine plastic litter, posing environmental hazards."&lt;SEP&gt;"Heavy metals are metallic elements that can be toxic and harmful to the environment and living organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Heavy metals are metallic elements with high density and potential toxicity to living organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Heavy metals are pollutants that affect insect physiology and genetic costs in contaminated areas."&lt;SEP&gt;"Heavy metals such as Cd, Hg, and Pb were analyzed in the polyurethane composition to assess their potential toxicity to Tenebrio molitor larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYETHER-PU FOAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A polymer material tested as a diet for mealworms, showing physical degradation over time."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of foam material composed of urethane and ether bonds, susceptible to biodegradation via enzymatic hydrolysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyether-PU Foam is a polymer product that undergoes chemical modification due to mealworm ingestion, leading to hydrolysis of urethane bonds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyether-PU foam is a type of plastic material that mealworms can consume, leading to its biodegradation as evidenced by changes in its chemical structure and mass loss."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyether-PU foam is a type of plastic material used as the sole diet for yellow mealworms in degradation experiments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT MICROBIAL COMMUNITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gut Microbial Community encompasses the bacteria and other microorganisms residing in the digestive tract of organisms, analyzed here through 16S rRNA gene sequencing."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut Microbial Community refers to the microorganisms inhabiting the digestive tract of organisms, which were significantly modulated in this study."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbial community consists of microorganisms present in the digestive system of T. molitor larvae, potentially aiding in the biodegradation of polypropylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbial community refers to microorganisms within the digestive system of yellow mealworms that potentially aid in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The microbial community within the gut of Tenebrio species that plays a role in PS biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbial community of larvae is analyzed to understand its role in Styrofoam degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbial community represents the microorganisms residing in the digestive tracts of larvae studied."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbial community of starvation mealworms is more diversified due to ingestion of frass, melted skeletons (containing chitin), dead mealworms, and digestion of body biomass including fats and proteins."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db</data>
    </node>
