    <node id="&quot;FTIR SPECTROMETER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A FTIR spectrometer was used to identify chemical changes in synthetic polymers during digestion by larvae, employing Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectrometer is a device used to analyze changes in functional groups of materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectrometer is used to analyze functional group changes in polyether-PU foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectrometer is an analytical instrument used for studying chemical compositions and changes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy equipment used to identify chemical changes in synthetic polymers during digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"The FTIR spectrometer is a device used for analyzing materials, granted access to by professor Jacek Katzer."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectrometer is used to determine changes in functional groups of PS and PE plastics after biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19868f1234aac10de61c710c8352c076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;T. MOLITOR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">**T. molitor**, scientifically known as *Tenebrio molitor*, is a species of darkling beetle belonging to the genus Tenebrio. The larval stage of this species, commonly referred to as yellow mealworms, plays a significant role in both ecological and scientific contexts due to its unique abilities.

The larvae of *T. molitor* exhibit remarkable capabilities in biodegrading various types of plastics, such as polypropylene (PP), polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), expanded polystyrene (EPS), polyether-polyurethane (PU) foam, polyethylene terephthalate (PET), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), and other synthetic materials like PVC. They are even capable of degrading layers of surgical masks through their gut digestion processes. Their plastic-degrading ability is attributed to intrinsic enzymatic processes that break down certain plastics, particularly PET. However, their efficiency is significantly enhanced by symbiotic interactions with gut microbiota, which facilitate the breakdown of other types of plastics. For instance, they harbor *Aeromonas sp. TM1* in their intestines, contributing to polystyrene degradation. Studies indicate that the specific gut microbes of *T. molitor* play a crucial role in enhanced plastic degradation, particularly in PP biodegradation, where they exhibit superior abilities compared to *Zophobas atratus*. Additionally, the larvae secrete an emulsifying agent that increases the bioavailability of PS, aiding microbial breakdown. Changes in gene expression and enzymatic activities further support their biodegradation capabilities, especially with PP polymers.

Despite their efficient depolymerization abilities, *T. molitor* larvae require supplementary nutrition for sustained survival when fed exclusively on plastic diets. Their growth and survival depend on reserve dynamics and the ability to utilize plastics like PS. Comparative analyses with other insect species, such as *Zophobas atratus* and *Zophobas morio*, have been conducted to assess differences in plastic degradation efficiency and impacts on gut health. Cytotoxicity studies using larval extracts and the publication of a draft genome of *T. molitor* have advanced understanding of its genetic makeup and metabolic processes. In experiments, *T. molitor* larvae demonstrated the ability to ingest LDPE foams while maintaining survival activities, showing higher specific consumption rates compared to *T. obscurus</data>
