    <node id="&quot;PE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PLASTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene (PE) is a petroleum-based synthetic polymer known for its high molecular weight, hydrophobic properties, and resistance to biodegradation. It is characterized by stable C-C and C-H covalent bonds, which contribute to its durability and persistence in the environment. PE exists in various forms, such as low-density polyethylene (LDPE) and linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), differing in terms of branching and crystallinity, which influence how effectively it can be consumed and degraded by organisms like Tenebrio molitor larvae (yellow mealworms) and superworms.

In experiments involving mealworms, PE was used to study both the survival of the larvae and the biodegradation process of this microplastic. Research has demonstrated that when ingested by mealworms, PE undergoes significant depolymerization and chemical modifications facilitated by gut microbes, particularly bacteria from the Enterobacteriaceae family. Mealworms metabolize and physically alter PE by oxidizing it and changing its structure, creating wrinkles and bubble-like protrusions after digestion. Importantly, no nanoplastic accumulation was observed in the frass produced by the larvae, indicating efficient processing of the material. However, despite their ability to degrade PE, mealworms do not derive sufficient nutrients from it to support their growth or development.

The biodegradation of PE leads to the formation of long-chain alkanes and oxygen-containing compounds. Fungi such as Rhodotorula and Cryptococcus, present in yellow mealworms, also play a role in breaking down PE. Although PE is considered non-hydrolyzable and more resistant to degradation than some other plastics, its lack of methyl side chains compared to polypropylene (PP) makes it relatively easier to biodegrade. Nevertheless, the biodegradation of PE by mealworms is relatively slow and limited compared to other plastics like polyethylene terephthalate (PET). The degradation rates vary depending on factors such as the origin of the larvae, dietary factors, and experimental conditions.

Furthermore, PE often contains trace elements like aluminum (Al), calcium (Ca), and hafnium (Hf), which may influence its environmental behavior and interaction with organisms. Studies reveal that PE induces oxidative stress in Tenebrio larvae and other organisms, highlighting both its potential risks and opportunities for waste management. In summary, PE serves as a critical subject of research due to its complex interactions with</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78cd8d4684fa9e0519635dba9f124a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PP (Polypropylene) is a polymer material being investigated for biodegradation potential."&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a thermoplastic polymer with the chemical formula \((\mathrm{C}_{3} \mathrm{H}_{6})_{n}\), characterized as a linear hydrocarbon resin. It forms three distinct chain structures—atactic, isotactic, and syndiotactic—depending on the position of methyl groups along its polymer chains. PP is widely utilized in various industrial and consumer applications due to its versatility and durability, particularly in packaging materials.

In the context of biodegradation studies, PP has been investigated for its potential to undergo depolymerization by gut microbes present in insect larvae such as *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworms) and *Zophobas atratus*. While PP is generally considered non-hydrolyzable and highly resistant to biodegradation due to its crystalline structure, limited evidence suggests that it can be partially consumed and degraded under specific conditions. Mealworms have shown the ability to ingest PP, using it as a carbon source for energy, though it lacks essential nutrients like nitrogen and other elements necessary for larval growth.

Studies indicate that the molecular weight parameters (Mn, Mw, and Mz) of PP change after passing through the larval gut, demonstrating a limited extent of depolymerization. This process appears to depend significantly on microbial assistance within the gut environment, as PP's natural resistance to biodegradation is otherwise inhibitory without such microbial involvement. Despite this, PP ranks as the second most favorable plastic for mealworm consumption compared to others like polystyrene, albeit at lower rates.

Although initial mass loss in mealworms was higher when consuming PP, its overall impact on larval mass over a 30-day period was relatively minimal. Testing with PP foam also confirmed the material's challenging nature for ingestion, reinforcing its inherent difficulty to degrade. As one of five plastics studied, PP highlights the need for further research into its biodegradation mechanisms and their implications for environmental sustainability.

In summary, while PP remains largely resistant to biodegradation, there is promising evidence of limited microbial-assisted depolymerization, suggesting potential pathways for addressing its environmental persistence.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7043eb8aa4221f396a5c65fb6493bd41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">PS, which stands for polystyrene, is a synthetic aromatic polymer composed of carbon and hydrogen. It is widely recognized for its hardness, gloss, and specific mass loss characteristics and exists in forms such as expanded polystyrene foam (EPS) and extruded polystyrene foam (XPS), commonly found in packaging materials like meat, fruits, vegetables, and foam structures like bead and Styrofoam forms. As a petroleum-based plastic, PS plays a critical role in various applications, including packaging, while also being central to scientific studies focusing on biodegradation.

In experiments involving T. molitor larvae (mealworms), PS has been used as a food source. While it can serve as a feeding substrate for these larvae, it lacks essential nutrients such as nitrogen, leading to high mortality rates when larvae are fed solely on PS. Among tested plastics, PS shows the highest protein content but remains insufficient for maintaining life due to the absence of key nutritional elements. However, when supplemented with other components like oatmeal or bran, larval survival improves, though pupation rates may still be affected.

Biological degradation of PS has been observed in larvae such as *Galleria mellonella* (waxworms), mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), superworms, and yellow mealworms (*Zophobas atratus*). These larvae significantly alter the surface of PS through mechanisms involving oxidation, depolymerization, benzene ring cleavage, and mineralization into biomass. Fungi such as Rhodotorula and Cryptococcus have also been associated with PS degradation in yellow mealworms. Gut microbes play a crucial role in this process, and antibiotic suppression affects the depolymerization of PS, indicating the importance of gut microbiota, including bacteria like PM S1 and *Priestia megaterium* S1.

Under normal conditions, PS exhibits non-hydrolyzable properties and is resistant to biodegradation, often accumulating as microplastics in the environment. However, under specific conditions, such as thermal degradation at certain temperatures, it undergoes molecular mass reduction. In summary, PS serves as both a substrate for biodegradation and a feedstock for experiments related to plastic degradation, impacting larval survival, growth, and reproduction while contributing to broader environmental and scientific research efforts.</data>
