    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SEM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"INSTRUMENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"SEM (Scanning Electron Microscopy) is a technique used to analyze morphological differences in frass samples from different mask layers."&lt;SEP&gt;"SEM (Scanning Electron Microscopy) is used to observe the morphological changes in PS films after microbial digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"SEM (scanning electron microscopy) is a technique used to visualize the surface structure of microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) is a versatile technique used to analyze and visualize the surface morphology, structure, and texture of various materials, particularly plastics, at high magnification. SEM captures detailed images of plastic surfaces, revealing their structural characteristics before and after processes such as microbial or insect digestion. This technique has been employed in multiple contexts, including the study of polyether-PU foam, polystyrene (PS), chitosan (CS), and polyethylene (PE) mulching films.

SEM is instrumental in observing the physical changes that occur during the biodegradation of plastics. For instance, it has been used to confirm the reduction in nanoplastic particle size caused by bacterial degradation and to characterize surface morphology changes in polyether-PU foam exposed to mealworms (*T. molitor*). Additionally, SEM studies have revealed pits, cracks, and other signs of degradation on plastic surfaces, providing insights into how gut microbes and larvae break down these materials. The technique also enables researchers to examine the residual plastics found in larval frass and compare the surface topography of treated versus untreated materials.

Beyond plastics, SEM is used to study the shape and structure of bacterial cells, such as those belonging to strains like PELW2042. Its ability to produce high-resolution images makes it invaluable for analyzing surface breakdown in various materials and understanding the mechanisms of biodegradation. In summary, SEM serves as a critical tool for investigating the physical and morphological changes in materials subjected to biological and environmental processes.&lt;SEP&gt;"Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) is employed to observe surface erosion on degraded PS films."&lt;SEP&gt;"Standard Error of the Mean, used to indicate variability in experimental data."&lt;SEP&gt;"Scanning Electron Microscope (SEM) is used to observe micro- and submicroparticles collected on a filter membrane."&lt;SEP&gt;"Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) is utilized to examine the surface morphology of original and residual plastic particles."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f102938f307d7404d3f31b7200d93768&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-576a8e18f3d6ea8c7d4a5f2cd554423c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </node>
