    <node id="&quot;MEALWORM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mealworms are insect larvae capable of digesting plastics, with varying efficiency depending on the type of plastic consumed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are insect larvae with high plastic-degrading capacities, utilized in the study for analyzing biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms, specifically the larval stage of the Tenebrio molitor beetle (commonly known as the darkling beetle) and belonging to the Tenebrionidae family, are insects that have garnered significant scientific interest due to their unique biological capabilities. Mealworms harbor gut bacteria capable of nitrogen fixation, which plays a crucial role in their metabolic nitrogen sources and adaptation to nitrogen-deficient diets. This nitrogen-fixing ability is further linked to their gut microbiota, which includes specific bacterial strains that aid in breaking down various synthetic polymers, such as plastics.

In studies, mealworms have demonstrated the ability to consume and degrade different types of plastics, including expanded polystyrene (EPS), polyethylene (PE), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polypropylene (PP), and polyether-polyurethane (PU) foam. Their plastic-degrading capacity is largely attributed to the activity of their gut microbiome, which actively participates in the breakdown and mineralization of these materials. While mealworms can survive on a plastic-only diet, additional nutrition is necessary for optimal growth and survival. When subjected to restricted or plastic-based diets, mealworms exhibit reduced biomass and survival time due to changes in lipid, carbohydrate, and protein levels. They produce frass, a byproduct with potential applications as fertilizer, during the process of consuming plastics. Additionally, they generate endogenous emulsifying agents that enhance the biodegradation of plastics like EPS.

Mealworms are compared with other beetle larvae, such as superworms (Zophobas morio), in terms of plastic consumption efficiency and resulting impacts. Studies also investigate their effects on growth, survival, and metamorphosis when exposed to microplastics, contributing to a deeper understanding of the ecological implications of microplastic interactions on their development. Overall, mealworms represent an important avenue for exploring solutions to plastic pollution, as their gut microbiome's ability to break down plastics and their adaptability to various diets make them valuable subjects in studies assessing metabolic changes due to plastic exposure, as well as broader investigations into environmental cleanup and sustainable practices.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09d03c0a2dce3f57876d209a83a2e82a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BRAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">BRAN, derived from the hard outer layers of cereal grains, is a plant-based feed ingredient rich in fiber, carbohydrates, and nutrients such as B vitamins. It plays an essential role in insect rearing, particularly for *Tenebrio molitor* larvae (commonly known as mealworms) and superworms. BRAN supports larval growth, survival, and pupation, making it indispensable for these insects to complete their life cycle. When mealworms are fed BRAN, they exhibit significant weight gain compared to those fed plastics like Styrofoam or polystyrene (PS). This makes BRAN an ideal baseline for comparison with plastic-fed groups, providing critical data on gut bacterial composition and enabling researchers to evaluate survival rates, mass changes, and gut microbiome effects against other diets, including plastics.

BRAN has been shown to result in the highest fatty acid content (254.4 g/kg dry matter) in mealworm larvae, positively affecting their nutritional profile. Additionally, it doubles the rate of polystyrene degradation by mealworms and superworms, making it an effective additive for studies exploring the interaction between worms and various types of plastics. However, its supplementation may slightly reduce the efficiency of polyethylene (PE) removal during the incubation process when used in a 1:1 ratio with high-purity PE microplastics. Furthermore, BRAN influences the thermal degradation of PS, with varying effects depending on its ratio to PS in the diet.

BRAN sustains the larvae's ability to process and degrade plastics over extended periods, supporting their health and functionality while enhancing their capacity to consume plastics like PS. It plays a crucial role in influencing the gut microbial communities of mealworms, which are critical for their ability to biodegrade plastics. Overall, BRAN is a vital component in experiments aimed at understanding and improving the degradation of plastics by mealworms, serving not only as a primary feedstock but also as a co-dietary supplement that optimizes the insects' performance in biodegradation processes. Additionally, BRAN is a conventional diet rich in carbohydrates and proteins, often used as a control in experiments to ensure sufficient nutrients for larval growth, and it can be used alongside antibiotics like gentamicin in dietary supplements for larvae experiments. 

In summary, BRAN is a dietary supplement tested for its effects on larval survival and PS consumption, supporting the completion of the life cycle in *T.</data>
