    <node id="&quot;16S RRNA GENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The 16S rRNA gene is used to identify microbial communities through amplicon sequencing."&lt;SEP&gt;The **16S rRNA gene** is a key genetic marker widely used for bacterial identification, classification, and phylogenetic analysis. This conserved gene serves as a critical tool in microbial ecology and microbiology studies due to its ability to provide insights into bacterial diversity and community composition. It has been successfully amplified from all isolates in various studies and is commonly employed through DNA sequencing techniques, including PCR amplicon sequencing, to identify and classify bacterial species.

In specific contexts, such as the study of gut microbiomes, the hypervariable regions (e.g., V3-V4 or V3) of the 16S rRNA gene are often targeted for microbial community analysis. These regions allow for detailed examination of bacterial communities within larval guts, facilitating metagenomic analysis to determine the microbial flora composition in the digestive tracts of organisms like mealworms. Techniques such as denaturing gradient gel electrophoresis (DGGE) have also been applied to assess gut bacterial diversity by amplifying specific regions of the 16S rRNA gene.

The 16S rRNA gene plays a crucial role in constructing phylogenetic relationships among bacterial isolates and confirming the identity of specific strains, such as identifying sample 3 as *Klebsiella oxytoca* ATCC 13182. Its utility spans diverse applications, including analyzing bacterial diversity in gut microbiomes, studying bacterial communities in environmental samples, and serving as a molecular marker for broader ecological investigations. Additionally, the gene is sequenced and analyzed using advanced technologies like Illumina sequencing to identify bacterial species and analyze gut microbial community structures.

Overall, the 16S rRNA gene remains an indispensable tool for understanding bacterial taxonomy, ecology, and evolution, enabling researchers to explore bacterial diversity and community dynamics across various environments and organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b74e88d1f94354055e078b38af6a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94bf67bf1b585ce1050569a2eb0000cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e56bce8206396081e0b7dccc47a10c9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1</data>
