    <node id="&quot;SURVIVAL RATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"HEALTH MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Survival rate indicates the proportion of organisms surviving under specific conditions, showing significant decreases in starvation groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate measures the proportion of larvae remaining alive under different feeding conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;The **SURVIVAL RATE** is a measure that evaluates the percentage of mealworms (often specifically referring to *Tenebrio molitor* larvae) that remain alive during an experimental period. This metric typically reflects the proportion of larvae surviving over time under various conditions, such as dietary changes, exposure to microplastics, or other environmental factors. It serves as an indicator of adaptability, resilience, and overall viability of the larvae throughout the experiment.

The survival rate is commonly assessed over a defined duration, often spanning 35 days, during which researchers monitor how different feeding conditions influence larval health. In some cases, the survival rate demonstrates a downward trend in the final 10 days of the experiment, suggesting potential stressors or limitations in the provided conditions. Additionally, this metric has been used to study the impact of microplastics on mealworm physiology, including oxidative damage caused by exposure to such materials.

Beyond being a straightforward measure of survival, the survival rate also provides insights into the adaptability and resilience of mealworms when exposed to varying diets, including unconventional food sources like plastics. It is further utilized in studies focused on plastic biodegradation, where the health and longevity of larvae are critical indicators of their effectiveness in breaking down plastic waste. Overall, the survival rate acts as both a physiological index and a health measure, reflecting the ability of organisms to thrive under specific experimental conditions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5318125e74217718b470b7721d1d6365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564</data>
