    <node id="&quot;ALPHA DIVERSITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Alpha Diversity measures the diversity of species within a single sample, including indices like Simpson, Ace, Chao1, and Shannon."&lt;SEP&gt;"Measure of microbial diversity within individual samples, analyzed using Majorbio Bio-Pharm Technology Co. Ltd.'s platform."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alpha diversity quantifies the richness and evenness of species within a sample, indicating microbial community complexity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alpha Diversity measures the richness and diversity of gut flora in larvae fed different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alpha diversity measures the complexity of species within a single sample, using indices like Chao1 and Shannon."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alpha diversity measures the diversity of species within a sample, often assessed using metrics like the Inverse Simpson Index."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alpha diversity measures species diversity within the gut microbiome of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alpha diversity measures the diversity within individual samples or communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alpha diversity quantifies the diversity of microbial communities within a sample."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PRINCIPAL COORDINATE ANALYSIS (PCOA)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A multivariate statistical method used to visualize patterns in complex datasets, often applied in microbial community studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Principal Coordinate Analysis (PCoA) reveals differences in gut microbiome composition based on diet using Bray–Curtis dissimilarity indices."&lt;SEP&gt;"Statistical method used to visualize differences in microbial community composition between samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCoA is used to analyze shifts in gut microbial community structures based on diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCoA is a statistical method used to visualize patterns in microbial community composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Principal Coordinate Analysis (PCoA) evaluates microbial composition differences among larvae fed PS, CS, and CB diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Principal Coordinate Analysis (PCoA) is a statistical method used to visualize differences in microbial community compositions based on dietary conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCoA is a statistical method used to analyze and visualize differences in microbial community structures at the operational taxonomic unit (OTU) level."&lt;SEP&gt;"Principal coordinate analysis (PCoA) is a statistical technique used to visualize similarities and differences in microbial communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCoA is a statistical method used to visualize and analyze the similarity or dissimilarity between microbial community samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Principal coordinate analysis is a statistical method for visualizing similarities or differences in data sets, often applied in ecological studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FASTP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SOFTWARE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Tool used for quality filtering of raw FASTQ files during sequence processing."&lt;SEP&gt;"fastp is a bioinformatics tool used for quality control of high-throughput sequencing data, involving the removal of adapter sequences and low-quality reads."&lt;SEP&gt;"fastp is software used for quality enhancement and trimming of raw sequencing reads."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FLASH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SOFTWARE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"FLASH (Fast Length Adjustment of Short Reads) is a computational tool designed to improve genome assemblies by merging paired-end reads."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tool used for merging paired-end reads during sequence processing."&lt;SEP&gt;"FLASH identifies and removes chimeric sequences from 16S rRNA gene sequencing data."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OTU&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"OTU (Operational Taxonomic Unit) measures bacterial community richness and diversity in gut microbiome studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"OTU (Operational Taxonomic Unit) represents a grouping of sequences with similar DNA or RNA sequences, used here to quantify microbial diversity."&lt;SEP&gt;"OTU refers to an operational taxonomic unit, which is highly related to each diet in the context of this study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Operational Taxonomic Unit, a grouping of similar sequences used in microbial community analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"OTU (operational taxonomic unit) represents a classification level used in microbial ecology to group similar organisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;IBM SPSS STATISTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"IBM SPSS Statistics is used for conducting statistical analyses such as ANOVA and pairwise comparisons."&lt;SEP&gt;"Statistical software used for analyzing survival rates and microbial diversity differences."&lt;SEP&gt;"IBM SPSS Statistics is used for statistical tests, employing one-way ANOVAs for significance testing."&lt;SEP&gt;"Statistical software used for conducting ANOVAs and pairwise comparisons in the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MEALWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms, specifically the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor* (commonly known as yellow mealworms) and *Tenebrio obscurus*, are macroinvertebrates belonging to the Tenebrionidae family. These insects also include species such as *Zophobas atratus* and originate from diverse regions like Tai'an, Shenzhen, and Guangzhou, which influence their metabolic capabilities. Mealworms play a significant role in biodegrading plastics, including polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polyether-polyurethane (PU) foam, high-density polyethylene (HDPE), polypropylene (PP), and microplastics such as LDPE, PS, and PVC.

The gut microbiome of mealworms plays a crucial role in facilitating the degradation of these plastics through enzymatic activity. Symbiotic microorganisms in their digestive tract convert plastics into carbon dioxide (CO₂), biomass, and frass, altering the crystalline structure and chemical composition of plastics and reducing their thermal stability. Advanced analytical techniques, such as SEM, FTIR, TGA, DSC, XRD, Raman spectroscopy, UV-Vis spectroscopy, NMR, and mass spectrometry, confirm their capacity to degrade plastics. Changes in the gut microbiome and enzymatic activities enable mealworms to break down materials like polystyrene and polyethylene, allowing them to survive on diets consisting solely of plastics for up to a month, though such diets provide little nutritional value and may stunt their growth compared to traditional food sources like wheat bran or superworm diets.

Mealworms have garnered significant scientific interest due to their unique ability to consume and biodegrade various types of plastics. They are the primary agents responsible for the biodegradation of polystyrene microplastics (PS MPs). They contain functional enzymes and gut bacteria that target and break down polymer chains, particularly shorter chains, altering their molecular weight and distribution. Their ability to biodegrade plastics varies with larval age, influencing microplastic consumption and degradation rates. Younger and older larvae exhibit differences in how they process plastics. Additionally, mealworms exhibit survival adaptations such as cannibalism or alternative feeding strategies when conventional food is scarce.

Mealworms are central to studies focusing on developmental responses and have been used in experiments for studying biodegradation and survival. While their ability</data>
