    <node id="&quot;CANNIBALISM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BEHAVIORAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cannibalism describes the behavior observed in starved or PS-fed mealworms, affecting their survival rates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is a behavior exhibited by Z. atratus larvae under nutrition limitation, contributing to higher cannibal rates."&lt;SEP&gt;"The act of consuming members of the same species, contributing to biological digestive products in the frass samples."&lt;SEP&gt;Cannibalism is a behavioral response observed in mealworms, specifically within the Tenebrio molitor larvae (commonly known as yellow mealworms), that occurs under conditions of nutrient deficiency, stress, or resource scarcity. This behavior involves the consumption of conspecifics—either living, dead, or molted exoskeletons—and serves as an adaptive mechanism to access essential nutrients and energy necessary for survival, growth, and nitrogen assimilation.

When deprived of regular food sources such as bran, wheat bran, dried whey, or plastic substrates like polyethylene (PE) or polystyrene (PS), mealworms resort to cannibalizing each other. This behavior has been documented in both larval and adult stages, with increased instances observed when water and protein content in the substrate are limited. In some cases, cannibalism affects the specific consumption rate of plastics, highlighting its impact on experimental setups where larvae and adults were housed together. To mitigate this behavior, pupae have been removed from populations to protect them from being consumed by larvae.

Cannibalism contributes to the survival of surviving larvae by providing additional energy and nutrients, though it may also lead to reduced survival rates overall due to increased mortality. The behavior can result in changes to biomass, mass, and frass composition, which includes biological digestive products derived from consumed conspecifics. Additionally, cannibalism among mealworms has been noted to occur more frequently in certain variants, such as the PE-reg variant, potentially indicating a behavioral adaptation driven by high pupae densities or environmental pressures.

Overall, cannibalism in T. molitor larvae is an innate behavior triggered by nutritional deficiencies and plays a critical role in their ecological dynamics, affecting population structure, survival rates, and resource utilization. It underscores the importance of adequate dietary resources in maintaining healthy mealworm populations and optimizing their utility in various applications, including biodegradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47c76e5149ac059b1960b533c9e99a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d01d291012bd548fc067e250ec39fba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;URETHANE BOND&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A chemical bond in polyether-PU foam that is subject to hydrolysis, contributing to the degradation of PU foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Urethane bonds are part of the polyether-PU foam structure, and their hydrolysis contributes to the breakdown of the polymer during biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYESTER-PU&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyester-PU is another form of polyurethane foam, differing from polyether-PU in its chemical structure and biodegradation properties."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FRASS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">FRASS refers to the excrement or waste material produced by insect larvae, particularly mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*) and superworms, during their feeding and digestion processes. This material is generated as a result of these larvae consuming and processing various types of plastics, including polyethylene (PE), polypropylene (PP), polystyrene (PS), polyether-polyurethane (PU) foam, expanded polystyrene foam (EPS), Styrofoam, and polyethylene terephthalate (PET). The FRASS encompasses both the excrement and any undigested material resulting from the larvae's diet, especially when they consume plastics like EPS. The excreted material constitutes only a fraction of the initial volume of EPS ingested, potentially around 49.2% in the case of polystyrene, and may contain unprocessed EPS particles along with insect body residues.

The FRASS produced by these larvae exhibits altered levels of nitrogen and minerals due to the consumption of specific plastics such as PS. It reflects structural and chemical changes in the plastics, including reduced molecular weights and evidence of oxidative transformations. Partially oxidized products of PS degradation are found within FRASS, which also contains residual polymer fragments that have undergone degradation, undigested PS particles, and modified polymers. This indicates that the larvae can chemically modify certain polymers during digestion through enzymatic or microbial activity in their digestive systems.

Research on FRASS often focuses on its role in evaluating the ability of larvae to consume and degrade different plastic materials. The excreted material is collected and analyzed to measure plastic digestion, biodegradation, and residual polymer content, with evidence of polystyrene depolymerization and metabolic changes found within the FRASS. Beyond its role in plastic degradation, FRASS has practical applications in sustainable agriculture due to its rich content of nitrogen and chitin, allowing it to act as a natural fertilizer or medium for growing plants, such as cacti. Additionally, it has potential applications in biogas production and biochar generation, expanding its utility in sustainable practices.

However, FRASS may potentially contain toxic substances depending on the type of plastic consumed, requiring careful consideration in its use. Furthermore, FRASS impacts soil microbial communities, processes, and metal bioavailability, making it an important factor in ecological and agricultural contexts. Despite some analytical challenges, such as interference with thermogravimetric analysis (TGA)</data>
