hunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f8516b10110f884279870c2ef887f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TG/DTG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"TG/DTG (Thermogravimetric Analysis/Derivative Thermogravimetric) is a method used to study thermal stability and decomposition behavior of polyether-PU foam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C 1S SPECTRA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C 1s spectra are part of XPS analysis, providing detailed information on carbon bonding environments in polyether-PU foam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HARD SEGMENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The hard segment of polyether-PU foam consists of urethane bonds and benzene rings, which undergo cracking during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The hard segment of polyether-PU foam that may be cracked during degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SOFT SEGMENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The soft segment of polyether-PU foam contains ether bonds, which are hydrolyzed during the biodegradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"The soft segment of polyether-PU foam, which includes polyl chains and is susceptible to oxidative reactions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROLYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A chemical reaction involving the splitting of bonds with water, utilized in treating plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrolysis refers to the breakdown of molecules by incorporating water, involved in the metabolism of isoniazid."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrolysis refers to the breakdown of urethane bonds in polyether-PU foam during mealworm digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"The breakdown of urethane bonds in PU foam through enzymatic hydrolysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrolysis is a chemical reaction where proteins are broken down into simpler compounds for energy use."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OXIDATIVE REACTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A reaction involving the initial cleavage of ether bonds in soft segments of polyether PU, contributing to its biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENZYMES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">Enzymes are biochemical catalysts produced by living organisms, including microorganisms and insects such as mealworm larvae (*T. molitor*) and *Galleria mellonella*. These proteins play a critical role in the degradation of complex polymers, including synthetic plastics, by breaking them down into simpler compounds with lower molecular weights. Enzymes are synthesized both by the larvae themselves and by gut microbiota, contributing significantly to processes like biomass production, polymer degradation, and biodegradation.

Specifically, enzymes such as phosphatases, esterases, leucine arylamidase, β-galactosidase, β-glucuronidase, α-glucosidase, cholesterol esterases, proteases, and lipases are involved in the breakdown of various types of plastics, including polystyrene (PS), polyether polyurethane (PU) foam, low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and polypropylene (PP). These enzymes catalyze reactions that oxidize ether bonds, hydrolyze urethane bonds, or cause surface alterations in plastics, enhancing their susceptibility to microbial degradation.

For instance, enzymes secreted by gut microbiota catalyze the decomposition of polymers such as polystyrene into fragments with lower molecular weights. Similarly, enzymes within the larval gut are responsible for breaking down LDPE, contributing to its depolymerization and biodegradation. Additionally, enzymes isolated from bacteria found in plastic-eating worms, such as *Brevibacillus borstelensis*, are being investigated for their potential to degrade plastics more effectively. The saliva of *Galleria mellonella* contains enzymes that play a key role in the breakdown of polyethylene, while enzymes secreted by the gut of *T. molitor* larvae facilitate the breakdown of microplastics through oxidation and depolymerization.

Although enzymes have demonstrated effectiveness in degrading certain synthetic polymers, their activity against some tested plastics may be relatively low, suggesting that further research is needed to optimize their efficiency. Overall, enzymes are essential components in the microbial and insect-mediated degradation of plastics, aiding in the depolymerization of complex molecules and contributing to sustainable solutions for plastic waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e56bce8206396081e0b7dccc47a10c9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C=C STRETCHING ON BENZENE RINGS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A chemical property observed at 1531 cm-1, indicating the presence of benzene rings in polyether-PU foam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;UREA BONDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bonds in PU foam that are thought to degrade via enzymatic hydrolysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ESTER LINKAGES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chemical bonds that decompose at higher temperatures during thermal analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FATTY ACID CHAINS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chemical bonds more resistant to thermal decomposition compared to urethane segments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ISOCYANATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A component of polyether-PU foam that influences the decomposition temperature of urethane bonds."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VOLATILIZED OXIDATION PRODUCTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Products formed during the degradation of polyether-PU foam, contributing to the loss of oxygen content."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THERMAL STABILITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure of how well a material resists decomposition under increasing temperatures."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thermal stability describes the ability of plastics to resist decomposition under heat, assessed through DSC-TGA analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thermal stability of Styrofoam and frass is assessed via TGA to understand the degradation process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AVERAGE MOLECULAR WEIGHT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure that decreases after degradation, affecting the thermal stability of materials."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TOTAL THERMAL WEIGHT LOSS RATIOS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure showing the extent of weight loss during thermal decomposition, indicating changes in material composition."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C-C GROUP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C-C Group represents a carbon-carbon bond identified in the chemical structure of polyether-PU foam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C-O GROUP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C-O Group represents a carbon-oxygen bond identified in the chemical structure of polyether-PU foam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C=O GROUP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C=O Group represents a carbon-oxygen double bond identified in the chemical structure of polyether-PU foam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;URETHANE LINKAGES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Urethane Linkages consist of C-C, C-O, and C=O groups within the polyether-PU foam structure, which are subject to hydrolysis upon ingestion by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SHANNON INDEX&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Shannon Index indicates higher species richness when higher values are observed, suggesting greater biodiversity in the PU-fed mealworm gut microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Shannon index is a diversity index used to quantify species richness and evenness in ecological communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Shannon index measures biodiversity, reflecting both species richness and evenness in microbial communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Shannon index measures the diversity of microbial flora, showing differences between bran-fed and EPS-fed groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Shannon index quantifies biodiversity and was measured as 1.691 for the bran-fed group and 0.731 for the EPS-fed group."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Shannon Index provides another measure of microbial diversity, revealing differences among various larval diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Shannon Index measures species richness and diversity within the gut microbiome of larvae fed different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Shannon Index measures the diversity of species within a community, accounting for both abundance and evenness."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Shannon index measures biodiversity, taking into account both richness and evenness of species in a community."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Shannon Index is a metric used to quantify the diversity of bacterial species in samples, showing higher values in CT samples compared to PS samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Shannon index measures diversity, showing comparable results between PS and control groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Shannon index measures the diversity of microbial communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Shannon index measures the diversity and evenness of microbial communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Shannon index measures both richness and evenness of microbial communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Shannon index quantifies the diversity of microbial communities, providing insights into gut microbiome health."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SIMPSON INDEX&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Simpson Index quantifies the degree of concentration when one or a few species dominate the community."&lt;SEP&gt;"Simpson Index reflects the evenness of microbial communities; lower values indicate higher biodiversity."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Simpson index measures the degree of concentration when one randomly samples from a population."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Simpson index measures alpha diversity in microbial communities and was used to assess differences in larval diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Simpson index quantifies biodiversity by measuring the probability that two randomly selected individuals belong to different species."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Simpson Index measures species richness and diversity, indicating lower values in PS samples compared to CT samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Simpson index evaluates species richness, indicating slightly lower richness in PS samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Simpson index quantifies the biodiversity of microbial communities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPIROPLASMATACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Another bacterial family showing significant shifts due to the PS diet in Tenebrio species."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasmataceae is a bacterial family common in insect guts, decreasing in abundance when mealworms are fed polyether-PU foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasmataceae is a bacterial family significantly enriched in the gut microbiota of T. molitor larvae fed with PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasmataceae is a family of bacteria present in the gut microbiome of T. obscurus and T. molitor, associated with changes in microbial structure upon PS ingestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasmataceae is a family of bacteria whose relative abundance changed noticeably in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio species when fed a PS diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasmataceae shows an increase from absent to 53.34% in PP-fed groups, strongly associating with PP degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENTEROCOCCACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial family associated with changes in the gut microbiome linked to PS biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a bacterial family whose abundance increases in mealworms fed PS diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is another bacterial family found in insect guts, also decreasing in abundance with polyether-PU foam feeding."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is another bacterial family whose relative abundance increases due to PET ingestion by T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is another dominant bacterial family observed in the bran-fed control group."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a bacterial family whose relative abundance increases significantly in T. obscurus after PS feeding, indicating its involvement in PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a family of bacteria that showed a noticeable change in relative abundance in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio species when fed a PS diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a common family present in all groups, contributing to the gut bacterial community's diversity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a bacterial family whose relative abundance decreases due to harmful polymer components."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a family of bacteria found in the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor larvae, which may be affected by microplastic ingestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"A bacterial family identified as part of the gut microbiome in T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae involved in LDPE degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STREPTOCOCCACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial family including Lactococcus garvieae, showing increased population density in the EPS-fed group."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae increases in relative abundance from 0.55% to 28.07% when mealworms consume polyether-PU foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a bacterial family contributing to the microbial diversity in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a bacterial family that includes Lactococcus lactis and Lactococcus garviae, which were more abundant in the gut samples of T. molitor fed with PS compared to the control."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae increases in relative abundance in the gut bacterial community of yellow mealworms fed with middle and inner layers of surgical masks."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a bacterial family that showed significantly higher abundances in middle-fed and inner-fed groups compared to the bran-fed control."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria present in the gut microbiome of T. obscurus and T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a common family shared across all groups, playing a role in the overall gut bacterial composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae includes gram-positive bacteria that grow in chains or pairs and contribute to plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria whose relative abundance increases with the ingestion of aged microplastics in Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Another bacterial family associated with the gut microbiome of T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae during LDPE degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria found in the intestines of mealworms fed with EPS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENTEROBACTERIACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">**Enterobacteriaceae**, a family of Gram-negative bacteria belonging to the Gammaproteobacteria class, plays a significant role in the degradation of various polymers and is prominently featured in the gut microbiota of insects such as *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworms) and other Tenebrionid species. This bacterial family includes several genera, such as *Cronobacter*, *Aeromonas*, *Klebsiella pneumoniae*, *Proteus*, *Erwinia olea*, *Enterobacter absuriae YTI*, *Enterobacter soli*, and *Klebsiella grimmontii*. 

Members of **Enterobacteriaceae** are known for their potential involvement in the breakdown of diverse synthetic materials, including polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), high-impact polystyrene (HIPS), polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHA), expanded polystyrene (EPS), polyether-PU foam, and even components of surgical masks or other plastic substrates. Research has shown that the relative abundance of **Enterobacteriaceae** increases markedly in larvae fed diets containing plastics compared to control-fed larvae. For instance, its abundance rose from 0.93% to 16.19% when polyether-PU foam was provided as a food source.

This bacterial family is commonly observed in the gut microbiome of larvae fed with PU (polyurethane) and EVA (ethylene-vinyl acetate) and is notably found across all groups but significantly increases in PP (polypropylene)- and PU-fed groups. The enrichment of **Enterobacteriaceae** in the guts of larvae exposed to plastic diets suggests its contribution to enhancing the degradation capabilities of host organisms toward plastics. Its increased presence in these contexts indicates its potential role in the transformation and disruption of synthetic materials under both anaerobic and aerobic conditions.

While **Enterobacteriaceae** is present in the gut microbiota of control-fed larvae, its abundance and activity are strongly associated with the consumption of plastic-based diets. Overall, **Enterobacteriaceae** represents an important group of microorganisms contributing to the biodegradation of plastics, particularly in insect models like *Tenebrio molitor* and related species involved in processes such as LDPE (low-density polyethylene) degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;XPS SPECTRA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"XPS Spectra is a process used to analyze the elemental composition and chemical structure of materials, revealing changes in polyether-PU foam after mealworm ingestion."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FTIR ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"FTIR (Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) is a technique used to confirm the identity of LDPE film materials and detect changes in chemical bonds."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR analysis detects functional group changes in polymers during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR Analysis (Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) is a versatile spectroscopic technique used to identify and characterize chemical changes in various materials, particularly in the context of biodegradation. This method analyzes infrared absorption spectra to detect differences in chemical bonds and functional groups, providing evidence of processes such as hydrolysis, oxidation, and metabolic transformations.

In applications related to biodegradation, FTIR Analysis confirms the breakdown of specific chemical structures, such as urethane bonds in polyether-PU foam, carbonyl and hydroxyl groups in polystyrene (PS), and ring cleavage in plastic polymers. It verifies the formation of new functional groups during the passage of plastics like PS through the mealworm gut, as evidenced by spectral data from frass collected from larvae. Additionally, FTIR Analysis has been instrumental in studying the degradation of other polymers, including polyurethane (PU), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), and polyethylene (PE).

The technique is widely employed to analyze feeding substrates, frass, and virgin materials, comparing their chemical properties before and after microbial or enzymatic treatment. FTIR Analysis detects oxidative functional groups formed during biodegradation, revealing the presence of hydroxyl groups in PS films post-degradation and confirming water formation during metabolic processes in mealworms. Furthermore, it characterizes the biodegradation and oxidation of plastic polymers, identifying changes in chemical bonds and functional groups that result from microbial activity or exposure to bacterial strains.

Overall, FTIR Analysis serves as an essential tool for understanding the mechanisms of plastic biodegradation, offering detailed insights into the chemical modifications that occur during these processes.&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FTIR) provides evidence of oxidation and depolymerization of LDPE foams."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CO-NH CHAIN SCISSIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"CO-NH Chain Scissions occur due to oxidation following exposure to ultraviolet light, leading to polymer degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MOLECULAR WEIGHT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure of the size of a molecule, used in this study to track changes during LDPE biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight (MW) refers to the mass of a molecule and serves as a critical parameter for understanding the properties, behavior, and degradation processes of polymers such as polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET). It quantifies the size of polymer chains and plays a pivotal role in assessing changes occurring during biodegradation, thermal degradation, and chemical degradation. Molecular weight includes number-averaged (\(M_n\)) and weight-averaged (\(M_w\)) measures, which provide insights into the efficiency of plastic degradation under various conditions.

Techniques like gel permeation chromatography (GPC) and Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) are commonly employed to determine molecular weight, offering critical information about polymer degradation, solubility, and biodegradability. During microbial degradation, the molecular weight of polymers decreases significantly, indicating depolymerization. This reduction enhances biodegradability, as smaller polymer chains are more easily broken down by microorganisms. For instance, studies involving the ingestion of plastics by larvae (e.g., mealworms or T. molitor larvae) have shown that molecular weight decreases after exposure to microbial action or larval digestion.

Molecular weight is also a key indicator of polymer chain size and complexity, influencing the structure and function of gut microbiomes in organisms that consume plastics. It affects the ability of these organisms to degrade polymers, thereby playing a crucial role in assessing the environmental impact and degradability of plastics, including Styrofoam. Changes in molecular weight reflect processes such as thermal, chemical, and microbial degradation, making it an essential tool for understanding the environmental fate of polymers. In summary, molecular weight is a critical parameter used to assess the degradation of plastics and describe the changes in the size of polymer chains during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight measures the size of molecules, influencing their properties and degradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight refers to the mass of molecules, which was measured for PS and LDPE polymers in the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a