    <node id="&quot;Z. ATRATUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Another species of insect larvae involved in studies on plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Z. atratus is a species known for its ability to degrade LDPE, contributing to the biodegradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;**Zophobas atratus (commonly known as superworms)** is a species of darkling beetle larvae that has garnered significant attention for its ability to degrade various types of plastics. These larvae, belonging to the Tenebrio genus, contribute to the biodegradation of polymers such as polypropylene (PP), polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), and polyurethane (PU) foams. Research indicates that Z. atratus generates reactive oxygen species (ROS) in its gut during the degradation process, which aids in breaking down plastic materials.

Studies have shown that when fed PP, the larvae exhibit shifts in their gut microbial communities, implicating *Enterobacter* sp. as a key player in PP degradation. The gut microbiota of Z. atratus plays a crucial role in the depolymerization of plastics, forming oxygenated functional groups such as carbonyl and hydroxyl groups during the biodegradation process. This bacterium-like activity within the gut microbiome contributes significantly to the breakdown of polymer chains.

In addition to its plastic-degrading capabilities, Z. atratus demonstrates strong cannibalistic behavior under nutritional stress, which may influence survival rates and frass production during experiments involving Styrofoam consumption. Comparisons with *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworms) reveal differences in gut microbial communities, leading to variations in biodegradation efficiency and cannibalism behaviors when fed different diets, such as PP or wheat bran (WB).

Furthermore, Z. atratus larvae are capable of performing depolymerization patterns for PS biodegradation while showing limited effectiveness on low-density polyethylene (LDPE). Their potential for degrading commercial PP foam makes them an important subject of study in the field of environmental science and waste management. Overall, Z. atratus, commonly referred to as superworms, represents a promising biological solution for addressing plastic pollution through its unique combination of enzymatic and microbial activities.&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus larvae are involved in the biodegradation of commercial PS products through their digestive processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e</data>
    </node>
