    <node id="&quot;OTUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"OTUs (Operational Taxonomic Units) are used as estimators for species richness and diversity in gut fungal communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"OTUs (Operational Taxonomic Units) represent groups of closely related organisms used for community similarity assessment."&lt;SEP&gt;"OTUs (Operational Taxonomic Units) represent the number of distinct taxa in a sample, which decreased significantly in plastic-fed groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Operational Taxonomic Units (OTUs) are used as markers to identify microorganisms strongly associated with polyether-PU degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Operational Taxonomic Units (OTUs) are used to classify groups of closely related organisms within microbial communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Operational Taxonomic Units (OTUs) serve as markers for classifying microbial taxa based on genetic similarity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Operational Taxonomic Units (OTUs) are groups of closely related organisms used in microbial ecology to classify species."&lt;SEP&gt;"Operational Taxonomic Units (OTUs) are clusters of closely related sequences used in microbial ecology."&lt;SEP&gt;"Operational Taxonomic Units represent clusters of closely related organisms identified through sequencing."&lt;SEP&gt;"Operational Taxonomic Units (OTUs) represent clusters of closely related organisms identified in microbial studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Operational Taxonomic Units represent distinct taxa identified during microbial community analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Operational taxonomic units (OTUs) represent microbial species identified through next-generation sequencing, with Citrobacter sp., Enterobacter sp., and Klyuyvera sp. being relevant to PP degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Operational Taxonomic Units, clusters of closely related organisms identified during microbial community analysis."</data>
