    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polystyrene is a synthetic polymer that undergoes degradation processes in certain environments."&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene (PS) is a synthetic aromatic hydrocarbon polymer derived from petroleum, characterized by its high molecular weight, stable structure, and thermoplastic properties. Due to these attributes, polystyrene is highly versatile and widely used in various applications such as packaging materials, food containers, disposable cutlery, construction materials, building insulation, and other consumer goods. Globally, approximately 35 million tons of polystyrene are produced annually, reflecting its significant industrial importance.

Despite its utility, polystyrene poses substantial environmental challenges due to its chemically stable carbon-carbon backbone, which makes it resistant to natural degradation under typical environmental conditions. This persistence contributes to pollution, particularly microplastic contamination in marine ecosystems, posing risks to aquatic life and human health. Polystyrene can degrade into microplastics through biological and physical processes, including the formation of nanoplastics, which affect environmental systems.

Research has shown that certain organisms, such as mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), superworms (*Zophobas atratus*), and greater wax moths (*Galleria mellonella*), can biodegrade polystyrene within their gut microbiomes. Through mechanical chewing and enzymatic decomposition facilitated by microbial communities, these organisms convert polystyrene into carbon dioxide (CO₂) and biomass, utilizing it as a carbon source and energy supply. However, when used as the sole food source, polystyrene results in high mortality rates among mealworms due to its low nutritional value, leading to significant changes in larval weight and fatty acid content, thereby impacting their development and nutritional status. The process of biodegradation by insect larvae also leads to the formation of microplastics and changes in gut microbiota. Additionally, studies have associated the biodegradation of polystyrene with elevated enzymatic activities of alkaline phosphatase, acid phosphatase, and esterase-lipase C8, indicating potential routes for its breakdown.

Thermal degradation of polystyrene involves the formation of radicals but often results in the creation of microplastics, further exacerbating environmental concerns. While polystyrene remains a valuable material in numerous applications, ongoing research into its degradation and sustainable disposal methods is crucial for mitigating its environmental impact. Efforts to enhance its biodegradability using microbial communities offer promising biological solutions for addressing its environmental impact. In summary, polysty</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-412d934721b214c2cd0565531172171c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf28f07f3e46b29e447aae784667c29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a706f2bd66291a3ef925114ffd9300&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a7768b61a3d166f2cd01d4e87ac88cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6464a2f3ca18442f70e254b9341a3e1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d57e056cd3b06