    <node id="&quot;POLYURETHANE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyurethane (PUR) is a polymer composed of organic units joined by carbamate links, used in foams, coatings, and adhesives."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane is a material examined for its biodegradation potential in vitro conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane is a polymer used in foam products and can undergo biodegradation under certain conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane is a type of plastic material that can be degraded through enzymatic processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane is a polymer synthesized by the reaction of Diisocyanate and Polyol, forming organic units called urethane. It is highly resistant to physical and biological degradation due to its chemical composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane is a synthetic polymer that can be degraded by certain bacterial consortia, as indicated in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane is a synthetic polymer used as food for T. molitor larvae in biodegradation experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane (PUR) is a versatile polymer that can be broken down by T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane is a plastic material utilized in experiments to assess its impact on larval gut microbiomes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane is a versatile polymer used in various applications, contributing to plastic waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67425e341a5b2c8102281581fa148554&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHOLESTEROL ESTERASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme capable of degrading poly(ether urethane) and poly(carbonate urethane)."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MEALWORM GUT MICROBIOME&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mealworm gut microbiome consists of various microorganisms that contribute to the degradation of polyurethane through synergic interactions."&lt;SEP&gt;"The community of microorganisms residing in the gut of mealworms, playing a key role in their digestion process."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbiome of mealworms contains microorganisms capable of secreting factors that enhance respiration and degradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"The mealworm gut microbiome consists of bacteria that can metabolize PS microplastics as a carbon source."&lt;SEP&gt;"The mealworm gut microbiome consists of microorganisms that contribute to the degradation of plastic through secreted factors."&lt;SEP&gt;"The mealworm gut microbiome consists of various microorganisms residing in the gut of mealworms, playing a role in PS biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The microbial community residing in the gut of mealworms, involved in PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The microbial community within the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, which plays a role in enhancing the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"The mealworm gut microbiome consists of bacteria that play a crucial role in digestion and nutrient absorption. It varies between individuals and environments."&lt;SEP&gt;"The mealworm gut microbiome consists of microorganisms involved in the digestion and biodegradation processes within the insect."&lt;SEP&gt;"The microbial community present in the gut of mealworms, responsible for biodegrading polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"The mealworm gut microbiome contains bacteria adapted to different concentrations of nanoplastics, allowing them to mineralize these particles effectively."&lt;SEP&gt;"The microbial community residing in the gut of mealworms, playing a role in the digestion and degradation of various substrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"The mealworm gut microbiome refers to the collection of microorganisms within the gut of mealworms, capable of degrading plastics like PE and PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"The microbial community within the gut of mealworms, shaped by their diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbiome of mealworms enhances the bioavailability and microbial biodegradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbiome of mealworms consists of microorganisms that play a key role in decomposing plastics and other materials ingested by the larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc75b7e672a7f7c036713dfa2675d79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a8b07ee5dc7a7af59a93d03311efe60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYPROPYLENE (PP)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A petro-based polymer that is part of the group of degradable plastics studied for its degradation by invertebrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polypropylene (PP) is a thermoplastic polymer used as a substrate for studying biodegradation by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polypropylene is a fossil-based polyolefin plastic widely used worldwide, resistant to biodegradation and a major source of plastic pollutants in the environment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polypropylene is a type of plastic polymer used as a diet for T. molitor larvae in the experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a thermoplastic polymer known for its versatility and widespread use in various applications, including packaging, textiles, storage containers, industrial products, and even disposable surgical masks. It is characterized by its durability, resistance to biodegradation, and high molecular weight, making it a persistent nonhydrolyzable polymer commonly found in plastics.

Despite its general resistance to degradation, research has shown that specific microorganisms and insects, such as mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae), can degrade polypropylene. Studies have demonstrated that yellow mealworms can ingest and break down polypropylene, with evidence of its breakdown observed through the analysis of frass extracts using solvents like 1,2 DCB or DCM. While polypropylene is not as readily consumed by mealworms compared to polystyrene, it still undergoes degradation processes similar to those seen in other plastics like LLDPE and HDPE. Edge chewing and consumption of polypropylene have been noted in experiments, particularly when presented in forms such as sheet-like bags or plastic packaging films.

Polypropylene accounts for approximately 18.9% of global plastic usage, contributing significantly to environmental contamination due to its persistence. However, its potential for mechanical recycling and biodegradation by certain organisms suggests promising avenues for reducing its environmental impact. As a synthetic plastic with chemical structures resembling those of natural polymers, polypropylene continues to be a subject of interest in studies assessing its biodegradability and physiological impacts on organisms such as insects.&lt;SEP&gt;"Polypropylene is a rigid plastic that T. molitor larvae cannot degrade."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polypropylene is a petro-based polymer that may undergo degradation in plastic-degrading organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polypropylene is a versatile plastic polymer used in various applications due to its durability and resistance to chemicals."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polypropylene is extensively used in various applications and contributes to plastic waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYVINYL CHLORIDE (PVC)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC) is a widely produced synthetic plastic polymer with significant applications across various industries. Known for its durability, versatility, and ease of fabrication, PVC holds a substantial market share, particularly in construction, where it is used in building materials, industrial products, daily necessities, artificial leather, pipes, wires and cables, and packaging films. It accounts for approximately 10.4% of global plastic production. However, PVC also poses notable health and environmental risks due to its hazardous nature as a chlorine-based, petroleum-derived plastic, exhibiting higher toxicity compared to other plastics.

PVC is not easily biodegradable under normal environmental conditions, contributing significantly to plastic waste challenges. Despite this general resistance to degradation, recent studies have demonstrated that certain organisms, such as Tenebrio molitor larvae (commonly known as yellow mealworms), possess the ability to consume and metabolize PVC through enzymatic activity within their digestive systems. These mealworms can break down PVC into simpler compounds, though PVC is one of the least favored plastics for consumption by these larvae. Exposure to PVC can be toxic, causing high mortality rates, inducing oxidative stress, and resulting in the lowest survival rate and highest body weight reduction among mealworms fed different types of plastics.

Additionally, specific bacteria isolated from insect larvae, such as *Achromobacter denitrificans Ebl13*, have shown potential in degrading PVC, further contributing to biodegradation research. While PVC traditionally resists natural breakdown processes, these findings suggest that certain organisms can contribute to its breakdown under specific conditions, albeit less effectively compared to other plastics like polystyrene (PS) or low-density polyethylene (LDPE). Researchers continue to evaluate the conversion efficiency of PVC by T. molitor larvae and analyze residual content via methods such as solvent extraction from frass. Further investigation is necessary to mitigate the toxicity effects on these organisms and optimize biodegradation techniques for sustainable waste management solutions.

In summary, Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC) is a versatile yet environmentally concerning plastic with applications across multiple sectors. Although it is generally resistant to biodegradation, recent studies indicate that certain organisms, including Tenebrio molitor larvae and specific bacteria, can contribute to its breakdown under specific conditions. These efforts aim to address the growing issue of PVC waste and promote more sustainable practices.</data>
