    <node id="&quot;X-RAY PHOTOELECTRON SPECTROSCOPY (XPS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A surface-sensitive quantitative spectroscopic technique used to analyze the elemental composition at the parts per thousand level."&lt;SEP&gt;"XPS is a technique used to verify changes in surface chemical components and functional groups during plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"XPS is a technique used to verify changes in surface chemistry and functional groups of polymer samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"XPS is an analytical technique used to determine the elemental composition at the surface of a material."&lt;SEP&gt;"XPS verifies changes in surface chemical components and functional groups of polymer samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"XPS is a surface-sensitive quantitative spectroscopic technique used to analyze the elemental composition and chemical state of materials, confirming the presence of new oxygen-containing functional groups on HDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"A technique used to investigate surface chemical components of materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"X-ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy is a technique used to analyze surface chemical components and functional groups of materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"XPS is a technique used to analyze the chemical composition of degraded PS films."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (FTIR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A spectroscopic technique used to characterize functional groups in materials by analyzing their absorbance of infrared light."&lt;SEP&gt;"A spectroscopic technique used to identify chemical functional groups and confirm chemical modifications in degraded PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"A technique used to obtain an infrared spectrum of absorption or emission of a solid, liquid, or gas, aiding in material identification and analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR analysis provides evidence of chemical changes during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is a process used to analyze modifications in functional groups of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR characterizes chemical modifications of polymer structure and monitors changes on the polymer surface."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is used in conjunction with TGA to identify decomposition products from biodegraded polymer samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR identifies chemical bonds and functional groups in frass, reflecting the biochemical interactions with microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is a spectroscopic technique used to identify chemical bonds and functional groups in the frass samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is a spectroscopic technique used to identify functional groups indicative of PS depolymerization and oxidation."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is a spectroscopic technique used to analyze chemical bonds and oxidation in polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR identifies the chemical bonds and functional groups present in Styrofoam and its derivatives."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is used to observe the physical and structural biodegradation of polyurethane foam after exposure to Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is a spectroscopic technique used to identify chemical bonds and functional groups in polymers during degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR was used to characterize functional groups in PS/CS feedstocks and frass samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is used to characterize changes in functional groups of plastics after depolymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy is a technique used to analyze the chemical composition of microplastics, showing changes in functional groups during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is an analytical method used to verify the formation of oxidized functional groups during the biodegradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is a technology used for analyzing chemical properties of materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy identifies chemical bonds and functional groups by analyzing the infrared spectrum absorbed by a sample."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GEL PERMEATION CHROMATOGRAPHY (GPC)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"GPC is a technique used for determining molecular weight distributions of polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"GPC is a technique used to characterize depolymerization by analyzing changes in molecular weight distribution of polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;Gel Permeation Chromatography (GPC) is an analytical technique primarily used to determine the molecular weight and molecular weight distribution of polymers. This method plays a critical role in analyzing the degradation processes of various plastics, including polystyrene (PS), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET). GPC provides insights into depolymerization, biodegradation, and oxidation processes by measuring changes in molecular weight during these transformations.

The technique separates molecules based on their size, making it particularly sensitive for detecting polymer residues compared to other methods like gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC-MS). GPC has been utilized extensively to study the breakdown of microplastics, revealing partial depolymerization and oxidation in materials such as PS. It is also employed to analyze polymer fragments found in biological matrices, such as fecula egested from Styrofoam-feeding larvae or frass samples containing PVC depolymerization products.

In studies involving PS, PET, and PVC, GPC confirms the molecular weight distributions of residual polymer pieces, offering evidence of plastic degradation. Additionally, it assesses molecular weight characteristics of polymers during depolymerization and monitors molecular weight changes post-depolymerization. Overall, Gel Permeation Chromatography serves as a vital tool for understanding the mechanisms of polymer degradation and biodegradation across multiple applications.&lt;SEP&gt;"GPC is a technique used to characterize the molecular weights of PS and PVC polymers after ingestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"GPC is an analytical technique used to confirm the depolymerization and biodegradation of plastics in this study."&lt;SEP&gt;"GPC is used to analyze molecular weight distributions of residual PS compared to original PS MPs."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gel permeation chromatography separates molecules based on size, commonly used to analyze molecular weight distributions of polymers."</data>
