    <node id="&quot;GALLERIA MELLONELLA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Galleria mellonella, or greater wax moth larvae, are investigated for their ability to biodegrade polystyrene and the effects on their core gut microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"Galleria mellonella, or wax moth caterpillars, exhibit the ability to bio-degrade polyethylene plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;**Galleria mellonella**, commonly known as the greater wax moth or wax moth, is a species of insect whose larvae, often referred to as waxworms or greater waxworms, have garnered significant attention due to their remarkable ability to degrade various types of plastics. These larvae possess both intrinsic enzymatic capabilities and microbial assistance, enabling them to effectively break down plastics such as polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polypropylene (PP).

Research has demonstrated that **Galleria mellonella** larvae can completely digest and biodegrade polystyrene microplastics without relying solely on gut microbiota. Instead, they exhibit an intrinsic enzymatic metabolism that allows them to metabolize long-chain hydrocarbons, such as beeswax, which they consume in their natural habitat. This metabolic ability also extends to the degradation of plastics like polyethylene, where experiments have shown that the larvae can chew through and degrade PE films, particularly low-density polyethylene (LDPE) substrates. Their capacity to consume and break down plastics makes them a promising candidate in addressing plastic pollution.

Additionally, the gut microbiome of **Galleria mellonella** larvae plays a significant role in the degradation process, hosting bacteria capable of breaking down plastics like polyethylene. Polystyrene-degrading bacteria have been isolated and characterized from these larvae, highlighting their potential for contributing to environmental solutions. The larvae utilize both their own metabolic processes and the contributions of their gut flora to achieve effective plastic degradation.

Beyond their plastic-degrading abilities, **Galleria mellonella** larvae are used in microbiome studies related to plastic degradation and are studied for their biological characteristics and interactions with other species. Overall, **Galleria mellonella**, or the greater wax moth, is an insect species whose larvae exhibit a unique combination of intrinsic enzymatic capabilities and microbial assistance, making them a valuable subject of study in ongoing efforts to combat environmental plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;"Wax moth larvae also studied for their ability to degrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Galleria mellonella, or greater wax moth larvae, contribute to the biodegradation of polyethylene and polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85ab9c6af791