    <node id="&quot;GENOTOXICITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Damage caused to genetic material due to nanoplastic exposure, leading to mutations or cell death."&lt;SEP&gt;"Genotoxicity refers to damage to genetic material caused by nanoplastics, leading to mutations or cell death."&lt;SEP&gt;"Genotoxicity refers to the potential of substances to damage genetic material, often assessed in studies involving biodegradable materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Genotoxicity refers to the potential of a substance to damage genetic material, leading to mutations or cancer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Genotoxicity refers to the potential of chemical compounds to damage genetic information within a cell, causing mutations."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ECOTOXICITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ecotoxicity involves adverse effects on living organisms due to exposure to toxic substances, such as microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ecotoxicity refers to the potential toxic effects of degradation products on other organisms, such as Daphnia magna and Rattus norvegicus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ecotoxicity refers to the toxic effects of substances on ecosystems, including microorganisms and other living organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"The potential of substances to cause harmful effects on ecosystems or living organisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENVIRONMENTAL IMPACT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Environmental Impact refers to the effects of biodegradation processes on ecosystems and natural environments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Environmental impact encompasses the effects of human activities, such as plastic degradation, on the environment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Environmental impact refers to the effect of human activities on the natural environment, including the role of plastic-degrading larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Environmental impact refers to the effect of human activities, such as plastic production, on ecosystems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Environmental impact refers to the effect of human activities, including plastic production and disposal, on the natural environment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Environmental impact refers to the effects of human activities, such as plastic use, on ecosystems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Environmental impact refers to the effect human activities have on the natural environment, including plastic pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Environmental impact encompasses the effects of microorganism-mediated plastic degradation on ecosystems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Environmental impact describes the effects of plastic waste, such as surgical masks, on ecosystems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Environmental impact refers to the effects of human activities, such as plastic waste, on the natural environment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Environmental impact encompasses the effects of plastic pollution and degradation methods on ecosystems and human health."&lt;SEP&gt;"Environmental impact refers to the broader implications of enhancing bioenergy production and carbon capture in microalgae through ultraviolet spectrum conversion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Environmental impact refers to the effects of human activities on the natural environment, including pollution caused by non-degradable plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-352650a946ab941cec3f7b9813faeee1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa27760a0a889fe7b58360dbb150cdf3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WASTE MANAGEMENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Waste management encompasses the collection, transport, processing, recycling, and disposal of waste materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waste management encompasses the collection, transportation, and disposal of waste materials, including plastic degradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waste management involves strategies for handling and recycling waste materials, including plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waste management involves strategies for handling and reducing plastic waste, including biological methods like biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waste management involves the collection, transportation, processing, and disposal of waste materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waste management involves strategies for handling waste materials, including the use of biological systems like larvae for breaking down plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waste management involves activities and actions required to manage waste from its inception to its final disposal."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waste management involves strategies for handling waste sustainably, including recycling and biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RESOURCE RECOVERY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Resource Recovery involves the extraction of valuable materials from waste products, such as the breakdown of plastics into reusable components."&lt;SEP&gt;"Resource recovery focuses on extracting valuable materials from waste for reuse or recycling."&lt;SEP&gt;"Resource recovery refers to the sustainable approach of utilizing insect-mediated biodegradation for waste management."&lt;SEP&gt;"Resource recovery involves reclaiming waste materials for reuse, exemplified by the sustainable approach of using mealworms for waste management."&lt;SEP&gt;"Resource recovery involves reclaiming valuable materials from waste, including plastics through biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SUSTAINABLE APPROACH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A sustainable approach aims to meet present needs without compromising future generations' ability to meet their own needs."&lt;SEP&gt;"A sustainable approach refers to methods that meet current needs without compromising future generations, exemplified by using larvae for resource recovery."&lt;SEP&gt;"A sustainable approach refers to methods and strategies aimed at reducing environmental impact while promoting resource efficiency."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sustainable Approach emphasizes methods that minimize environmental harm while promoting efficient resource utilization."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PACKAGING FILM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Packaging film is a material susceptible to damage by insect pests, highlighting potential biodegradation mechanisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Packaging film refers to thin sheets of plastic used for packaging, often susceptible to damage by insect pests."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INSECT PESTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Insect pests are insects that cause damage to crops, stored products, or materials such as packaging film."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insect pests such as darkling beetles and moths are capable of chewing and degrading various plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insect pests, such as those studied in packaging film damage, include species capable of degrading plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GC-MS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"GC-MS (Gas Chromatography-Mass Spectrometry) is an analytical technique used to identify and quantify compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC-MS) is a technique used to analyze chemicals present in the worm frass and carcasses."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC-MS) is employed to detect intermediates of PS/CS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC-MS) was utilized to identify degradation products generated from the biodegradation of polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Expanded polystyrene is a material biodegraded and disintegrated by land snails Achatina fulica."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene is a type of plastic studied for its biodegradation by mealworm larvae under varying feeding conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene is a type of plastic that land snails like Achatina fulica can biodegrade."&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is a lightweight, rigid foam plastic material derived from polystyrene. It is widely utilized for packaging and insulation due to its excellent thermal properties and cost-effectiveness. EPS is known for its role in various industries, including construction, shipping, and food storage.

Research has shown that Expanded Polystyrene can undergo biodegradation by certain organisms, such as the larvae of Tenebrio molitor (commonly known as mealworms). These larvae are capable of consuming and partially degrading EPS, altering its chemical structure during digestion. Additionally, studies have explored the potential of other organisms, like land snails, in contributing to the biodegradation of this material. Such research aims to identify microorganisms capable of breaking down EPS, offering promising avenues for waste management and environmental sustainability.

Despite its utility, Expanded Polystyrene is associated with environmental concerns, as it can release toxic leachates over time. Efforts are underway to develop recycling techniques for EPS, including dissolution methods and nanoprecipitation, which could help mitigate its ecological impact. Overall, Expanded Polystyrene remains a significant focus in both industrial applications and scientific studies aimed at enhancing its recyclability and reducing its environmental footprint.&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene is a type of plastic that undergoes biodegradation by mealworm larvae under various feeding strategies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LAND SNAILS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"INSECT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Land snails are capable of depolymerizing and biodegrading polystyrene foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Land snails, particularly Achatina fulica, have shown potential in the biodegradation and disintegration of expanded polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Land snails, such as Achatina fulica, contribute to the biodegradation of certain plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Land snails, such as Achatina fulica, possess the ability to degrade expanded polystyrene through biological mechanisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Land snails are non-insect organisms with potential for studying plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC POLYMERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic polymers are synthetic materials made from long chains of molecules, many of which are subject to biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic polymers include various types of plastics such as HDPE, LDPE, LLDPE, PP, PVC, and PS, targeted for microbial degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic polymers include materials like polyethylene and polystyrene, whose physical, thermal, and mechanical properties affect degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic polymers, such as trans-trans-muconic acid, potentially contribute to elevated synthesis of certain metabolites in the insect gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic polymers are synthetic materials with low nutritional value tested as mealworm feeding sources."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHEMICAL RECYCLING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chemical recycling includes methods such as pyrolysis and gasification, converting plastic waste into valuable chemical substances or precursors."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chemical recycling involves breaking down plastics into their monomers or other basic chemicals for reuse."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chemical recycling involves breaking down plastics into their original monomers for reuse."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chemical recycling involves breaking down plastics into their monomers for reuse, offering an alternative to traditional disposal methods."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRIBOLIUM CASTANEUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Tribolium castaneum is a beetle species whose larvae have been found to harbor polystyrene-degrading bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;**Tribolium castaneum**, commonly known as the red flour beetle, is a species of darkling beetle that has gained significant attention for its role in biodegradation, particularly in the context of plastic waste management. This insect, which can be managed using standard loose plastic packaging, possesses larvae capable of harboring polystyrene-degrading bacteria. The beetles themselves are studied for their ability to chew, eat, and degrade various plastics, including expanded polystyrene (EPS), also known as Styrofoam. 

Research indicates that **Tribolium castaneum** plays a crucial role in plastic-degrading processes, with potential applications for addressing global plastic waste challenges. The species has been shown to biodegrade EPS effectively, offering promising solutions for environmental sustainability. Additionally, it is hypothesized to possess mechanisms similar to those of *Tenebrio molitor* for the biodegradation of polyethylene terephthalate (PET).

The gut microbiota of **Tribolium castaneum** includes bacteria such as *Acinetobacter*, which have been isolated from the larvae and found to degrade polystyrene. These microbes contribute significantly to the beetle's ability to digest certain plastics, making it a subject of interest in microbiome studies focused on investigating plastic degradation. Furthermore, the interaction between **Tribolium castaneum** and other organisms, such as *Rhizophora dominica*, has been explored in the context of managing loose plastic packaging.

Beyond its role in biodegradation, **Tribolium castaneum** is also studied for pest management and its life cycle characteristics, including weight variations. The species is capable of living on a diet of plastic due to its digestive abilities, further highlighting its potential in both ecological and industrial applications. Overall, **Tribolium castaneum** represents an important model organism in the pursuit of sustainable solutions for plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;"Tribolium castaneum is a species of darkling beetle capable of biodegrading plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19868f1234aac10de61c710c8352c076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYETHYLENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene (PE) is a widely used synthetic thermoplastic polymer and one of the most commonly produced plastics globally. Known for its high molecular weight, durability, versatility, and resistance to natural degradation processes, polyethylene plays a critical role in various applications, particularly in packaging materials and plastic products due to its strength. It exists in different forms, such as high-density polyethylene (HDPE) and low-density polyethylene (LDPE), which are utilized across numerous industries. Despite its inherent recalcitrance to biodegradation, which poses significant environmental and health hazards due to its persistence, research has demonstrated that certain microorganisms, enzymes, and insects can degrade polyethylene under specific conditions.

Mealworm larvae (*Tenebrio molitor*), among other organisms like superworms, greater wax moth larvae (*Galleria mellonella*), and Indian mealmoth larvae (*Achroia grisella*), have been shown to degrade polyethylene. The degradation process involves enzymatic activities within their gut microbiota, which assist in breaking down the polymer. This microbial activity influences the bacterial community composition in the larvae’s digestive system. Regranulated polyethylene (PE-reg) can serve as a nutrient source for mealworm larvae, promoting their development with minimal mass loss while influencing their metabolism and fatty acid profiles.

During degradation, polyethylene undergoes changes in molecular weight, oxidation level, and weight loss rate. Although generally considered non-biodegradable under natural conditions, studies indicate that specific bacterial strains, such as *Rhodococcus ruber*, and the saliva of certain larvae contribute to the depolymerization process. Polyethylene can also degrade into micro- and nanoplastics over time, contributing to environmental pollution. Waste samples containing polyethylene are commonly found at landfill sites, underscoring the need for effective biodegradation solutions to manage its environmental footprint.

Ongoing research reflects efforts to address the environmental impact of polyethylene by exploring microbial and insect-based degradation pathways as promising avenues for reducing its persistence in ecosystems. Polyethylene continues to be a widely studied plastic polymer for both its degradation patterns and associated health impacts, characterized by its versatility and widespread use in modern manufacturing.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47c76e5149ac059b1960b533c9e99a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e56bce8206396081e0b7dccc47a10c9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df24ea37743c236d369911024fc20bfc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6