    <node id="&quot;POLYPROPYLENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polypropylene is another type of plastic that contributes to microplastic pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a thermoplastic polymer extensively utilized in various industrial applications, including packaging, textiles, and the construction of disposable medical masks such as surgical masks. It shares structural similarities with polyethylene, characterized by a simple carbon-to-carbon backbone and rigid crystalline thermoplastic structures. Polypropylene is widely studied for its biodegradation potential, particularly through gut-microbe-dependent mechanisms in certain insects like the larvae of Tenebrio molitor (yellow mealworms) and Zophobas atratus (superworms). These larvae possess gut microbiota capable of degrading polypropylene via enzymatic or microbial processes, contributing to depolymerization.

While polypropylene is generally considered non-hydrolyzable and environmentally persistent, research has shown that specific microorganisms and enzymes can facilitate its breakdown. Ongoing studies aim to further explore and enhance the biodegradability of polypropylene, addressing its environmental persistence and potential impacts on insect metabolic pathways. Polypropylene's susceptibility to biodegradation under specific microbial conditions offers promising avenues for reducing its environmental impact. This plastic material is extensively used in plastics and is analyzed for its degradation properties, particularly focusing on its interaction with gut microbes in organisms like yellow mealworms and superworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748</data>
