3b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606</data>
      <data key="d1">Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is a type of thermoplastic polymer derived from petroleum. It is characterized by its lower density and more branching compared to other polyethylenes such as high-density polyethylene (HDPE) and linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE). Its linear chain structure with short branches (2 to 6 carbon atoms long) hinders close packing, resulting in relatively low crystallinity (45-60%), which provides flexibility. This makes LDPE suitable for various packaging applications, including films, foams, and flexible packaging.

LDPE has been extensively studied for its biodegradation properties using isotopic and spectroscopic methods. Certain species, particularly *Tenebrio molitor* larvae (mealworms), *Zophobas atratus* larvae (superworms), and the larvae of the greater wax moth, are capable of metabolizing LDPE. These larvae cause significant depolymerization and biodegradation of LDPE, leading to structural changes, loss of carbon and hydrogen, weight reduction, and molecular weight alterations. The transformation of LDPE from a crystalline polymer into an amorphous state after being metabolized by mealworms highlights the significant changes occurring during the biodegradation process.

Compared to HDPE, LDPE generally induces less mass gain in mealworms during specific time intervals, such as at the 10- and 30-day marks, although it shows faster degradation rates due to its physical properties. However, under certain conditions, LDPE can induce oxidative stress and ecotoxicity in larvae. Studies have also explored how antibiotics affect LDPE depolymerization patterns and how LDPE influences gut microbiome composition. LDPE's consumption rates are relatively low compared to polystyrene but similar to those of HDPE. In experiments involving microbial degradation analysis, LDPE is often used in the form of beads or foam as a dietary component for larvae to understand the mechanisms of depolymerization and the impact on gene expression and enzymatic activity.

Beyond biological studies, LDPE's thermal behavior and potential use in producing petrochemical feedstock are also areas of interest. Additionally, LDPE undergoes oxidative degradation when buried under bioactive soil, further emphasizing its susceptibility to degradation. LDPE shows different depolymerization patterns compared to other plastics like polypropylene (PP) and polystyrene (PS). Overall, LDPE remains a critical subject in</data>
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
    </node>
