    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EARTHWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Earthworms (Eisenia fetida) exhibit defense responses when exposed to microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are soil-dwelling invertebrates that contribute to soil health but have limited plastic-degrading abilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are soil-dwelling organisms affected by microplastics, showing higher mortality and weight reduction."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are soil-dwelling organisms that can be affected by microplastic exposure, leading to oxidative stress and genetic damage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are soil-dwelling organisms that can biofragment plastics during digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are soil-dwelling organisms that may contain microplastics when ingested by predators like chickens."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are soil-dwelling organisms that play a critical role in nutrient cycling and soil structure, often used as bioindicators in ecological studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms contribute to soil restoration by facilitating the decay of low-density polyethylene through bacteria present in their guts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms were mentioned as an analogous organism where plastic degradation activity has been observed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms (Lumbricus terrestris) degrade polyethylene particles through physical and biochemical processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are soil-dwelling invertebrates found to ingest microplastics in environmental studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are soil-dwelling invertebrates capable of biofragmenting plastics but lack the enzymatic capability for complete degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are organisms capable of breaking down bioplastics such as polylactic acid, although excretion occurs slowly."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms, particularly Eisenia fetida, disintegrate polyactic acid microplastics into sub-micron microplastics within their digestive tracts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PE MICROPLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PE microplastics are small plastic particles derived from polyethylene that pose environmental and biological risks."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE microplastics refer to polyethylene particles of microscopic size used in studies of plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE microplastics refer to polyethylene particles smaller than 300 μm, used in experiments for studying degradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene microplastics used as a feedstock for mealworm larvae, which are relatively difficult to digest."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;REACTIVE OXYGEN SPECIES (ROS)">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen, often associated with oxidative stress."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROPLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">Microplastics are small plastic particles, typically measuring less than 5 mm in size, that originate from the breakdown of larger plastic materials such as expanded polystyrene (EPS), polystyrene, or polyethylene (PE). These particles are generated through processes like fragmentation, degradation, and biodegradation, which can occur due to environmental factors or biological agents such as insect larvae. Microplastics are found in various environments, including landfills, aquatic ecosystems, marine environments, terrestrial areas, freshwater systems, water bodies, sediments, soils, agricultural lands, sewage sludge from wastewater treatment plants, worm frass, and seafood.

Characterized by their tiny size (ranging from 1–20 μm), large specific surface area, and strong adsorption capacities, microplastics can accumulate in the environment and organisms, affecting biological processes and posing significant environmental and health risks. They have been found to adsorb heavy metals, antibiotics, and other substances, influencing aquatic organisms, soil ecosystems, and gastrointestinal environments. Additionally, microplastics serve as a substrate for microbial colonization and degradation, highlighting their dual role in both contamination and potential remediation efforts.

The pervasive nature of microplastics across diverse environments contributes significantly to pollution. They enter food chains, affecting a wide range of organisms, including marine life such as corals, oysters, worms, snails, fish, and terrestrial creatures like insects (e.g., mealworms) and earthworms. Ingestion of microplastics can lead to physiological effects, disrupting lipid homeostasis, stress responses, feeding and metabolic processes, and causing overall negative impacts on marine and terrestrial ecosystems. Microplastics induce oxidative stress and physiological damage when ingested by organisms, particularly invertebrates, and may also cause harm to digestive systems.

Research has also suggested that microplastics may interact with SARS-CoV-2 RNA fragments, potentially influencing viral transport and exposure. Despite their persistent nature and associated concerns, certain microorganisms can degrade microplastics, and extensive studies are underway to address the challenges they pose, particularly in marine environments. Mitigation strategies, such as longer incubation periods with worms, are being explored to combat their widespread presence.

Overall, microplastics remain an emerging concern in environmental science, studied for their environmental and health impacts across various ecosystems. Their persistent nature and potential toxicity underscore the urgent need for solutions to minimize their generation and mitigate their effects on ecosystems and human well-being.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a7768b61a3d166f2cd01d4e87ac88cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6464a2f3ca18442f70e254b9341a3e1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8f385513fcff2125529486530ad5a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aface14c9ee7dd5ccd25e7094ed5c1c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df896f6505a6c800d1edd6c75fce9b4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MNP S&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MNP s (micro- and nanoplastics) are extremely small plastic particles generated during the biofragmentation process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GASTROINTESTINAL SYSTEMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gastrointestinal systems refer to the organs involved in digestion, including the stomach and intestines."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DIGESTIVE BIOFRAGMENTATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Digestive biofragmentation is the process by which ingested plastics are broken down into smaller particles within the digestive system."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RESIDUAL PLASTIC PARTICLES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Residual plastic particles are undigested plastic fragments that remain after the biodegradation process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOFRAGMENTATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biofragmentation is the breakdown of plastics into smaller particles through biological activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofragmentation refers to the biological process of breaking down plastics into smaller particles."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofragmentation is the process by which ingested microplastics are broken down into smaller particles within the digestive system of organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofragmentation refers to the breakdown of plastics into smaller particles within the digestive system of organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofragmentation refers to the process by which organisms break down ingested plastics into smaller particles."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofragmentation is the process where living organisms break down plastic waste into smaller plastic fragments such as micro- and nano-plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INCOMPLETE DIGESTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Incomplete digestion occurs when not all ingested material is fully broken down by the digestive system."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AVERAGE WEIGHT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Average weight refers to the mean body weight of a population of organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"The average weight of mealworms was measured to evaluate the metabolic effects of microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOMASS LEVELS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biomass levels indicate the total mass of living organisms in a given system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biomass levels represent the growth and survival of mealworm larvae, influenced by dietary supplements."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biomass levels represent the total mass of mealworm larvae under different dietary treatments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WATER CONTENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL CHARACTERISTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Water Content describes the amount of water available in a given environment, influencing microbial activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Water content is a measure of hydration in the larvae, influenced by the type of plastic they consume."&lt;SEP&gt;"Water content is crucial for mealworm growth and metabolism, lacking in Styrofoam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Water content measures the amount of water present in an organism or substance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Water content refers to the level of water within the mealworm larvae's biomass after the experimental period."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TOTAL CARBOHYDRATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Total carbohydrate refers to the carbohydrate content in mealworm larvae, which decreases during PE biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Total carbohydrate refers to the combined amount of sugars, starches, and fibers in an organism's diet."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FAT CONTENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Fat content assesses the fat levels present in mealworm larvae at the conclusion of the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat content measures the amount of lipids present in an organism's body or diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Measurement of fat levels in mealworm larvae, influenced by diet conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat content refers to the lipid composition in the barley bran, providing energy and structural components."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat content in larvae decreases significantly when fed with polyethylene, indicating metabolic shifts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat content is a measure of lipid levels in insects, which decreases during starvation and influences taste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat content is a nutrient determined using the Soxhlet method with n-heptane as the extraction solvent."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat content refers to the lipid portion determined using methods like Soxhlet extraction."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat content refers to the proportion of fat stored in the insect's body, which can exceed 50% of the total dry mass in some studied insects."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf28f07f3e46b29e447aae784667c29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PROTEIN CONTENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Measurement of protein levels in mealworm larvae, influenced by diet conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Protein content determines the protein levels in mealworm larvae post-experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Protein content refers to the amount of protein in mealworm larvae, which increases due to enzyme biosynthesis for PE biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Protein content refers to the amount of protein present in an organism's body or diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"The protein content varies in the biomass and frass of mealworms depending on the feeding strategy."&lt;SEP&gt;"Protein content in mealworm larvae varies depending on their diet, with polystyrene providing higher protein levels."&lt;SEP&gt;"Protein content refers to the amount of protein measured in the mealworm larvae using the Bradford method."&lt;SEP&gt;"Protein content refers to the amount of protein present in a biological sample, often related to dietary intake."&lt;SEP&gt;"Protein content refers to the amount of protein in yellow mealworm larvae, which ranks first among live animal protein feeds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Protein content was determined using the Kjeldahl method, indicating the protein levels in yellow mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CO-DIET BRAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Co-diet bran is a dietary supplement used to enhance mealworm survival and mitigate oxidative stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"Co-diet bran refers to the supplementation of bran alongside polyethylene in the diet of mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Co-diet bran refers to the supplementation of bran with PE to reduce oxidative stress in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Co-diet bran is a supplementary food source used in combination with plastics to improve the health and physiological performance of larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Supplementing co-diet bran alleviates oxidative stress in mealworms exposed to MPs."&lt;SEP&gt;"Supplementation of co-diet bran enhances the PP consumption rate in insect larvae, similar to previous observations on PS and LDPE degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYACTIC ACID (PLA)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PLA (Polyactic Acid) is a biodegradable plastic often compared to polyethylene in terms of degradation rates."&lt;SEP&gt;"PLA is a type of biodegradable plastic that accelerates pupation but prevents eclosion in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyactic acid is a biodegradable plastic that mealworm larvae have been shown to break down."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyactic acid is a biodegradable polymer with potential applications in various industries."&lt;SEP&gt;"PLA is a biodegradable plastic evaluated for its degradation by insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"PLA, or polyactic acid, is a bioplastic that T. molitor larvae can hydrolyze."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyactic acid is a biodegradable plastic that earthworms can degrade easily but excrete slowly."&lt;SEP&gt;"PLA is a bio-based plastic that earthworms can disintegrate into sub-micron microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;1,2,4-TRICHLOROBENZENE (1,2,4-TCB)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene is a chemical reagent used in extraction experiments to measure fractions of frass residue."&lt;SEP&gt;"1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene is a solvent used for extracting and dissolving polypropylene during HT-GPC analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HIGH-TEMPERATURE GEL PERMEATION CHROMATOGRAPHY (HT-GPC)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"HT-GPC is a method used to analyze the molecular weight distribution of polymers during the degradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"HT-GPC is a process used to determine the molecular weight of PP polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"HT-GPC is a technique employed to analyze the depolymerization and biodegradation of ingested PE microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Technique used to characterize molecular weight changes in PE after bacterial treatment."&lt;SEP&gt;"HT-GPC is utilized to determine molecular weight values of virgin and residual PE-MPs."&lt;SEP&gt;"HT-GPC is a process used to analyze the molecular weight of polymers extracted from frass samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"HT-GPC is a technique used to determine molecular weight parameters of polymers such as \(M_{\mathrm{n}}, M_{\mathrm{w}}\), and polydispersity index (PDI)."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-temperature gel permeation chromatography determines molecular weight distributions of polymers at elevated temperatures."&lt;SEP&gt;"HT-GPC is an advanced technique used to analyze changes in molecular weight during depolymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"HT-GPC is employed to identify the depolymerization of PE polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SURVIVAL RATE (SR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"HEALTH MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A metric reflecting the proportion of larvae surviving under specific dietary conditions, such as those fed different types of PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"Percentage of mealworm larvae surviving over a specific period under various diet conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate measures the proportion of mealworm larvae surviving over a six-week test period."&lt;SEP&gt;"SR indicates the percentage of organisms surviving under specific conditions, such as during PET feeding experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival Rate is a measure of the percentage of live larvae remaining over time, calculated using the formula SR = N_c / N × 100% where N is the initial number of larvae and N_c is the number of live larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate quantifies the percentage of larvae surviving under different dietary conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival Rate measures the percentage of mealworms surviving over a 98-day period under different feeding conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"The survival rate measures the viability of mealworms when fed Styrofoam compared to conventional diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate assesses the viability of organisms exposed to microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival Rate measures the percentage of live lesser waxworms compared to the initial number."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate quantifies the percentage of waxworms surviving under specific dietary conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival Rate measures the percentage of mealworms surviving under different feeding conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival Rate measures the proportion of mealworms surviving under different feeding conditions over time."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate is a measure used to determine the viability of mealworms under different dietary conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate measures the percentage of mealworms that survived under different feeding conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5af82472112cbc9e76b1fd1ac6e8c54e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326</data>
    </node>
