    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROLASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydrolase is an enzyme involved in the biodegradation of ingested plastics by mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrolase is an enzyme that catalyzes the hydrolysis of PS derivatives during anaerobic biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OXIDASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Oxidase is an enzyme involved in oxidation reactions, tested using tetramethyl-p-phenylenediamine for the oxidase activity of BIT-26T."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidase is an enzyme that catalyzes oxidation reactions involving molecular oxygen."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidase is an enzyme whose activity differs between variants and depends on the diet provided to larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidase is another enzyme involved in the digestion and biodegradation processes of plastics within mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidase is an enzyme whose activity differs between variants and depends on the diet provided to larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ce1f7a057da80b206f8354c70d120c9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ROS LEVELS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Reactive oxygen species (ROS) levels indicate oxidative stress in mealworm larvae after PE ingestion."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MDA CONTENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIPID"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Malondialdehyde (MDA) content measures lipid peroxidation and oxidative damage in mealworm larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SOD&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"SOD (Superoxide Dismutase) is an enzyme that protects cells from oxidative damage by catalyzing the dismutation of superoxide radicals."&lt;SEP&gt;"SOD is an antioxidant enzyme that helps to scavenge free radicals in the organism."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superoxide dismutase (SOD) is an antioxidant enzyme that helps eliminate superoxide radicals in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superoxide dismutase (SOD) is an antioxidant enzyme that converts superoxide anions into hydrogen peroxide and oxygen, protecting tissues from ROS damage."&lt;SEP&gt;"SOD (superoxide dismutase) is an enzyme involved in antioxidant defense mechanisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superoxide dismutase (SOD) is an activated antioxidant enzyme that scavenges excess peroxidative free radicals, reducing oxidative stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superoxide dismutase is an enzyme involved in mitigating oxidative stress in organisms exposed to plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superoxide dismutase (SOD) activity was analyzed to evaluate antioxidant performance."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GST&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"GST (Glutathione-S-transferase) is an enzyme involved in detoxification processes by conjugating glutathione to toxins."&lt;SEP&gt;"GST scavenges metabolites of lipid peroxidation, playing a role in the antioxidant defense system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Glutathione S-transferase (GST) scavenges metabolites of lipid peroxidation in mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Glutathione-S-transferase (GST) scavenges metabolites of lipid peroxidation and helps alleviate oxidative damage caused by ROS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CAT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"CAT is an enzyme involved in the breakdown of hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen, contributing to the antioxidant system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Catalase (CAT) is an enzyme that protects cells from oxidative damage by breaking down hydrogen peroxide."&lt;SEP&gt;"Catalase (CAT) breaks down hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen, playing a role in reducing oxidative stress."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STORED FAT CONTENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIPID"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Stored fat content in mealworm larvae is decomposed during the biodegradation process, prioritized over protein decomposition."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DIGESTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Digestion is the process by which mealworm larvae break down ingested materials, including PE, into absorbable nutrients."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestion is the process of breaking down food into nutrients that the body can use, occurring intensively in this context."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestion refers to the process by which mealworms break down food materials, including synthetic polymers, into absorbable nutrients."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestion refers to the biological process by which nutrients are broken down in the velvetbean caterpillar with the help of gut bacteria."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aad5e97885ea1116956513db244b8fb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SURVIVAL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Survival describes an organism's ability to endure under starvation conditions based on somatic maintenance costs."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival indicates the ability of mealworm larvae to withstand environmental stressors such as microplastic exposure."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival measures the ability of mealworm larvae to live under different dietary conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival indicates the ability of mealworms to withstand environmental stressors, such as microplastic feeding."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GROWTH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Growth indicates the increase in size and development of mealworm larvae under various feeding conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Growth refers to the increase in size or weight of mealworms over time, influenced by diet and microplastic exposure."&lt;SEP&gt;"Growth represents the increase in size and development of mealworms influenced by dietary factors such as plastic consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;"Growth refers to the biological development that can be negatively impacted by plastic contamination in aquatic environments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Growth refers to the increase in size and weight of mealworms during their developmental stages."&lt;SEP&gt;"Growth represents the developmental process of larvae, hindered by nutritional deficiencies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Growth is the process of increasing in size and complexity, critical for the development of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Growth of mealworms is modulated as a result of exposure to ultrahigh-MW polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Growth refers to the increase in biomass and volumetric length of T. molitor larvae, influenced by dietary conditions and observed through DEB model fitting."&lt;SEP&gt;"Growth refers to the increase in size or development of structures in organisms, inhibited by starvation and insufficient diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Growth represents the increase in size or mass of an organism, driven by energy allocation from reserves."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff228c7711f94fb27152b187b1fa3eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METABOLIC TURNOVER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Metabolic turnover refers to the rate at which mealworm larvae metabolize nutrients and energy."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CELLULAR STRUCTURE&quot;">
