    <node id="&quot;LARVAL TISSUES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL FEATURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Larval tissues refer to the developing tissues of mealworm larvae, which are protected by antioxidant defenses."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OXIDATIVE DAMAGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Oxidative damage occurs in organisms due to the presence of reactive oxygen species induced by microplastic exposure."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidative damage occurs when ROS levels overwhelm antioxidant defenses, causing cellular harm."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidative damage refers to harm caused by reactive oxygen species, affecting cellular structures and functions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NANOPLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">Nanoplastics are extremely small plastic particles, typically measuring less than 100 nm in size and often derived from the fragmentation of larger plastics. They are smaller than microplastics and can penetrate cellular structures, causing genotoxicity, organ damage, and physiological effects in organisms, including invertebrates. Due to their ultra-small size, nanoplastics have high mobility, increased toxicity, and the ability to interact with other pollutants, contributing significantly to environmental contamination.

These particles can disrupt biological processes in plants and other organisms, affect microbial signaling, and cause issues such as infertility and inflammation in aquatic species. Nanoplastics may also pose risks to human health due to their persistence and capacity to penetrate biological barriers, raising additional ecological and health concerns. Although some studies have reported the absence of nanoplastics in certain contexts, they are considered emerging environmental contaminants, particularly when derived from materials like polyethylene (PE).

Additionally, specific bacterial strains isolated from insect larvae, such as those found in mealworm frass, can degrade nanoplastics. Techniques such as microbial digestion and physical separation methods are used to isolate these tiny plastic particles from complex matrices. Despite ongoing research, nanoplastics remain a significant concern for both environmental and biological systems due to their potential to cause cellular damage, toxicity, and broader ecosystem disruption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606</data>
    </node>
