      <data key="d1">"An insect whose gut harbors Pseudomonas aeruginosa capable of degrading PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms can degrade low-density polyethylene and polystyrene, with evidence of both fragmentation and shifts in gut microbiota."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms, or Zophobas atratus, demonstrate limited biodegradation potential for low-density polyethylene and polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms are larvae capable of breaking down and converting polystyrene into minerals."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms are larvae capable of surviving and consuming plastics like polystyrene and polyurethane, exhibiting higher plastic consumption rates compared to yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms are larvae capable of biodegrading polypropylene, similar to mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms are larvae of Zophobas atratus, known for harboring plastic-degrading bacteria in their gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms are the larvae of Zophobas atratus, known for their ability to harbor bacteria capable of degrading plastics like PS under aerobic conditions."</data>
