    <node id="&quot;DARK MEALWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dark Mealworms (larvae of Tenebrio obscurus) are insects involved in the biodegradation of low-density polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dark Mealworms (larvae of Tenebrio obscurus) exhibit plastic-degrading capabilities similar to those of yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dark mealworms (Tenebrio obscurus) are another species of insect larvae involved in the biodegradation of polymers such as polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dark mealworms (larvae of Tenebrio obscurus) are another species capable of biodegrading plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dark mealworms, larvae of Tenebrio obscurus, contribute to the biodegradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dark mealworms (Tenebrio obscurus larvae) have been shown to degrade plastics such as polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dark mealworms (Tenebrio obscurus) contribute to the biodegradation of polystyrene alongside yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dark mealworms (larvae of Tenebrio obscurus) contribute to the biodegradation of low-density polyethylene through gut microbe-independent mechanisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dark mealworms (Tenebrio obscurus) are insects capable of biodegrading polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dark mealworms (larvae of Tenebrio obscurus) exhibit gut microbe-independent mechanisms for the depolymerization of low-density polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dark mealworms, or Tenebrio obscurus, are another species of darkling beetle larvae studied for plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dark mealworms refer to the larval stage of Tenebrio obscurus, also capable of degrading plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Larvae of T. obscurus, demonstrating LDPE biodegradation capabilities similar to T. molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Larvae of Tenebrio obscurus, capable of digesting and biodegrading polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT MICROBES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROBE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gut microbes in mealworms produce substances that increase microbiological respiration, collaborating with host enzymes for efficient plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbes refer to the microorganisms residing in the digestive tracts of various organisms, including insects like mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae), superworms, and lesser waxworms. These microorganisms play a critical role in digestion, metabolism, immune function, and the biodegradation of plastics and other materials. 

Specifically, gut microbes contribute to the depolymerization and biodegradation of polymers such as polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), polypropylene (PP), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET) within the digestive systems of their host organisms. They exhibit chemotaxis towards aromatic compounds like benzoate, amino benzoate, and fluorobenzoate, which may enhance their ability to degrade plastic metabolites. 

In Tenebrio molitor larvae, gut microbes are essential for the enzymatic degradation of microplastics, LDPE (low-density polyethylene), PS foam, and PP foam. While some studies suggest that these microbes work synergistically with the host larvae to facilitate plastic degradation, others indicate that they may not always be essential for the process but can significantly enhance it. 

Antibiotic suppression tests have provided evidence of the crucial role played by gut microbes in the biodegradation of plastics, although their involvement may vary depending on species and environmental conditions. Microscopic observations confirm the activities of gut microbes during the biodegradation process, highlighting their importance in breaking down ingested plastics within certain insect species. 

Overall, gut microbes represent a key component of the biodegradation processes occurring in the digestive systems of larvae and other insects, contributing to the breakdown of complex polymers and supporting the health and functionality of their hosts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
