      <data key="d1">"Galleria mellonella larvae contribute to the biodegradation of polyethylene and polystyrene, similar to mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic degradation refers to the biological breakdown of plastic materials, associated with specific gut fungi such as Issatchenka, Rhodotorula, and Cryptococcus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic degradation refers to the breakdown of plastic materials into simpler compounds, often mediated by microbial activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process by which plastics are broken down into smaller components by biological or chemical means."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic degradation is the process by which plastics are broken down into smaller components, often involving enzymatic or microbial activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic Degradation refers to the metabolic activity of bacteria breaking down plastics in the larval gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process by which Tenebrio larvae break down plastic polymers using their gut microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic degradation is the process by which microorganisms break down plastics into simpler compounds, potentially contributing to environmental cleanup."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic degradation refers to the biological process by which microorganisms break down plastic materials, potentially altering gut microbial communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic degradation refers to the breakdown of plastic materials, potentially mediated by microbial activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic Degradation involves breaking down synthetic polymers, possibly mediated by gut microbes in A. diaperinus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic degradation refers to the biological breakdown of plastic materials by microorganisms and their enzymes."&lt;SEP&gt;"The enzymatic breakdown of plastic materials by microorganisms within the gut of yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic degradation involves the breakdown of plastics into smaller components through biological or chemical processes."</data>
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
