    <node id="&quot;PROTEOBACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYLUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">**Proteobacteria** is a major bacterial phylum that plays a significant role in the gut microbiome of insects, particularly in the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworms). This phylum encompasses gram-negative bacteria and includes diverse classes such as Alphaproteobacteria and Betaproteobacteria. It also comprises several important genera, including *Acinetobacter*, *Serratia*, *Pseudomonas*, *Cronobacter*, *Kosakonia*, *Aeromonas*, and *Klebsiella pneumoniae*. Proteobacteria is notable for its versatility and functional importance, contributing to processes such as the degradation of natural polymers and environmental pollutants.

In mealworms, Proteobacteria represents one of the dominant bacterial communities within the gut microbiota. Its relative abundance varies depending on the larval diet, with studies showing both increases and decreases under different feeding conditions. For example, the levels of Proteobacteria were observed to increase significantly in mealworms fed polyurethane (PU), polystyrene (PS), or PVC, suggesting their potential role in degrading plastics and other pollutants. Conversely, when larvae were fed diets containing PS, cellulose (CS), or PET, the relative abundance of Proteobacteria tended to decrease compared to those fed conventional diets. 

Interestingly, Proteobacteria exhibits an inverse relationship with another major phylum, Firmicutes, within the gut microbiota of mealworms. This dynamic balance between the two phyla highlights the adaptive nature of the gut microbial community in response to dietary changes. Proteobacteria serves as an indicator taxon, helping to elucidate shifts in microbial community composition driven by varying diets.

Despite fluctuations in abundance, Proteobacteria remains predominant in both PS-fed and control groups, with notable increases in relative frequency in PS samples compared to control samples. Additionally, it dominates in PU- and EVA-fed groups, reinforcing its significance in the gut bacterial community under these conditions. Proteobacteria's ability to degrade plastics makes it a key player in ecological processes, especially in bioremediation and insect-mediated plastic degradation.

Beyond its role in pollutant degradation, Proteobacteria supplies essential amino acids to its insect hosts, contributing to the overall health and functionality of the insect gut microbiome. This phylum is not only predominant in the digestive tracts of various insects but also crucial for maintaining the nutritional balance and metabolic activities necessary for</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19868f1234aac10de61c710c8352c076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608</data>
