    <node id="&quot;FIRMICUTES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYLUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Firmicutes is a bacterial phylum showing relatively stable proportions across all feeding variants."&lt;SEP&gt;"Firmicutes is a phylum of bacteria that includes classes such as Sphingobacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Firmicutes is another dominant bacterial phylum found in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;Firmicutes is a dominant bacterial phylum in the gut microbiome of mealworms, including species such as Tenebrio molitor larvae, and plays a significant role in plastic degradation processes. This phylum includes gram-positive bacteria and encompasses various classes, such as Sphingobacteria, and genera like Paraclostridium, contributing to the breakdown of plastics such as polyurethane (PU), polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), and high-density polyethylene (HDPE). 

Studies have shown that the relative abundance of Firmicutes can vary depending on the diet provided to the mealworms. For instance, Firmicutes was enriched in bran-fed mealworms, indicating dietary influence on gut microbiota. Conversely, its abundance decreased significantly in mealworms fed with PS diets but increased in groups fed with PU or cellulose (CS). Additionally, higher relative abundances were observed in PS- and CS-fed larvae compared to those fed with control bran-based diets. However, some studies noted a decrease in Firmicutes abundance in plastic-fed yellow mealworms compared to bran-fed controls.

Despite these variations, Firmicutes generally constitutes a significant portion of the gut microbiota in mealworms and has been found to maintain relatively stable proportions across different feeding conditions. It is also associated with PS-degrading strains, including *Exiguobacterium sp. YT2*, further highlighting its role in microbial plastic degradation. Overall, Firmicutes contributes significantly to the microbial diversity and functionality of the gut microbiome in mealworms, particularly in the context of degrading various types of plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACTINOBACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYLUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">Actinobacteria is a phylum of gram-positive bacteria that plays a significant role in the gut microbiomes of mealworms, particularly in species such as Tenebrio molitor and T. obscurus larvae. This bacterial phylum is often found in high abundance and has been identified as one of the dominant groups in the gut microbiota of mealworms across various feeding conditions, including those involving polystyrene and HDPE degradation.

Actinobacteria contributes to the biodegradation of plastics, with specific species like Rhodococcus ruber and Rhodococcus sp. demonstrating abilities to degrade styrene and other polymers. Additionally, genera such as Mycobacterium, Arthrobacter, Corynebacterium, and Kocuria are included within this phylum, each playing unique roles in nutrient provision and food processing within insect digestive tracts. Notably, Actinobacteria not only aids in breaking down complex materials but also supports the nutritional needs of its insect hosts.

While some descriptions refer to Actinobacteria as a class rather than a phylum, the prevailing consensus identifies it as a phylum-level taxon encompassing diverse classes and genera. Sequences related to Actinobacteria have even shown similarities to genes involved in alkane degradation, further underscoring its ecological significance. Overall, Actinobacteria is a key component of mealworm gut microbiomes, contributing both to plastic degradation and the overall health and sustenance of its host insects.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8b79a6f4c112b0b62ada6756140e3e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608</data>
