    <node id="&quot;GUT BACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROBE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacteria present in the gut of T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae, involved in LDPE biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut bacteria are microorganisms present in the digestive systems of T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae, potentially involved in polymer degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut bacteria are microorganisms present in the mealworm gut that contribute to nitrogen-fixing activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut bacteria are the bacterial communities residing in the digestive tract of organisms, playing a role in digestion and potentially plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;Gut bacteria are microorganisms residing in the digestive tracts of various animals, including insects such as the mealworm *Tenebrio molitor* and *T. obscurus*, as well as the velvetbean caterpillar (*Anticarsia gemmatalis*). These microorganisms play a crucial role in digestion, immune function, and the biodegradation of ingested materials. Specifically, gut bacteria contribute to the breakdown of complex polymers by secreting enzymes and extracellular depolymerases, which facilitate the depolymerization of substances like exopolysaccharides (EPS), polystyrene (PS), and Styrofoam.

In mealworms, gut bacteria have been shown to degrade plastics through oxidative enzymes, forming biofilms on polystyrene surfaces and generating cracks or holes observable via scanning electron microscopy. This process is supported by interactions with host larvae, involving altered gene expression that enhances plastic degradation. Studies using bacterial colony counts, PCR, and RT-qPCR techniques have demonstrated the ability of gut bacteria isolated from *T. molitor* larvae to grow on polystyrene as a sole carbon source, further emphasizing their role in metabolic degradation.

Additionally, antibiotic suppression experiments confirm the essential role of gut bacteria in the degradation of plastics such as PS within mealworms. Overall, gut bacteria are vital contributors to the development processes in certain organisms, the digestion of waste materials, and the biodegradation of environmentally persistent plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbes present in the digestive system of larvae, which are suppressed by antibiotics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut bacteria in mealworms contribute to the biodegradation of PS MPs by breaking down polymer chains nonspecifically."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut bacteria encompass various microorganisms residing in the gastrointestinal tract."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut bacteria play an important role in the digestion of various waste materials by secreting enzymes that break down complex polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e56bce8206396081e0b7dccc47a10c9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aad5e97885ea1116956513db244b8fb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e</data>
