      <data key="d1">"A statistical method used to analyze microbiota and chemical data in this study."&lt;SEP&gt;"A statistical method used to assess discrimination between different feeding strategies based on identified components."&lt;SEP&gt;"A statistical method used to identify patterns and relationships between variables."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCA is a statistical method used to differentiate samples based on metabolite profiles, showing distinct differences between PET-fed and agar-fed larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCA is a statistical method used to reduce the dimensionality of data while retaining trends and patterns."&lt;SEP&gt;"A statistical method used to assess discrimination between different feeding strategies."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCA was used to determine relationships between observations based on the Pearson correlation-similarity matrix."&lt;SEP&gt;"Principal Component Analysis is a statistical method used to test correlations between diets and examined parameters."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCA is a statistical method used to analyze and visualize the variance among different groups of gut metabolites, showing distinct clustering patterns based on dietary conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCA is a statistical technique used for analyzing and visualizing patterns in gut microbiome data."&lt;SEP&gt;"Principal component analysis (PCA) is a statistical technique used to reduce data dimensions and visualize patterns, applied here to microbial communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCA is a statistical method used to model differences in metabolic profiles between control insect samples and various feeding conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Statistical method used to evaluate results from PS biodegradation, showing relationships between variables."&lt;SEP&gt;"Principal Component Analysis (PCA) based on Unweighted UniFrac distinguishes core gut fungi across different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCA is a statistical technique used to analyze genus-level compositional profiles of microbial communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"A statistical method used to identify patterns and relationships between variables."</data>
