    <node id="&quot;T. MOLITOR LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"T. molitor larvae represent the larval stage of the mealworm beetle used in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae, or mealworms, possess a remarkable capacity to degrade plastics due to their gut microbiota."&lt;SEP&gt;**T. molitor larvae**, the larval stage of the mealworm beetle (*Tenebrio molitor*), are insect organisms renowned for their unique ability to biodegrade various types of plastics, including polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polystyrene (PS), expanded polystyrene (EPS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and polypropylene (PP). These larvae play a crucial role in experiments focused on plastic degradation, where they consume microplastics and produce chemical compounds as byproducts. Their enzymatic activity enables them to break down long-chain polymers, allowing them to use plastics as an energy source while impacting their growth, weight changes, and survival rates depending on the type of plastic provided in their diet.

The gut microbiome of **T. molitor larvae** is highly dynamic and adapts when exposed to different materials, such as polystyrene. Microbial species like *Lactococcus* and *Exiguobacterium sp. YT2* associated with these larvae contribute significantly to the degradation of plastics and other substrates, such as agar. This symbiotic relationship between the larvae and their gut microbiota facilitates the breakdown of complex polymers through enzymatic processes and microbial interactions.

Research has shown that **T. molitor larvae** exhibit high efficiency in degrading polymers like PET, PS, and PP, making them valuable model organisms for understanding the mechanisms of biodegradation. Their study offers promising insights into addressing plastic pollution and enhancing environmental sustainability by leveraging biological and microbial processes involved in plastic degradation. Overall, **T. molitor larvae** represent a key biological solution for managing plastic waste due to their ability to degrade plastics with varying molecular weights.&lt;SEP&gt;"\(T. molitor\) larvae are beetle larvae capable of processing various plastics and utilizing their gut microbiome for biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c5fc4d4324334254706d6d5f9312276&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47c76e5149ac059b1960b533c9e99a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
