    <node id="&quot;ENTEROCOCCUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial genus with differential abundance between styrene and ethylene diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"A genus within Firmicutes associated with the PU diet, known to be involved in PU degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a bacterial genus detected in the gut microbiome of mealworms, contributing to the microbial diversity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus of bacteria associated with PE diet in the gut of both yellow and black mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus of bacteria capable of producing esterases and participating in compound degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus of gram-positive bacteria belonging to the order Bacilli."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus of bacteria found predominantly in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is commonly detected in both Tenebrio species across all treatments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus of bacteria found dominant in the gut microbiomes of larvae consuming plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus of bacteria identified as part of the gut microbiome in yellow mealworms and dark mealworms, contributing to the community diversity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is another common genus in the mealworm gut microbiome, absent in the foregut and anterior midgut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus of bacteria identified in the gut of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"A genus of bacteria found in the gut of insect larvae contributing to PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus of bacteria found in the guts of all three species' larvae, playing a crucial role in polystyrene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus of bacteria found within the gut microbiota of the three insect species studied, potentially aiding in PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus of bacteria with significant changes in abundance when associated with specific diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a bacterial genus capable of producing esterases, playing a role in the degradation of substrates such as indoxacarb insecticide."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus of gram-positive bacteria belonging to the order Bacilli."</data>
