      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nitrosomonas is a bacterial genus associated with nitrogen fixation in the polystyrene diet microbiota."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrosomonas is a genus of bacteria capable of nitrogen fixation, found in the microbiota of polystyrene-fed larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d47c76e5149ac059b1960b533c9e99a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NITROSIRA SPP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nitrosira is a bacterial genus linked to nitrogen fixation in the polystyrene diet microbiota."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrosira is a genus of bacteria involved in nitrogen fixation, characteristic of the polystyrene diet."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d47c76e5149ac059b1960b533c9e99a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ESTERASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme that catalyzes the hydrolysis of esters into an acid and an alcohol."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterase is an enzyme whose activity increases in larvae fed on polyethylene diets, indicating enhanced enzymatic activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterase is another enzyme detected in culturable gut bacterial flora isolated from diamondback moth, contributing to indoxylcarb degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterase is an enzyme involved in breaking down ester bonds in various substrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterase is an enzyme involved in the enzymatic recycling of thermoplastic polyurethanes, contributing to the breakdown of ester bonds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterase is an enzyme whose activity increases in larvae fed on polyethylene diets."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c68f3ddf96d927254424a036cd8b9acf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47c76e5149ac059b1960b533c9e99a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SYMBIONTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Symbionts are microorganisms that live in a mutualistic relationship with their host, promoting nitrogen assimilation and survival in nutrient-deficient environments."</data>
