    <node id="&quot;PRIESTIA MEGATERIUM S1&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Priestia megaterium S1 is a polymer-degrading gut microbe isolated from T. molitor larvae, as mentioned in related literature."&lt;SEP&gt;"Priestia megaterium S1 is a polymer-degrading microbe isolated from the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, characterized by its effective biodegradation capacity on plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Priestia megaterium S1 is the specific bacterial strain responsible for the degradation of LDPE, HDPE, and PS beads."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STYROFOAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">STYROFOAM, a brand name for expanded polystyrene foam, is the most popular form of polystyrene and is primarily used in insulation boards, packaging materials, food packaging, foam insulation, insulating building elevations, disposable cups, boxes, and cushioning materials in packaging. It is a lightweight synthetic polymer made up of over 98% polystyrene (PS), known for its low density and hygroscopic properties. Despite its utility, STYROFOAM contributes to environmental concerns such as marine debris due to its non-biodegradable nature when left untreated.

Recent scientific studies have explored the potential biodegradability of STYROFOAM by certain insects, particularly mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae) and superworms (*Zophobas morio* larvae). In these experiments, STYROFOAM serves as both an energy source and nutrient source for the larvae, with microorganisms present in their gut enabling its degradation. Mealworms are capable of consuming and breaking down the material, converting it into carbon dioxide within 24 hours. While STYROFOAM lacks sufficient nutrients for sustained growth on its own, it provides an energy source when mineralized, allowing mealworms to survive and grow while consuming it in controlled experimental settings. 

Additionally, STYROFOAM can undergo thermal decomposition, producing styrene as a primary degradation product. Traditionally considered non-biodegradable plastic waste, ongoing research indicates that mealworms and superworms can effectively break down STYROFOAM, offering promising applications in waste management. Studies using STYROFOAM as a control material have identified certain gut bacteria found in *Tenebrio molitor* L. that contribute to its degradation. These findings suggest not only environmental benefits but also potential applications in animal or human nutrition, although further investigation is required to fully understand and optimize these processes. The use of STYROFOAM in experiments studying nitrogen fixation further underscores its significance in addressing environmental challenges associated with polystyrene-based products.

Overall, STYROFOAM's role in biodegradation experiments highlights its potential to contribute to innovative solutions for reducing plastic waste and addressing environmental concerns.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494