    <node id="&quot;TEMPERATURE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Temperature is a critical factor influencing the development and survival of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Temperature is a physical property that was maintained at \(25 \pm 0.5^{\circ} \mathrm{C}\) in the experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Temperature plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics, influencing the rate and efficiency of the process."&lt;SEP&gt;"The consistent maintenance of temperature at 37 °C during the experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;Temperature is a critical environmental factor that significantly influences the growth, activity, and survival of mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae), as well as their ability to degrade plastics such as polystyrene (PS). This physical property plays a pivotal role in determining the metabolic rates of organisms involved in biodegradation, including both mealworms and bacteria. 

In experimental setups, temperature is often carefully controlled to optimize conditions for larval growth, microbial activity, and plastic degradation. Optimal temperatures for these processes range between 20°C and 30°C, with specific studies indicating that 25°C ± 1°C and 28°C ± 1°C are particularly effective for enhancing PS degradation efficiency and mealworm survival. For example, experiments have shown that the highest rates of polystyrene removal occur at around 25°C, while other studies suggest that 28°C is ideal for the enzymatic activity of certain biodegradation processes, such as those involving PM S1.

Temperature also affects the eating habits, mobility, and overall intake capacity of mealworms, influencing their development speed and rearing efficiency. It has been observed that variations in temperature can alter the plastic consumption rates and survival rates of mealworms, emphasizing its importance as a controlled variable in scientific investigations. Furthermore, temperature directly impacts bacterial growth and activity, which are essential components of the biodegradation process.

Overall, temperature serves as a key determinant in the success of biodepolymerization by mealworms and associated microorganisms, ensuring optimal conditions for the efficient breakdown of plastics. Its influence extends across multiple biological processes, making it an indispensable parameter in both laboratory experiments and natural ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;"The temperature was maintained at 25 ± 0.5 °C to simulate optimal growth conditions for mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Temperature was maintained at \(25 \pm 0.5\) \({ }^{\circ} \mathrm{C}\) during the experiment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-372aa232f6e8aad1d8e0a93f16a42b0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014b72d0a2657cbc89c4dee5f2b32bba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c16f995987af72f86486559556052e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d01d291012bd548fc067e250ec39fba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
