    <node id="&quot;CO2&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carbon dioxide is a byproduct of polystyrene biodegradation, with approximately 47.7% of ingested Styrofoam carbon being converted into CO2."&lt;SEP&gt;**CO2 (Carbon Dioxide): A Key Metabolic Byproduct in Plastic Biodegradation**

CO2 (carbon dioxide) is a critical metabolic byproduct generated during the biodegradation of various plastics, including polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), expanded polystyrene (EPS), and low-density polyethylene (LDPE). This process involves both microbial activity and the digestive actions of organisms such as mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae), superworms, and larvae of *Tribolium molitor*. CO2 serves as an indicator of the mineralization of plastics, reflecting their conversion into simpler compounds.

During the degradation of plastics like PS, approximately 47.7% of the ingested carbon can be converted into CO2, highlighting its role as a final product in the complete degradation of styrene and related compounds. Microbes such as *Exiguobacterium sp.* strain YT2 and strain LG3 contribute to this process, producing CO2 as a byproduct of their metabolic activities under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions. CO2 is also measured using methods like the Sturm Test, respirometry tests, gas chromatography, and headspace analysis in serum bottles to assess microbial activity and confirm the breakdown of ingested plastics.

In the case of PE degradation within mealworms, nearly 40% of the ingested carbon is converted into CO2, underscoring its significance as a metabolic product formed from the breakdown of ingested EPS carbon in the gut of these larvae. Similarly, during LDPE digestion, CO2 is produced as one of the end products of oxidation during the biodegradation process. The concentration of CO2 in the headspace of certain experimental setups, such as those involving strain LG3, has been observed to increase significantly over time under anaerobic conditions, further confirming its role as the end product of anaerobic biodegradation.

In summary, CO2 is a vital metabolic byproduct of the biodegradation processes involving both microbial respiration and larval digestion. It plays a significant role in confirming the mineralization and degradation of plastics such as PE, PS, PET, EPS, and LDPE, serving as a measurable indicator of successful plastic breakdown.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc75b7e672a7f7c036713dfa2675d79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26</data>
    </node>
