    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic is a material that undergoes degradation processes, with its weight changing due to ingestion and egestion by insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic is a synthetic material made from polymers and widely used in various industries."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic is used as a diet component in the experiment but cannot provide sufficient nutrition to support larval growth and development."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic materials are widely used due to their low cost, hardness, durability, and lightweight. They include petroleum-based plastics such as Polystyrene (PS) and Polyethylene (PE&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic served as the sole feedstock for larvae in the experiment, affecting their growth and survival."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastics such as PE, PS, PP, and PVC undergo degradation processes mediated by gut bacteria and extracellular depolymerases."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic refers to synthetic materials that are tested for microbial degradation and surface properties."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastics such as PE, PP, PVC, PET, PUR, PLA, etc., are polymers that T. molitor larvae degrade."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic refers to solid hydrocarbons such as plastic materials, which serve as a carbon source in experiments involving microbial degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic represents synthetic materials like polystyrene, which insects consume leading to metabolic stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic refers to synthetic materials like PET and PS foam used as a diet source for larvae in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic refers to materials made from polymers, which can enter compost and agricultural soils."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic refers to synthetic materials that insects can degrade or metabolize, serving as carbon substrates for their metabolic processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastics, including PET, PE, PS, Expanded PE, PP, and PLA, were used as the primary carbon source for the insect models."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic refers to synthetic materials made from polymers, which have become a significant environmental concern due to their persistence and pollution potential."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic refers to materials like polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE) that are being biodegraded by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic refers to various types of materials tested in the experiment, including PS, PVC, PP, HDPE, and LDPE, which were consumed by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastics, including PP, HDPE, LDPE, and PVC, were tested as food sources for mealworms to observe their metabolic responses."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic refers to materials like PP and PU, which are widely used in manufacturing."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7043eb8aa4221f396a5c65fb6493bd41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b47eb2d42413e11dbd4e9bb9f2df12f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f4c282fe2323dc6e310501af888c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LOW DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of plastic polymer used as feedstock for degradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE is a form of polyethylene characterized by its molecular formula -(CH₂-CH₂&lt;SEP&gt;"Low Density Polyethylene (LDPE) is a type of plastic used in experiments as a diet for larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE is a type of plastic used in various applications and can be pyrolyzed to produce petrochemical feedstock."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE is a type of plastic that is the focus of microbial degradation studies, often used in packaging materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is a plastic material that can be degraded by microbial activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low Density Polyethylene (LDPE) is a type of plastic that can be degraded by bacterial consortia isolated from plastic garbage processing areas."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low Density Polyethylene is a form of polyethylene, widely used in daily life, occupying 32% of global plastic consumption in 2015 and 39.8% in 2019."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low density polyethylene is a type of plastic reported to be degraded by certain insect species."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE is a type of plastic that undergoes biodegradation through microbial activity and has effects on soil bacterial communities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81b6237c84f002117fbfe7575d9cdd6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HIGH DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (HDPE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of plastic polymer used as feedstock for degradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is another form of polyethylene with the same molecular formula -(CH₂-CH₂</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
