    <node id="&quot;PEPTONE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Peptone is a component of Blood Agar that provides essential nutrients for microbial growth."&lt;SEP&gt;"Peptone is a component of MRD liquid, providing nitrogenous compounds for microbial growth."&lt;SEP&gt;"Peptone is a source of amino acids and peptides in nutrient broth, supporting bacterial growth."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NACL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A chemical component used in microbial media preparation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sodium chloride (NaCl) is added to nutrient broth to maintain osmotic balance for bacterial cultures."&lt;SEP&gt;"NaCl (sodium chloride) is a key ingredient in the preparation of the saline solution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sodium chloride (NaCl) regulates osmotic balance in Blood Agar."&lt;SEP&gt;"NaCl is sodium chloride, used in both the inorganic salt medium and saline solution for osmotic balance."&lt;SEP&gt;"NaCl (sodium chloride) is added to various media formulations, including LCFBM and saline water, to maintain osmotic balance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sodium chloride maintains osmotic balance in CFBM."&lt;SEP&gt;"NaCl (sodium chloride) concentration affects bacterial growth and is expressed as a percentage by weight."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sodium chloride, a component of LB medium providing osmotic balance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sodium chloride, a salt used in the preparation of LB medium to maintain osmotic balance."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014b72d0a2657cbc89c4dee5f2b32bba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TOLUENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TOXIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A harmful compound detected in PS samples but not in the gut and frass of larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Toluene is used in the MATH test to assess the hydrophobicity of bacterial cell surfaces."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AUGUSTA ET AL. 1993&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"RESEARCH PAPER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Augusta et al. 1993 describes the methodology for performing the clear zone assay to detect enzymatic activity against plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ROSENBERG ET AL. 1980&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"RESEARCH PAPER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Rosenberg et al. 1980 outlines the MATH test method for measuring bacterial cell surface hydrophobicity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MEALWORM GUT BACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Diverse microorganisms inhabiting the midgut content of Styrofoam-eating mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworm Gut Bacteria are microorganisms isolated from the gut of mealworms, capable of degrading plastics such as LDPE, HDPE, and PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworm gut bacteria are the source of isolates S1 to S5, capable of degrading polymers like LDPE, HDPE, and PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworm gut bacteria are microorganisms inhabiting the digestive system of mealworms and targeted by antibiotic screening."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOFILM FORMATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biofilm Formation describes the colonization of microorganisms on plastic surfaces during degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm Formation refers to the process where bacteria adhere to surfaces and form structured communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation refers to the process by which microorganisms adhere to surfaces and develop complex structures."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation involves the colonization of surfaces by microorganisms, forming structured communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation refers to the aggregation of bacteria on surfaces, enhancing their survival and activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm Formation is a process where Rhodococcus ruber forms structured communities on surfaces, aiding in biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is a process analyzed statistically to evaluate microbial activity related to NPS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process by which microorganisms form communities on surfaces, often associated with biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is a physiological change exhibited by bacteria like Enterobacter hormaechei LG3 during PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process by which microorganisms form structured communities on surfaces."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process of forming biofilms on polystyrene surfaces by certain bacteria."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56425bd62513be46b9165bfd84f0dd0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aad5e97885ea1116956513db244b8fb6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;16S RRNA GENE SEQUENCING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"16S rRNA Gene Sequencing is a technique used to identify and classify bacterial species based on their ribosomal RNA sequences."&lt;SEP&gt;"Illumina MiSeq sequencing of the 16S rRNA gene is used to investigate the effect of plastic diets on the bacterial community within the gut of larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sequencing of the V3/V4 regions of the 16S rRNA gene analyzed gut microbial community structure."&lt;SEP&gt;"16S rRNA gene sequencing is used to analyze microbial community structure."&lt;SEP&gt;"16S rRNA gene sequencing is used to identify gut microbiota in mealworms and other organisms involved in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"16S rRNA gene sequencing is a technique suggested for identifying specific genera within unclassified Enterobacteriaceae involved in plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SEM ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"SEM (Scanning Electron Microscopy) provides detailed images of polyurethane surfaces to observe physical changes during degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"SEM Analysis (Scanning Electron Microscopy) examines surface changes in plastic beads after microbial degradation at high magnification."&lt;SEP&gt;"SEM Analysis examines surface changes in plastic beads after microbial degradation at high magnification."&lt;SEP&gt;"SEM Analysis reveals structural changes on the surface of LDPE, HDPE, and PS beads, indicating biodegradation by PM S1."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BOD (BIOCHEMICAL OXYGEN DEMAND)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"BOD measures the amount of oxygen required by microorganisms to break down organic material in water."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PRESTIA MEGATERIUM S1&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Prestia megaterium S1 is a bacterial species identified through 16S rRNA gene sequencing, belonging to the family Bacillaceae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACILLACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacillaceae family was enriched in bran-fed mealworms, indicating bacterial composition shifts due to diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillaceae is a bacterial family prevalent in the gut microbiota of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillaceae is a bacterial family that includes genera such as Bacillus, Metabacillus, and Cytobacillus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillaceae is a family of gram-positive bacteria that includes Prestia megaterium S1 and related genera."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillaceae is a family of bacteria found in the anterior gut of mealworms when fed a standard diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillaceae is a bacterial family found to be more abundant in PS samples relative to CT samples."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FTIR SPECTROSCOPY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"FTIR Spectroscopy identifies chemical bonds and functional groups in materials before and after microbial treatment."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR Spectroscopy is a technique used to analyze chemical changes in LDPE beads during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR Spectroscopy is a technique used to analyze chemical changes in polymers during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR Spectroscopy is a technique used to analyze the chemical structure of materials by measuring absorption of infrared light."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR Spectroscopy is used to analyze functional groups and chemical moieties in polymer molecules before and after degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR Spectroscopy is a technique used to characterize chemical changes in PS films after microbial incubation."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR Spectroscopy is a method used to examine chemical alterations at the surface of residual PVC fragments."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectroscopy is used to analyze gaseous compounds produced during thermal decomposition, providing insights into chemical composition changes."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectroscopy analyzes chemical bonds in PU foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy identifies organic materials based on their molecular vibrations."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectroscopy is a technique used for the structural characterization of plastic debris, supported by statistical analysis methods."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectroscopy is an analytical technique used for identifying chemical substances based on their infrared spectra."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROBE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PM refers to a microbe that survives on different concentrations of carbon sources and is involved in the degradation of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STURM TEST&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Sturm Test confirms the biodegradation of LDPE, HDPE, and PS beads by measuring carbon dioxide production."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sturm Test is a method used to determine CO2 evolution due to plastic biodegradation, involving the measurement of carbon dioxide released during polymer breakdown."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KOH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"KOH (Potassium Hydroxide) is a chemical used to absorb CO2 generated from the breakdown of polymers during the Sturm Test."&lt;SEP&gt;"KOH (potassium hydroxide) is used to adjust the pH of the polymer emulsion to 7.0."&lt;SEP&gt;"KOH (potassium hydroxide) is used to digest bacterial growth during the NPS analysis process."&lt;SEP&gt;"KOH was used for microbial digestion at 10% concentration to digest microbial cells without affecting nanoplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"KOH is a chemical reagent used to digest intestine samples during the analysis of residual plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BARIUM CHLORIDE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Barium Chloride is a chemical used to precipitate CO2 in the form of barium carbonate during the Sturm Test."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMER FILMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polymer films are materials used in the Sturm Test to evaluate biodegradation by microbes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOFILM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">**Biofilm Summary:**

A biofilm is a structured community of microorganisms that adheres to surfaces, forming a cooperative and organized structure. These communities are enclosed in a self-produced extracellular matrix, which facilitates their attachment and provides structural integrity. Biofilm formation occurs through the colonization and growth of microorganisms on various surfaces, including plastic materials such as polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE), where it plays a significant role in biodegradation processes.

Biofilms can be formed by diverse microorganisms, such as bacteria like Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450, Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451, PELW2042, and gut bacteria. During the degradation of plastics, biofilm formation involves the production of extracellular polymeric substances (EPS) that enhance microbial attachment and support colonization. This process leads to physical and chemical changes in the material, such as the development of cracks and holes on the surface of PS films.

The formation of biofilms has been observed under specific environmental conditions, influencing their structure and function. For instance, biofilm formation was noted on PE mulching films and polystyrene sheets during studies involving strains such as LG3. SEM analysis has confirmed the adherence of microorganisms on the surface of plastic beads, providing visual evidence of biofilm structures.

Overall, biofilms contribute to the breakdown of materials, particularly plastics, by enhancing microbial attachment and promoting degradation mechanisms. They represent a complex ecological system where microorganisms interact with each other and their environment, playing critical roles in processes such as plastic degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787</data>
    </node>
