    <node id="&quot;LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"LDPE is another form of polyethylene that mealworms can degrade efficiently."&lt;SEP&gt;Low-Density Polyethylene (LDPE) is a type of polyethylene characterized by its low density, flexibility, and significant levels of both long-chain and short-chain branching. This structure results in lower crystallinity (approximately 40–50%), making LDPE less resistant to biodegradation compared to high-density polyethylene (HDPE). It is a thermoplastic polymer derived from petroleum and widely used in various applications such as packaging materials, plastic bags, and household products.

LDPE has been extensively studied for its biodegradability due to its susceptibility to microbial degradation. Microorganisms, including certain bacterial isolates like *Achromobacter denitrificans Ebl13* and other microbial species, can degrade LDPE, particularly after thermooxidative pretreatment. Additionally, larvae of insects such as *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworms) and *Corcyra cephalonica* have demonstrated the ability to consume and biodegrade LDPE, with evidence of depolymerization occurring during digestion. Residues from this process can be extracted from frass using solvents like 1,2-dichlorobenzene (1,2 DCB).

LDPE serves as a carbon source for bacteria and other organisms, contributing to its inclusion in larval diets and broader biodegradation studies. While it is hydrophobic and generally resistant to biodegradation, LDPE undergoes significant degradation and fragmentation in the gut of *Tenebrio molitor* larvae. Despite being less preferred by mealworms for consumption compared to other food sources, LDPE still shows signs of scratches, holes, and depolymerization when exposed to these larvae. 

As a microplastic, LDPE's lower crystallinity and more branched structure make it more amenable to biodegradation than other forms of polyethylene, positioning it as a key focus in efforts to address plastic waste through biological means. Its properties and applications highlight its importance in both industrial use and environmental research, with ongoing investigations into its biodegradation using isotopic and spectroscopic methods.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HIGH-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (HDPE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of plastic polymer that can be degraded by certain bacterial strains."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of polyethylene with higher crystallinity, making it more resistant to biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a sturdier variant of polyethylene and is being researched for microbial degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-Density Polyethylene is a polymer used in biodegradation studies, serving as a carbon source for bacterial isolates."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene (HDPE) is a more rigid form of polyethylene and is resistant to biodegradation but can be degraded by certain insect larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene is another form of polyethylene often used in various applications."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a type of plastic characterized by its stability and resistance to degradation, used in products like agricultural films, toys, and bags."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a type of polyethylene with long linear chains and minimal branching, characterized by high crystallinity (60–80%)."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a type of plastic that Achromobacter species have been observed breaking down."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a type of polyethylene that is particularly resistant to degradation and contributes significantly to environmental pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a type of polyethylene with high crystallinity and low branching, commonly used in plastic manufacturing."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-Density Polyethylene is a plastic type tested in the experiment, with several marks and holes caused by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene is a durable plastic type studied for mealworm consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene is also less favorable for mealworm consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene (HDPE) is a thermoplastic polymer used in various applications, including packaging materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a plastic material that can be colonized by microorganisms like Achromobacter xylosoxidans, contributing to its biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a durable form of polyethylene that mealworms can also consume and biodegrade."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HEXADECANE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hexadecane is a hydrocarbon used to measure bacterial hydrophobicity by observing its interaction with bacterial cells."&lt;SEP&gt;"This compound shows decreased excretion in all three diets compared to the control diet."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e4030ea35bee29093c1a8f19bc90774</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TURBIDITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Turbidity indicates bacterial growth in the culture, observable as cloudiness in the medium."&lt;SEP&gt;"Turbidity refers to the cloudiness of a solution, used here to measure bacterial transfer from aqueous to hexadecane phases."&lt;SEP&gt;"Turbidity measures the cloudiness of a liquid caused by suspended particles, such as bacteria or NPS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBON-STARVED CONDITIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONDITION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carbon-starved conditions describe an environment where bacteria lack sufficient carbon, influencing their hydrophobicity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NON-POLAR POLYMERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Non-polar polymers are materials that do not have a significant dipole moment, impacting bacterial degradation mechanisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLEAR ZONE TEST&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Clear zone test is a method to screen bacteria for their ability to degrade polymers by forming visible zones of clearance."&lt;SEP&gt;"The clear zone test identifies plastic-degrading microorganisms based on their ability to degrade plastics in agar media."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMER SOLUBILIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polymer solubilization refers to the breakdown of polymers into soluble components, facilitated by bacterial enzymes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PM S1&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PM S1 is a bacterial isolate identified from mealworm gut microbiome capable of degrading plastics like LDPE, HDPE, and PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"PM S1 is a microbial strain capable of degrading LDPE, HDPE, and PS beads. It consumes dissolved oxygen and produces carbon dioxide as part of its metabolic processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c</data>
