    <node id="&quot;HYDROGEN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydrogen is an element found in LDPE, HDPE, and PS beads, whose percentage also decreases during the degradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrogen is another element found in PP, contributing to the energy source for larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOLOGICAL OXYGEN DEMAND&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biological Oxygen Demand supports studies on weight loss and CO2 evolution during the degradation process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FUNCTIONAL GROUPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">Functional Groups are specific groups of atoms within molecules that determine the chemical properties and influence the characteristic chemical reactions of those molecules. These groups play a crucial role in the degradation processes of materials such as plastics, particularly when subjected to biological or enzymatic attacks, as seen in the gut of larvae like *Tenebrio molitor*. 

Changes in Functional Groups can indicate oxidation and biodegradation processes occurring during the breakdown of polymers, which is often observed in the frass (excrement) produced by these organisms after consuming plastics such as polystyrene (PS) or polyethylene (PE). The presence of new oxygen-containing functional groups, such as carbonyl (C=O), hydroxyl (R-OH), and ether (C-O) stretches, has been detected in the frass, suggesting intermediate chemical modifications caused by enzymatic activity.

These structural units can be identified and analyzed using techniques such as Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FTIR) or Attenuated Total Reflectance FTIR (ATR-FTIR), which detect the presence of specific atomic or molecular groups within compounds. This analysis helps to reveal the nature of polymer degradation, including the formation of new functional groups that arise during the biodegradation process. Overall, Functional Groups serve as critical indicators of chemical transformations and reactivity in both natural and synthetic materials undergoing degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140</data>
    </node>
