    <node id="&quot;BIODEGRADABLE BIOPLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biodegradable bioplastics are environmentally friendly plastics produced from biowastes as part of a circular bioeconomy."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RHODOCOCCUS RUBER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Rhodococcus ruber is a bacterium capable of colonizing and degrading polyethylene through biofilm formation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rhodococcus ruber is a bacterium capable of colonizing, forming biofilms, and biodegrading polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodococcus ruber is a bacterium belonging to the Phylum Actinobacteria and Family Nocardiaceae. It is an actinomycete species known for its versatile capabilities in degrading various types of plastics and hydrocarbons. Rhodococcus ruber is particularly noted for its ability to colonize surfaces, form biofilms, and degrade polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS). The bacterium achieves this through dedicated transport systems and enzymatic activity, enabling it to break down complex polymers such as polyethylene and extracellular polymeric substances (EPS).

Notably, Rhodococcus ruber has been observed to partially degrade polystyrene, contributing to bioremediation efforts. It employs different alkane hydroxylase systems to degrade hexane and related hydrocarbons, further showcasing its metabolic versatility. Studies have reported that Rhodococcus ruber can achieve up to an 8% biodegradation rate of polyethylene, although one contradictory description mistakenly referred to it as a fungus. Despite this, the overwhelming consensus is that Rhodococcus ruber is indeed a bacterial species with significant potential in plastic degradation and environmental cleanup. Its capacity to form biofilms enhances its ability to adhere to plastic surfaces and improve biodegradation efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aad5e97885ea1116956513db244b8fb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4</data>
