    <node id="&quot;MICROBE-DEPENDENCE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microbe-Dependence refers to the reliance on microbial activity for the successful degradation of polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbe-dependence indicates the reliance on microbial action for the degradation of certain materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbe-dependence refers to processes or phenomena that rely on microbial activity, such as the degradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbe-dependence describes the reliance on gut microbes for the degradation of certain plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbe-dependence refers to the reliance of certain biological processes, such as polymer degradation, on gut microbes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROBE-INDEPENDENCE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microbe-Independence describes processes where biodegradation occurs without significant microbial involvement."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbe-independence describes processes or phenomena that occur without the need for microbial involvement, contrasting with microbe-dependence."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbe-independence suggests the ability of organisms to degrade materials without significant microbial involvement."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbe-independence indicates the ability to degrade plastics without relying on gut microbes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENZYME&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Enzymes are biological catalysts secreted by T. molitor larvae and their gut microbes to facilitate the depolymerization of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes are biological catalysts that facilitate reactions such as the breakdown of polymers during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes produced by the gut microbiota of mealworms facilitate the chemical breakdown of polystyrene into smaller molecules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes are potential biocatalysts derived from gut microbiota for the degradation of plastic waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes are biological catalysts that facilitate the degradation of complex substrates like plant biomass and synthetic polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes produced by organisms like Galleria mellonella play a key role in breaking down complex polymers such as polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes secreted by Tenebrio molitor play a key role in the biodegradation of PET independent of gut microbes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes are biological catalysts that accelerate chemical reactions, including those involved in the breakdown of plastics by mealworm larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c31fc195067eeb8acb7f98417bd6744</data>
    </node>
