    <node id="&quot;ENTEROBACTER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Enterobacter is a bacterial genus associated with the PP diet in superworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria associated with polypropylene degradation in the gut of superworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of Gram-negative bacteria, some species of which are involved in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a bacterial genus within the γ-proteobacteria class, known for its styrene-degrading abilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a bacterial genus found in the gut microbiome of mealworms. Its presence suggests variations in bacterial communities among samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria found predominantly in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria, with species like Enterobacter asburiae YT1 contributing to polyethylene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus identified as predominant in larval gut microbiota across all diet groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria identified in the posterior gut of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria isolated from the gut of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria found in the core gut microbiome of greater wax moths."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of gram-negative bacteria, demonstrating differential responses to PP and WB diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a bacterial genus known for its involvement in various biological processes such as nitrate reduction and iron cycling."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus containing species capable of anaerobic styrene biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria capable of efficiently carrying out bioreactions under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of gram-negative bacteria involved in various biological functions, including nitrate reduction and probiotic effects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria contributing to nitrogen fixation in certain insect species."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069</data>
    </node>
