    <node id="&quot;TRICHODERMA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Trichoderma is a fungal genus associated with the breakdown of plastics, identified in the gut microbiota of polystyrene-fed larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trichoderma is a fungal genus considered as a potential candidate for plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trichoderma is a fungal genus involved in PS plastic degradation in mealworm guts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trichoderma is a fungal genus overrepresented in PS samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trichoderma is a fungal genus that appeared overrepresented in PS samples with a Log2-fold change of 11.8, but was recovered rarely in resamples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trichoderma is a fungal genus with species like Trichoderma viride involved in LDPE degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WHEAT BRAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Wheat bran is a normal feedstock for mealworms, providing essential nutrients."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat bran is a normal food source for mealworms, providing essential nutrients and trace elements required for growth and enzyme production."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat bran is a nutrient used to feed mealworms during breeding."&lt;SEP&gt;Wheat bran, the hard outer layer of wheat grain and a by-product of wheat milling, serves as a critical dietary component for Tenebrio molitor (yellow mealworms) and Zophobas atratus (superworms) larvae in both conventional rearing practices and experimental setups. It acts as a rich source of dietary fiber and nutrients, supporting larval growth, development, and overall survival. Wheat bran is widely recognized as a standard culture substrate for raising yellow mealworms under controlled conditions, ensuring consistent feeding environments. It functions as a control diet in experiments, providing a baseline for comparison with alternative diets, such as those containing plastics or microplastics.

In laboratory settings, wheat bran not only supports optimal growth and development in mealworm larvae but also results in the highest total fatty acid content while generally maintaining low larval mortality rates and faster development cycles. However, moderate mortality rates have been observed in some cases. Wheat bran enhances the resistance of mealworms to microplastic toxicity and increases the degradation rate of plastics, making it an essential element in studies focused on biodegradation processes. When mixed with polymers like polystyrene (PS), wheat bran creates a balanced feedstock that promotes PS consumption rates while facilitating plastic degradation.

Additionally, wheat bran has been used alongside polypropylene foam to enhance consumption rates in Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus larvae during experimental preparation phases. Despite occasional complications in analytical interpretations, such as thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), wheat bran remains a fundamental food source for T. molitor larvae before, during, and after experiments. Its presence in the diet alleviates toxicity caused by microplastics, extending its role beyond mere sustenance to aid in the degradation of plastics and support overall larval health and development. Although alternatives like rolled barley have occasionally been used due to differences in nutritional availability, wheat bran continues to be a key dietary supplement for yellow mealworms, serving as a co-dietary component in experiments aimed at enhancing PS degradation capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat bran is a nutritional component reviewed for its composition and nutritional quality."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf28f07f3e46b29e447aae784667c29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a706f2bd66291a3ef925114ffd9300&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba22196a59b67cbf67af7d76a039af1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d01d291012bd548fc067e250ec39fba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a170da66288c73388872b8a43fa51e33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ATR-FTIR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"INSTRUMENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"ATR-FTIR (Attenuated Total Reflectance Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) is a method for analyzing chemical changes in plastics after digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"ATR-FTIR (Attenuated Total Reflectance Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) is a technique used to analyze the chemical composition of surgical mask layers and their residues after digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"ATR-FTIR is a spectroscopic technique used to analyze chemical bonds and confirm oxidation and degradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"ATR-FTIR is employed to characterize the depolymerization and degradation of residual polymers in larval frass."&lt;SEP&gt;ATR-FTIR (Attenuated Total Reflectance Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) is a versatile spectroscopic technique used to analyze the chemical composition, structure, and functional groups of various materials, particularly plastics. This method plays a critical role in studying degradation processes, including those occurring in low-density polyethylene (LDPE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), and nanoplastics.

ATR-FTIR is employed to examine changes in chemical bonds and functional groups on the surface of plastics such as PE mulching films and PS films, both before and after degradation or microbial treatment. It can also confirm alterations in the chemical composition of nanoplastics following degradation or exposure to bacteria. Furthermore, ATR-FTIR is instrumental in analyzing the degradation of plastics within environments like worm guts and after ingestion by larvae, providing insights into the biodegradation process.

Additionally, this technique is used to characterize major functional groups in frass samples (the waste material produced by insects feeding on plastics) by comparing them to the original plastics, thereby offering valuable information about the transformation that occurs during biodegradation. Overall, ATR-FTIR serves as an essential analytical tool for understanding the complex chemical changes associated with plastic degradation and transformation in various environmental and biological contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;"ATR-FTIR (Attenuated Total Reflectance Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) is used to analyze the functional group changes in plastics after consumption by yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"ATR-FTIR is a method used to analyze the biodegradation of foam plastics after passing through the larval guts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Attenuated Total Reflection Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (ATR-FTIR) is used to evaluate chemical modifications in ingested polymers after digestion by larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ITS GENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"ITS gene is targeted in Illumina MiSeq sequencing to analyze gut fungal communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"The ITS (Internal Transcribed Spacer) gene is used for fungal identification and classification in the gut microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"The ITS gene is sequenced to study fungal communities in larval gut microbiomes."&lt;SEP&gt;"The ITS gene is used to analyze fungal diversity in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"The ITS gene is targeted in PCR amplification to identify fungal species in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"The ITS gene is used for fungal identification in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms."</data>
