    <node id="&quot;PS (POLYSTYRENE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PS is a synthetic polymer used as a substrate in experiments to study larval feeding behavior and biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene (PS) is a thermoplastic polymer widely utilized in various applications, including the preparation of emulsions and processed through methods such as extrusion, injection molding, and press molding. In scientific studies, PS serves as a critical substrate for biodegradation experiments, often involving mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae) and other organisms like *Zophobas atratus*. 

PS acts as both a sole carbon source for bacterial cultures and a feedstock material in experimental setups. When ingested by mealworms, it undergoes significant biodegradation processes within their gut environments. These processes involve depolymerization, molecular weight reduction, and the formation of new oxidized functional groups, including monomers and oligomers. Evidence of ring cleavage has also been observed during these degradation events.

The biodegradation of PS is facilitated by specific microbial communities present in the gut microbiome of insects, such as unclassified Enterobacteriaceae, Hafnia-Obsumbacter sp., and other bacteria capable of degrading polystyrene under aerobic and anaerobic conditions. This degradation leads to changes in molecular weight, structural modifications, and even mineralization of the plastic material. Additionally, the process affects larval survival rates, growth patterns, and overall metabolic responses, with variations noted when comparing PS to other plastics like polyethylene (PE).

Polystyrene's degradation kinetics have been extensively studied in relation to larval development, highlighting its potential as an environmentally sustainable solution for plastic waste management. Overall, PS represents a key subject in research focused on understanding microbial-mediated plastic degradation and its implications for ecosystem health.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PE (POLYETHYLENE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of plastic that is chewed and potentially degraded by certain insect larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE is another type of plastic used as a feedstock, causing more adverse effects on larval survival and growth compared to PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE is a type of plastic that can be degraded by certain bacteria, such as Klebsiella pneumoniae Mk-1."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE is a type of plastic that can be degraded by yellow mealworms. The molecular weight of PE decreases when ingested by these organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE is the most common plastic globally and a focus of biodegradation studies involving insect larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene is a type of plastic whose degradation involves specific gut microbial communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE is a type of plastic that shows limited extent depolymerization when consumed by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE is another type of plastic tested in the experiment, showing ingestion and degradation by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE (Polyethylene) is a polymer studied for its degradation by waxworms and used as a dietary component in experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE is a common plastic polymer being studied for its biodegradability by insect larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE is a polymer that undergoes biodegradation and chemical modification during the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE, or Polyethylene, is a type of plastic that undergoes biodegradation in this context, with its consumption and residual amounts measured."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE is another type of plastic foam ingested by mealworms, which undergoes digestion and biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE is a type of polymer tested for its biodegradation process when ingested by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-109486cd939a227e73354d0478c59ecf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181</data>
    </node>
