    <node id="&quot;SUPERWORM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Common name for Zatr larvae, involved in PS metabolism studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm (Zophobas aratus) is another insect species that ingests and degrades plastics, showing distinct gut microbiome changes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm (Zophobas atratus) is a species of larva capable of consuming and degrading plastic polymers such as PE and PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm refers to the larvae of Zophobas atratus, which consume plastics at higher rates compared to yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms are another insect species studied for their reaction to unconventional diets, showing less impact compared to mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm refers to a type of larva used in experiments for consuming PS and producing frass, which affects plant growth."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms (Zophobas morio) are insect larvae with a higher rate of polystyrene consumption compared to mealworms, also producing nutrient-rich frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus Fab., a larger species of beetle larva, exhibits greater capacity for plastic degradation and ingestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm refers to Zophobas atratus, an insect whose intestinal microbiota includes PS-degrading bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm is a species of larvae that shows the highest PS consumption rate and survival rate among the tested species. It also exhibits significant weight changes due to PS feeding."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm is a species of larvae whose gut microbiome changes upon feeding on polystyrene, showing decreased community diversity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm is a species whose intestinal microorganisms exhibit high potential for degrading polystyrene (PS) into low-molecular-weight substances."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm is the larval form of Zophobas atratus, exhibiting the highest capacity for degrading polystyrene among the studied species."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm (Zophobas atratus) larvae show distinct preferences and efficiencies in ingesting plastics, associated with gut microbiome changes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms (Zophobas atratus Fab.) exhibit preferences and efficiencies in ingesting different types of plastics, associated with changes in gut microbiome composition."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;YELLOW MEALWORM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor Linn.) show distinct plastic ingestion preferences and efficiencies linked to gut microbiome alterations."&lt;SEP&gt;The **Yellow Mealworm**, which is the larval form of *Tenebrio molitor* (a species of beetle), plays a significant role in the biodegradation of various types of plastics. These larvae are commonly studied for their ability to consume and degrade polymers such as polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), and even polyvinyl chloride (PVC). Their gut microbiome is highly efficient, containing bacteria capable of breaking down plastic compounds, including disrupting benzene ring structures in certain polymers.

Research indicates that while yellow mealworms can degrade plastics like PS, their consumption rates vary depending on the type of plastic and dietary conditions. Compared to other larvae, such as superworms, yellow mealworms may exhibit lower plastic consumption rates but still demonstrate notable capabilities in depolymerizing plastics. Studies have shown that they convert a portion of ingested plastic into carbon dioxide (CO₂) and alter the molecular weight of residual polymer chains, contributing significantly to environmental science efforts aimed at reducing plastic waste.

However, feeding exclusively on plastics like polystyrene negatively impacts the larvae's health, leading to decreased survival rates, significant weight loss, and reduced community diversity within their gut microbiomes. The microbial communities residing in the yellow mealworm's digestive system adapt dynamically to different dietary inputs, responding to shifts between natural food sources and plastic-based diets. This adaptability highlights the importance of understanding how artificial ecosystems influence these microbial populations.

In summary, the **Yellow Mealworm (*Tenebrio molitor*)** is an insect species whose larval stage exhibits remarkable potential for biodegrading plastics through its specialized gut microbiome. Despite challenges posed by plastic-only diets, this organism remains a key subject in studies exploring sustainable solutions for plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa27760a0a889fe7b58360dbb150cdf3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aac53ba8b358857c4dcd3b6cd9e8991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ATR-FTIR ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"ATR-FTIR analysis involves using infrared spectroscopy to identify chemical bonds and functional groups in materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"ATR-FTIR analysis is a spectroscopic technique used to study chemical changes in PE during degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"ATR-FTIR analysis provides evidence for oxidation and de-polymerization of plastics by the larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"ATR-FTIR analysis is a spectroscopic technique used to study the chemical structure of nanoplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"ATR-FTIR analysis is used to identify changes in functional groups within PS and PU foam plastics after ingestion by larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OXIDATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Oxidation involves the loss of electrons during a reaction, often associated with the degradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidation involves the modification of functional groups in microplastics, observed in both MLS and OLS mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidation involves the modification of functional groups, observed in frass excreted by old mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidation is a chemical process where oxygen is added to the polymer chain, leading to its degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidation refers to the chemical process where plastics are broken down in the larval guts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidation is a chemical process occurring in the guts of larvae that contributes to the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidation refers to the addition of oxygen functional groups into polymer chains, which is a key step in plastic degradation observed in the guts of both species."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidation refers to the biochemical process where enzymes secreted by bacteria like WJ2020 and WJ2025 promote oxygen immobilization on the PS film surface."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidation refers to the chemical modification of plastics through the addition of oxygen, observed during the biodegradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidation is a chemical process where substances react with oxygen, contributing to the alteration of plastic properties."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidation refers to the incorporation of oxygen into the polymer chain, a key step in the degradation of polystyrene by larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidation refers to the chemical process occurring in plastics during their degradation by yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidation refers to the chemical process where polypropylene undergoes molecular changes, contributing to its degradation within the larvae’s digestive system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidation is a chemical process that modifies polymer surfaces, facilitating microbial colonization and degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Degradation indicates the breakdown of plastics into simpler compounds by the larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Degradation is the process by which insects break down Styrofoam, potentially making it safe for consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;"Degradation is the process by which complex substances like polyurethane are broken down into simpler compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Degradation refers to the breakdown of complex substances into simpler ones through enzymatic or microbial action."&lt;SEP&gt;"Degradation refers to the process by which specific bacteria break down plastics like PP, PU, and EVA in the guts of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Degradation refers to the biological process by which the bacterium breaks down poly(ethylene terephthalate)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Degradation refers to the breakdown of polystyrene by bacterial strains as part of their metabolic processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67425e341a5b2c8102281581fa148554&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d7f6907ee7ce47b00537b1458fcc029</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WEIGHT CHANGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Weight change in mealworms reflects their adaptation to plastic as a food source, with fluctuations indicating potential nutritional implications."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight change reflects the growth and development of larvae over the 45-day experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight change reflects the physiological response of larvae to various diets over the test period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight change measures alterations in the mass of PS films after exposure to bacteria, indicating biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LIFE STAGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Different stages of T. molitor lifecycle including larvae, pupae, and beetles."&lt;SEP&gt;"Life stage refers to the developmental phases of insects being studied for their ability to digest Styrofoam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Life stage refers to the developmental phases of larvae, which were adversely affected by sole plastic diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Life stages describe developmental phases of organisms. In this context, it refers to the larval stage of Tenebrio molitor, which shows reduced growth and survival when fed solely on polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Life stage describes the developmental phases of T. molitor larvae, including pupation and adult transformation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ADVERSE EFFECTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"HEALTH MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Adverse effects refer to harmful outcomes caused by exposure to certain substances, such as microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Adverse effects refer to harmful outcomes experienced by organisms exposed to pollutants such as microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Adverse effects refer to the negative impacts on larval growth, pupation, and overall life cycle due to plastic consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;"Negative impacts on larval weight change and survival, particularly observed in EVA-fed groups."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AMENDMENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Amendment involves adding nutrients like bran to improve sustained plastic consumption and mitigate adverse effects."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ATR-FTIR SPECTRA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"ATR-FTIR spectra provide evidence of chemical changes in plastics after being consumed by larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C=O STRETCH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C=O stretch indicates the presence of carbonyl groups formed during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"C=O stretch refers to the chemical bond vibration observed in ATR-FTIR analysis, indicating oxidation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"C=O stretch represents the presence of carbonyl groups detected in degraded plastics, indicating the addition of oxygen functional groups during the degradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"C=O stretch refers to the incorporation of oxygen into polymer chains, confirming oxidation during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"C=O stretch refers to the carbonyl bond that appears as a new peak in FTIR spectra during the biodegradation of LDPE."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CH2 STRETCH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"CH2 stretch is a functional group whose disappearance indicates depolymerization of hydrocarbons in plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"CH2 stretch refers to the chemical bond vibration in polyethylene, whose intensity decreases during degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
