    <node id="&quot;CARBOHYDRATE METABOLISM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carbohydrate metabolism refers to the processes by which organisms break down carbohydrates into energy, associated with certain gut fungi like Hyphopichia and Debaryomyces."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbohydrate metabolism refers to the processes involved in breaking down carbohydrates, which are also impacted by gut microbes during PET degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CO-CULTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A co-culture consists of both mealworms and beetles sharing the same habitat, potentially affecting EPS consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;"An experimental setup involving 50 mealworms and 50 beetles, used to study EPS consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;"An experimental setup where both mealworms and beetles are present in the same habitat."&lt;SEP&gt;"Co-culture involves studying the interaction between mealworms and beetles to understand potential synergistic effects on EPS consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;"Co-culture refers to an experimental setup where both mealworms and beetles coexist and interact within the same habitat."&lt;SEP&gt;"Co-culture refers to the cultivation of multiple microbial species together, potentially enhancing plastic degradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Co-culture refers to the combined action of two bacterial strains (Acinetobacter sp. strain NyX450 and Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451) for effective polyethylene degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09d03c0a2dce3f57876d209a83a2e82a</data>
    </node>
