      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dark Fermentation is a process involving anaerobic breakdown of organic materials, typically associated with hydrogen production."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MARINE ENZYMES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Marine Enzymes are enzymes derived from marine sources like algae, bacteria, fungi, and sponges, having various biotechnological applications."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ZOPHOBAS ARATUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Zophobas aratus larvae are insects known for their capability to degrade foam plastics, linked with changes in gut enzyme activity and microbiome composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas aratus, also known as superworms, possess the ability to degrade plastics such as low-density polyethylene and polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas aratus, commonly known as the superworm, shows preferences for ingesting different plastics and contributes to biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas aratus larvae contribute to the biodegradation of foam plastics, affecting gut enzyme activities and microbiome changes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas aratus, also known as superworms, are larvae capable of degrading low-density polyethylene and polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas aratus, or superworms, play a role in polypropylene degradation through gut microbial activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas aratus, also known as superworms, are larvae capable of degrading foam plastics and show distinct gut microbiome changes upon ingestion of different plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas aratus, commonly known as superworms, are insects with larvae capable of degrading polypropylene through gut microbe-dependent mechanisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas aratus, also known as superworms, play a role in the degradation of polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENZYMATIC HYDROLYSATES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Enzymatic Hydrolysates refer to the breakdown products of enzymatic reactions, often used in biotechnological applications."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacteria are discussed in terms of their adhesion capacity, flagellar function, and chemotactic responses that enhance their ability to seek carbon sources under starvation conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria are microorganisms present in the gut microbiota of larvae, showing variations based on diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria are single-celled microorganisms that play significant roles in processes like biodegradation and enzymatic activities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria include species like Brevibacillus borstelensis that produce enzymes capable of breaking down plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria present in plastic-eating worms produce enzymes capable of degrading plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Refers to various types of bacteria involved in processes such as hydrocarbon degradation, biodegradation of contaminants, and enzymatic activities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria are microorganisms isolated from mealworm larvae guts and cultured on PCA and NA media."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbes responsible for various biological processes, including the degradation of biodegradable plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria are essential microorganisms involved in the breakdown of various substances, including plastics and natural polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria isolated from the gut of waxworms can also contribute to the degradation of LDPE, albeit at much slower rates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria isolated from the gut of mealworms play a key role in the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria present in the gut of T. molitor are potentially responsible for the biodegradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria in the mealworm gut contribute to the biodegradation of EPS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria capable of growing on NPS emulsion as a sole carbon source were used to evaluate enzyme activities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria present in the guts of mealworms aid in the degradation of plastics, particularly polystyrene, through biological processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbes forming clear zones on FMM agar plates identified via 16 S rRNA gene sequencing."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria are single-celled microorganisms that participate in various biological processes, including biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-050a4c71685bf71165985a79f528ed58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d57e056cd3b06b37d22117fc75e3a4df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
