      <data key="d2">chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f4c282fe2323dc6e310501af888c85</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic-Eating Waxworms harbor bacterial strains capable of degrading polyethylene within their gut microbiota."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic-eating waxworms are larvae of moths that have gut microorganisms capable of degrading plastics like polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic-eating waxworms harbor bacterial strains in their guts capable of degrading polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic-eating waxworms refer to caterpillars capable of consuming and breaking down plastics, harboring gut bacteria involved in this process."&lt;SEP&gt;"The bacterial strains from the guts of plastic-eating waxworms degrade polyethylene polymers effectively."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms possess gut bacteria capable of degrading polystyrene, providing insights into biodegradation mechanisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic-eating waxworms harbor gut bacteria that contribute to the biodegradation of polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic-eating waxworms are a specific type of waxworm whose gut microbiota has been shown to biodegrade polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are the larvae of wax moths, some of which have been observed to consume and biodegrade plastic materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic-eating waxworms harbor bacterial strains capable of polyethylene biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic-eating waxworms harbor gut bacteria responsible for the biodegradation of plastics such as polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic-eating waxworms are larvae of moths that exhibit the ability to biodegrade plastics like polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic-eating waxworms, specifically the larvae of Tenebrio molitor, have demonstrated the ability to biodegrade polyethylene."</data>
      <data key="d0">"INSECT"</data>
    </node>
