    <node id="&quot;HIGH-IMPACT POLYSTYRENE (HIPS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"High-impact polystyrene is a type of polystyrene with added rubber for increased toughness."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-impact polystyrene is used for liquid containers and toys due to its durability."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A lightweight product provided by Woosung Resin Co., Ltd., consisting of 93–96% EPS, 4–7% pentane, and less than 1% hexabromocyclododecane."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is a type of polystyrene foam that was tested in the experiment, showing significant larval weight gain when consumed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded Polystyrene is a type of polystyrene feed used in the experiment, affecting larval weight significantly."&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is a lightweight foam form of polystyrene, commonly referred to as Styrofoam. It is characterized by its lower density and compressive strength compared to other forms of polystyrene, such as XPS. EPS is widely used in packaging, building insulation, and various other applications due to its durability and insulating properties. However, it is also known for being resistant to natural degradation, contributing significantly to plastic waste accumulation, including environmental contamination with microplastics and potential environmental hormones.

Despite its durability, EPS can be degraded by certain organisms and processes. Notably, it has been observed that Tenebrio molitor larvae (mealworms) and Tribolium castaneum can consume and biodegrade EPS, leading to significant mass loss during experiments. This degradation capability makes EPS more bioavailable for these organisms compared to other forms of polystyrene. Additionally, certain microorganisms have shown the ability to degrade EPS, further highlighting its potential for biological breakdown under specific conditions.

In scientific studies, EPS has been used as a dietary component for mealworms to assess its impact on their growth and health, as well as to evaluate its degradability. These studies indicate that EPS undergoes broad depolymerization when consumed by mealworms, suggesting a possible solution for managing EPS waste through biological means. Overall, while EPS contributes to environmental challenges due to its persistence, research into its biodegradability offers promising avenues for reducing its ecological footprint.&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded Polystyrene is a type of polystyrene used for packaging, insulation, and buoyancy, contributing to microplastic pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is a type of polystyrene used as insulation or packaging material, contributing significantly to environmental pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene is a commercial product containing less than 1% of polymers with MW &gt; 1000 kDa."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene is used in building insulation and packaging applications."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a32ea55d79e87933e26b4165db47b1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8b79a6f4c112b0b62ada6756140e3e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a170da66288c73388872b8a43fa51e33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503</data>
