    <node id="&quot;EPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"EPS (Expanded Polystyrene) is a plastic material degraded by some Enterobacteriaceae species."&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is a lightweight, rigid foam plastic material with an amorphous structure primarily produced from the styrene monomer. It often contains an effervescent gas, giving it its characteristic foamy texture. EPS exists in both ordinary white and grey forms, with the grey variant containing added graphite. This synthetic material is widely used for insulation in construction and packaging but has also become a significant component of construction waste and marine ecosystems.

In scientific studies, EPS has been extensively investigated for its biodegradability, particularly through interactions with biological systems such as mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae). These larvae can consume, chew, and digest EPS, leading to depolymerization and the production of smaller polymer chains and other byproducts. The consumption of EPS affects the development and health of the larvae, making it a subject of interest in evaluating the effects of plastics on biological systems. Research shows that EPS exhibits the highest mass loss among studied materials during degradation processes.

Additionally, EPS has been studied as a potential carbon source for certain bacterial strains involved in polystyrene degradation. However, it should be noted that while EPS refers to expanded polystyrene in this context, it must be distinguished from Extracellular Polysaccharides (also abbreviated as EPS), which serve a different role as a carbon source for bacterial strains degrading polystyrene. Despite being consumed by organisms like mealworms, EPS does not provide the necessary nutrients required for their optimal growth and survival.

Overall, EPS plays a crucial role in experiments exploring plastic biodegradation and its environmental implications, with consumption rates varying based on pre-treatments. Its low density and versatile applications make it a common material in insulation, packaging, and various other industries.&lt;SEP&gt;"ePS refers to emulsified polystyrene, a substrate used for screening anaerobic PS-degrading bacteria in this study."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS is a metabolite used as the sole energy source for mealworms after seven days of feeding with wheat bran."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is a form of polystyrene fed to mealworms, showing degradation after ingestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is a material fed to mealworms to enrich polystyrene-degrading microorganisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09d03c0a2dce3f57876d209a83a2e82a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACHROIA GRISELLA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A. grisella is a species of lesser waxworm identified through molecular taxonomy, capable of degrading polyethylene (PE) by chewing and breaking it down."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia Grisella, the lesser waxworm, efficiently biodegrades high-density polyethylene (HDPE)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella is one of the insects capable of breaking down plastics like PS, PE, PVC, and PP."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella, or lesser waxworms, efficiently biodegrades polyethylene (HDPE) waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella, known as the lesser waxworm, is efficient in biodegrading polyethylene (HDPE) waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella, also known as the lesser waxworm, has been shown to efficiently biodegrade polyethylene (HDPE) waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella, the lesser waxworm, is a species of moth larvae involved in plastic biodegradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella, commonly known as the lesser waxmoth, demonstrates efficient biodegradation capabilities for polyethylene (HDPE) waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella is another moth species involved in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella larvae contribute to the biodegradation of PE plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella, commonly known as the lesser waxworm, is a species of moth whose larvae exhibit the ability to biodegrade polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella, or the lesser waxworm, is an insect whose larvae efficiently degrade polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella, or the lesser waxmoth, demonstrates efficient biodegradation of high-density polyethylene (HDPE) waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella, or the lesser waxworm, demonstrates efficient biodegradation capabilities for high-density polyethylene (HDPE) waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella, or lesser waxworm, efficiently degrades polyethylene (HDPE) waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella is a species of moth larvae with plastic-degrading capabilities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-47ee82f655853704e6653d8146973278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af5eb6a8326b4977801e2f7694a2c750&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RNA SEQUENCING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"RNA sequencing is a powerful technique used for whole-transcriptome analysis to study gene expression changes related to plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"RNA sequencing is a process used to analyze gene expression changes in mealworms."</data>
