    <node id="&quot;FTIR SPECTRA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"FTIR (Fourier Transform Infrared) spectra are used to analyze chemical bonds and structures in materials, aiding in understanding degradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra provide chemical information about MPS feedstock and frass, highlighting differences in methylene rocking vibrations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy data, used to identify chemical bonds and groups in polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra were used to analyze the chemical composition of PU1 and PU2, revealing differences in additives and structures."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra provide insights into changes in polymer surface chemistry during plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra provide information about the chemical bonds and functional groups present in the decomposition products."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra provide information about the chemical structure of samples treated with enzymes."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra is a process used to identify functional groups in residual polymers extracted from frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra are used to analyze residual PE polymers in the frass of larvae fed on different types of polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra analyze chemical bonds and functional groups, providing evidence of PS degradation through changes in bond intensities."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR Spectra are used to analyze oxidation and depolymerization processes in the gut of larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR Spectra refers to the measurement of vibrational modes of molecules in a sample, providing information about functional groups and chemical bonds."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra (Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy) identifies chemical bonds and functional groups in PP foam and frass samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra analysis helps identify functional groups and chemical bonds in biodegraded plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra provide evidence of oxidation and depolymerization of LDPE foams through chemical bond analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier Transform Infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy is employed to characterize chemical changes in residual plastics after biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBONYL GROUPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carbonyl groups (C═O) appear in FTIR spectra as evidence of biodegradation, indicating structural changes in PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups (C═O) are detected in FTIR analysis, indicating the presence of oxidized functional groups formed during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chemical functional groups identified in FTIR spectra, indicating oxidation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups are chemical functional groups that may contribute to the absorbance in FTIR spectra, indicating potential biochemical oxidation of polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups are chemical structures formed during the degradation of polyethylene, indicating changes in its molecular structure."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups are another type of oxygen-containing functional group detected during the biodegradation of PE polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups are newly formed in the egested frass of A. grisea larvae, confirming the biodegradation of polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chemical groups detected in modified PS residues, indicating oxygen incorporation during degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups are functional groups observed in the degraded polystyrene samples, indicating the occurrence of oxidation and depolymerization."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROXYL GROUPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chemical functional groups identified in FTIR spectra, indicating hydroxylation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups are chemical structures formed during oxidation of microplastics, indicated by broadened peaks in FTIR spectra."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups (-OH) are identified through FTIR analysis, suggesting hydrophilic surface properties in the frass samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups are indicated by broadened peaks in FTIR spectra, suggesting hydrogen bonding and carboxylic acid formation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The broadening of peaks at 2500-3500 cm\({}^{-1}\) is associated with hydrogen bonds of hydroxyl groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups (−OH) are detected in chemical shift regions, indicating evidence of PET molecular transformations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups are chemical functional groups formed on the surface of polystyrene during degradation, decreasing its hydrophobicity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chemical groups identified in PS residues, reflecting structural changes due to biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups are detected in the frass samples, suggesting a change in the hydrophobic properties of the polymer surface."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups appear in the FTIR spectra of frass samples, indicating changes in surface properties."</data>
