    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MW&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">Molecular weight (MW), specifically the weight-average molecular weight (Mw), is a critical parameter used to characterize the size and distribution of polymer chains in various materials such as polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), polypropylene, polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET). Mw measures the weighted average molecular size within a polymer sample, reflecting larger values compared to the number-average molecular weight (Mn) due to skewed distributions. This parameter plays a key role in influencing the biodegradability, enzymatic attack availability, and degradation rates of polymers.

In studies involving the biodegradation of polymers, Mw provides insights into the extent of degradation by indicating changes in polymer size during the process. For instance, the weight-average molecular weight of PVC significantly decreased after consumption by mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), and similar observations were noted for PE mulching films and surgical mask layers. Additionally, Mw has been employed to evaluate the degradation of polymer residues from antibiotic-treated larvae frass. While MW was found to be a key factor affecting the rate and extent of polymer biodegradation, one study noted that molecular weight did not show significant correlations with plastic consumption. Despite this, Mw remains an essential measure in understanding the central part of the molecular weight distribution curve and the mass distribution of polymer chains.

Mw also influences microbial community composition and serves as a crucial metric in polymer science and biodegradation studies, offering valuable information about the molecular characteristics of polymers and their behavior during degradation processes. In experiments involving "MW larvae," supplementation had notable effects on their survival and weight, though these findings pertain to biological aspects rather than polymer properties. Furthermore, Mw characterizes the molecular weight distribution of ingested microplastics and represents the weight-average molecular weight of polystyrene, which influences the specific chain pull-out rate (SCPR) and degradation process. Overall, Mw is a key parameter used in evaluating polymer degradation, particularly in plastics like PS and PE, and is widely utilized in gel permeation chromatography (GPC) analysis to assess changes in plastics during biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542</data>
    </node>
