      <data key="d1">"A type of white blood cell involved in immune response, measured in the experiment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BASOPHILS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"IMMUNE RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of white blood cell involved in immune response, measured in the experiment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RED BLOOD CELLS (RBC)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Red blood cells are responsible for oxygen transport and were measured in the experiment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HEMOGLOBIN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen, measured in the experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hemoglobin is a protein in red blood cells responsible for oxygen transport and measured here in grams per deciliter."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HEMATOCRIT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hematocrit measures the proportion of blood volume occupied by red blood cells, expressed as a percentage."&lt;SEP&gt;"The proportion of blood volume occupied by red blood cells, measured in the experiment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MCV&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MCV (mean corpuscular volume) measures the average size of red blood cells in femtoliters."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MCH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MCH (mean corpuscular hemoglobin) measures the average amount of hemoglobin per red blood cell in picograms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MCHC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MCHC (mean cell hemoglobin concentration) measures the average concentration of hemoglobin in red blood cells in grams per deciliter."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLATELET&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Platelets are small blood cells involved in clotting, measured here in thousands per microliter."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RDW&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"RDW (red cell distribution width) measures variability in red blood cell size, expressed as a percentage."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MPV&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MPV (mean platelet volume) measures the average size of platelets in percentage units."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"TP (total protein) measures the total amount of proteins in blood serum, expressed in grams per deciliter."&lt;SEP&gt;"Total protein (TP) measures the combined level of albumin and globulin in the blood, reflecting nutritional status and liver function."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ALBUMIN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROTEIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Albumin is a major protein in blood plasma, responsible for maintaining oncotic pressure and transporting substances."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GLUCOSE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Glucose is a carbohydrate metabolized by Mixta tenebrionis to produce acids."&lt;SEP&gt;"Glucose is a simple sugar produced during the degradation of cellulose and hemicellulose."&lt;SEP&gt;"Glucose is a simple sugar produced from mannose during catabolism and serves as an essential energy source."&lt;SEP&gt;"Glucose is a simple sugar used as a positive control in experiments to test microbial activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Glucose is a sugar that serves as the primary energy source for cells, measured here in milligrams per deciliter."&lt;SEP&gt;"Glucose is used as a positive control in experiments to test microbial respiration activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Glucose is present in the hemolymph of insects and is vital for maintaining organ function. It can also be produced from proteins during gluconeogenesis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-146789a250a0dbaae0d8438de692e191&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BUN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"BUN (blood urea nitrogen) measures the amount of urea nitrogen in blood, indicating kidney function."&lt;SEP&gt;"Blood urea nitrogen (BUN) measures the amount of urea nitrogen in the blood, indicating kidney and liver function."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bf6d17dfbab29bcb302ae97679bc903</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CREA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Crea (creatinine) measures waste products filtered by the kidneys, reflecting renal function."&lt;SEP&gt;"Creatinine is a waste product of muscle metabolism, used as a marker for kidney function."</data>
