    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE MICROPLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6464a2f3ca18442f70e254b9341a3e1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
      <data key="d1">"Parental exposure to polystyrene microplastics at environmentally relevant concentrations shows negligible transgenerational effects on zebrafish."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene microplastics are small particles of polystyrene, a common plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene microplastics combine with other chemicals like phthalates to exert toxic effects on aquatic organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tiny particles of polystyrene, a synthetic polymer, often found as environmental pollutants."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene Microplastics are small plastic particles that induce microbial dysbiosis and dysfunction in seawater."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene microplastics are small plastic particles causing gut toxicity in Eisenia fetida and affecting gut bacteria communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene microplastics exhibit reduced toxicity to earthworms when coated with a protective layer formed by soil particles."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene microplastics can be completely digested and degraded by greater wax moth larvae independently of gut microbiota."</data>
