    <node id="&quot;ZOPHOBAS MORIO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Zophobas morio, commonly known as superworms, are another genus of larvae capable of degrading expanded polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas morio, or the superworm, exhibits feeding and degradation effects on plastic materials."&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas morio, commonly known as superworms or supermeal worms, are insect larvae belonging to the Tenebrionidae family, which also includes Tenebrio molitor (mealworms). These insects have garnered significant scientific interest due to their unique capabilities and potential applications. 

Superworms are studied for their ability to consume and degrade various types of plastics, including polystyrene (commonly referred to as Styrofoam) and expanded polystyrene (EPS), although their efficiency in degrading polyurethane (PU) is noted to be lower compared to T. molitor. Their gut contains microorganisms that play a crucial role in the biodegradation of these materials, making them a subject of interest in ecological and environmental studies focused on plastic waste management.

In addition to their plastic-degrading abilities, Zophobas morio are also recognized for their nutritional value, being investigated as an edible insect source. They are evaluated not only for their nutrient content but also for how their diet, such as polystyrene, affects oxidative stress parameters. Furthermore, the larvae of Zophobas morio are a source of anticancer peptides, adding to their potential benefits in biomedical research.

Overall, Zophobas morio represents a promising species in both environmental science and nutrition, with its larvae demonstrating plastic-eating behavior and offering valuable contributions to human health and sustainability studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EXIGUOBACTERIUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Exiguobacterium is a bacterial genus capable of creating biofilms and degrading polystyrene in the gut of Tenebrio molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Exiguobacterium is a bacterial genus found in the digestive tract of Tenebrio molitor larvae, aiding in the breakdown of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Exiguobacterium is a genus of bacteria primarily responsible for the degradation of PS in T. molitor larvae as reported in previous studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ASH CONTENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ash content refers to the mineral content in mealworms, which increases when fed Styrofoam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ash content refers to the mineral content of an organism, which was found to increase in mealworms consuming Styrofoam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ash content was determined by drying samples in a muffle oven at 500°C."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ash content represents the mineral composition of the barley bran."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FAT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Fat is a key nutrient content measured in the larvae, reflecting their energy reserves."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat is a lipid component found in mealworms, with levels changing based on the availability of food sources."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat levels were observed to decrease in mealworms fed with Styrofoam compared to those on a conventional diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat percentage in insects was calculated by extraction using hexane in a Soxhlet system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat refers to lipids stored in organisms, which were reduced in mealworms fed with Styrofoam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat is a macronutrient present in significant amounts in yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat is another significant nutritional component of superworms, enhancing their utility as pet feed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fats are essential nutrients derived from the transformation of organic waste by yellow mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat is an essential macronutrient measured in the larvae's biomass to assess dietary effects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat is a lipid component present in barley bran at 4.5% concentration."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat refers to the lipid content measured in larvae and excrement, influenced by the type of feed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat is a nutrient that accumulates in the insect fat body and serves as a basic source of energy for insects."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a170da66288c73388872b8a43fa51e33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CYTOTOXICITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cytotoxicity is a biological response studied in the context of mixture effects between emerging organic contaminants."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cytotoxicity measures the potential toxic effects of substances on cells; no significant increase was observed in insect extracts from Styrofoam-fed insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cytotoxicity refers to the quality of being toxic to cells, measured in this study using various assays."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cytotoxicity refers to the quality of being toxic to cells, often measured in vitro using assays like MTT, NR, SRB, and LDH."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cytotoxicity refers to the quality of being toxic to cells, which can be observed in organisms consuming polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"The quality of being toxic to cells, studied in edible insects consuming polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cytotoxicity refers to the quality of being toxic to cells, often measured as part of assessing biological responses to substances."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENTOMOPHAGY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Entomophagy is the practice of eating insects, explored in the context of consumer acceptance of insects fed on polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Entomophagy refers to the practice of eating insects, which is gaining popularity due to its nutritional and environmental benefits."&lt;SEP&gt;"Entomophagy refers to the practice of eating insects, which is influenced by the type of feed consumed by the insects."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOACTIVE PEPTIDES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bioactive peptides are small proteins found in insects that exhibit various health-promoting properties."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a</data>
    </node>
