      <data key="d2">chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STARVATION PERIOD&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EXPERIMENTAL CONDITION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A starvation period refers to the 72-hour interval where larvae are deprived of food to prepare them for experimental diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Starvation period refers to a 48-hour duration where mealworms were not fed prior to the experimental diet introduction."&lt;SEP&gt;"Starvation period refers to the time when mealworms are deprived of food to prepare them for experimental diets."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;YLS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"YLS represents the young larval stage of mealworms, during which they consume less microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"YLS represents the youngest larval stage of mealworms, showing specific mortality rates and growth patterns."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MLS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MLS refers to the middle larval stage of mealworms, exhibiting distinct survival and growth characteristics."&lt;SEP&gt;"MLS refers to the older larval stage of mealworms, where specific biological processes and responses occur."&lt;SEP&gt;"MLS represents the middle larval stage of mealworms, showing intermediate levels of microplastic consumption."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OLS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"OLS denotes the oldest larval stage of mealworms, demonstrating unique responses to diet and survival."&lt;SEP&gt;"OLS represents the old larval stage of mealworms, exhibiting the greatest microplastic consumption and degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBOXYL GROUPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carboxyl groups are functional groups consisting of a carbon atom double-bonded to an oxygen atom and single-bonded to a hydroxyl group."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carboxyl groups were too small to be noticed in the current study but have been observed in previous research."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C—N STRETCH VIBRATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C—N stretch vibration indicates the presence of nitrogen-containing functional groups formed during microplastic oxidation."&lt;SEP&gt;"C—N stretch vibration is a chemical property observed in the FTIR spectra, indicating modifications to the polymer structure."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;M_N&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"M_n represents the number-average molecular weight, used to assess polymer chain breakdown."&lt;SEP&gt;"M_n represents the number-average molecular weight, which decreased in fecula compared to control PS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;M_W&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"M_w is the average molecular weight closest to the center of the molecular distribution curve, used to evaluate depolymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"M_w represents the weight-average molecular weight, also showing a decrease in fecula compared to control PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"M_w represents the weight-average molecular weight, indicating changes in polymer size distribution."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;M_P&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"M_p reflects the peak molecular weight, providing insights into molecular weight distribution patterns."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DEATH RATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"HEALTH MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure calculated as 100 minus the survival rate, indicating larval mortality."&lt;SEP&gt;"Death rate measures the frequency of deaths among mealworms across different groups and stages."&lt;SEP&gt;"Death rate measures the percentage of larvae that die under specific experimental conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SURVIVING PERIOD&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Surviving period indicates the duration for which mealworms survive under various feeding conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FITTED DEATH RATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The fitted death rate quantifies the mortality trends among mealworms under different feeding conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LARVAL WEIGHT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Larval weight measures the mass of mealworm larvae, which is reduced due to microplastic ingestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Larval weight reflects the growth status of mealworms and varies with diet, age, and survival outcomes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METHYLENE ROCKING VIBRATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Methylene rocking vibration refers to a specific chemical property identified in FTIR analysis of feedstock and frass."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HALF BRAN DIET&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Half bran diet involves replacing half of the bran with MPS, assessing its effects on mealworm survival and growth."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PUPATION DISTRIBUTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Pupation distribution describes the timing and proportion of mealworms transitioning into pupae over time."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FOOD INTAKE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Food intake measures how much mealworms consume, decreasing significantly in older larvae or those failing to pupate."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GROWTH RATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Growth Rate measures the increase in bacterial population over time, reflecting the efficiency of nutrient utilization or environmental conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Growth rate indicates the speed at which mealworms increase in size, influenced by diet and life stage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Growth rate refers to the speed at which mealworms increase in size, which is affected by diet and microplastic exposure."&lt;SEP&gt;"Growth rate measures the increase in size or mass of mealworm larvae over time under different feeding conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROPLASTICS (MPS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MPs refer to small plastic particles (&lt;300 μm) derived from larger plastic waste through biotic and abiotic reactions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics (MPs) are small plastic particles that can cause physiological stress and toxicity in organisms upon ingestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are small plastic particles ingested by T. molitor larvae as part of the experimental diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are small plastic particles less than 5 mm in diameter, often resulting from the breakdown of larger plastic debris and posing environmental risks."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are small plastic particles that have toxic effects on organisms such as mealworms, leading to weight loss, mortality, and developmental delays."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are small plastic particles derived from the breakdown of larger plastics, including PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are small plastic particles that serve as substrates for studying the biodegradation capabilities of organisms like mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are pollutants derived from PP degradation, found in marine, soil, and landfill environments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are small plastic particles less than 5 mm in size that pose environmental and health risks due to their persistence and toxicity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics refer to small plastic particles used in the study, including PE film and PS foam, which were consumed by T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics (MPs) are small plastic particles that undergo fragmentation and biodegradation processes when ingested by certain organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are small plastic particles that result from the degradation of larger plastic items and serve as a substrate for biodegradation studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HORMONAL SYSTEM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL FUNCTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The hormonal system regulates developmental processes like pupation in mealworms, potentially disturbed by nutrient deficiencies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DYNAMIC ENERGY BUDGET MODEL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This model explains how energy is assimilated from food and accumulated during the larval stage, impacting growth and pupation."&lt;SEP&gt;"This model explains how energy is assimilated from food and utilized during larval development."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENERGY RESERVE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Energy reserve refers to the stored energy in mealworms, mobilized during development to support vital functions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FEEDING CONTROL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Feeding control refers to the standard diet used as a baseline for comparing the effects of experimental diets on mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ARENICOLA MARINA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Arenicola marina is an aquatic lugworm species studied for its response to microplastic exposure."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FOLSOMIA CANDIDA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Folsomia candida is a springtail species whose reproduction is impacted by microplastic exposure."</data>
