      <data key="d0">"HORMONE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Juvenile hormone influences insect growth and development, disturbed by inadequate nutrition."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;B VITAMINS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"B vitamins are essential nutrients provided by yeast, contributing to the growth and health of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"B vitamins, including thiamin, riboflavin, and pantothenic acid, play critical roles in mealworm metabolism."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;REPRODUCTION BUFFER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Reproduction buffer is a mechanism that accumulates resources until a threshold is reached, triggering pupation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reproduction buffer stores energy intended for reproduction; it influences pupation timing and egg production in later stages."&lt;SEP&gt;"The reproduction buffer accumulates in the larval phase and triggers pupation when it exceeds a critical value."&lt;SEP&gt;"The reproduction buffer represents stored energy that triggers pupation when it reaches a certain threshold."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ESSENTIAL NUTRIENTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Essential nutrients are required for proper development and growth of organisms, and their insufficiency can lead to developmental delays such as postponement of pupation in worms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Essential nutrients are required for the growth, development, and pupation of mealworms; deficiencies lead to reduced fertility and survival rates."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Worms naturally degrade plastics and produce frass that can support plant growth. They are more resistant to environmental factors than pure enzymes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Worms, specifically mealworms, are high-protein organisms used as feed for poultry and fish and play a role in the degradation of microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FOOD CHAIN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microplastics can transfer into the human body through the food chain, threatening both human and wildlife health."&lt;SEP&gt;"The food chain represents the transfer of energy and materials between organisms in an ecosystem."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHICKEN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chickens are animals that ingest microplastics-fed earthworms, contributing to the trophic transfer of microplastics."</data>
