    <node id="&quot;INSECT LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Insect larvae are capable of chewing and damaging various types of plastic films, including PE, PVC, and polyester."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insect larvae from families such as Tenebrionidae and Pyralidae have been identified for their ability to biodegrade various plastic polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insect larvae represent an early developmental stage in insects, such as mealworms, crucial for studying their ability to degrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insect larvae are the immature stage of insects that secrete emulsifiers to mediate the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insect larvae, such as those of Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus, play a crucial role in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insect larvae, particularly those of mealworms, have been found capable of biodegrading hydrocarbon-based plastics through their gut microbiota."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insect larvae, such as superworms and yellow mealworms, have been discovered to ingest and degrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insect larvae, including Tenebrio molitor, Galleria mellonella, and Zophobas atratus, are capable of consuming and degrading polystyrene through their gut microbiota."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insect larvae are the immature forms of insects, including those capable of degrading plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insect larvae, such as Zophobas atratus, are studied for their ability to biodegrade plastics into microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0</data>
    </node>
