      <data key="d2">chunk-4c5fc4d4324334254706d6d5f9312276</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PET&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PET (Polyethylene Terephthalate) is a hydrolyzable polymer that is more susceptible to biodegradation compared to non-hydrolyzable plastics like PE."&lt;SEP&gt;"PET is a type of plastic that shows a positive metabolic interaction with BSF larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) influenced metabolic pathways, particularly upregulating certain biochemical reactions involving nucleic acids and amino acids."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene terephthalate is a type of plastic that induced specific metabolic changes in BSF larvae, such as elevated pyrimidine metabolism."&lt;SEP&gt;PET, or polyethylene terephthalate, is a synthetic polymer widely used in packaging, fibers, and various commercial products. It consists of polymerized units of monohydroxyethyl terephthalate (MHET) and is commonly referred to as polyester in the textile industry. PET serves as both an energy source and diet component for Tenebrio molitor larvae (mealworms) in scientific studies, influencing their gut microbiome, metabolism, and survival.

Research indicates that PET undergoes biodegradation through enzymatic and microbial processes, facilitated by the larvae's gut microbes. This degradation involves molecular transformations such as depolymerization, oxidation, and production of intermediates like benzoic acid, protocatechuic acid, and catechol. Techniques such as gel permeation chromatography (GPC), Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), and proton nuclear magnetic resonance (¹H NMR) have been employed to analyze these changes.

The efficiency of PET degradation varies depending on its molecular weight (MW), with evidence showing measurable rates of consumption and removal by T. molitor larvae. Additionally, specific enzymes and gut microbes contribute to the breakdown of PET, highlighting potential applications for reducing plastic waste through biological processes. Overall, PET represents a significant focus in studies exploring microbial and enzymatic plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;"PET is another type of plastic that has been extensively studied for its biodegradation potential."&lt;SEP&gt;"PET (Polyethylene Terephthalate) is a polymer material relevant in studies of biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c5fc4d4324334254706d6d5f9312276&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
