    <node id="&quot;NANOPLASTICS (NPS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"NPs are smaller-sized plastic particles (&lt;1 μm) produced through the fragmentation of MPs in organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics (NPs) are extremely small plastic particles, potentially more toxic than microplastics but not detected in this study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are tiny plastic particles that may accumulate in biological systems but did not appear in the frass of PE-fed and PS-fed T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are ultra-small plastic particles smaller than 1 micron, formed through the degradation of plastics and contributing to environmental pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are even smaller plastic particles that can result from further degradation of microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are even smaller plastic particles, representing an emerging concern in environmental contamination studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are smaller pollutants resulting from PP degradation, also contributing to environmental contamination."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics (NPs) are extremely small plastic particles generated through the fragmentation of larger plastics by macroinvertebrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are extremely small plastic particles that pose challenges in environmental health studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </node>
