      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterium known for its ability to degrade PET in vitro, but with lower efficiency compared to T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"A bacterium known for its ability to degrade PET in vitro."&lt;SEP&gt;"A bacterium known for its high PET-degrading capacity, though it has a longer half-life for PET degradation compared to T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ideonella sakaiensis 201-F6 is a bacterium within the phylum Proteobacteria known for PET degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MOLECULAR WEIGHT (MW)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure used to assess the degradation of polystyrene by observing changes in residual PS in frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"MW refers to the mass of polymer chains, which influences their susceptibility to enzymatic attack and degradation rates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight parameters like Mn, Mw, and Mz provide insights into changes in polymer properties during degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight is a measure used to determine the size of polymer molecules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight is a measure of the mass of a molecule, impacting the biodegradation rate of polypropylene by Tenebrio molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight refers to the mass of a molecule, which is measured for PP polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight refers to the weight of polymer chains, which influences the biodegradability of polypropylene by the larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight is a key factor influencing the biodegradability of polyethylene, with lower molecular weights showing greater extents of depolymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight describes the size of polymer chains, impacting the extent of polystyrene biodegradation in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight is a measure used to characterize the size of polymer chains, with different averages such as weight-average, number-average, and size-average molecular weights."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;1H NMR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"1H NMR (Proton Nuclear Magnetic Resonance) is a spectroscopic technique used to study molecular structures and chemical shifts in biodegraded PP polymer residues."&lt;SEP&gt;"1H NMR (Proton Nuclear Magnetic Resonance) is a spectroscopic technique used to study the structure and dynamics of organic molecules."&lt;SEP&gt;"1H NMR (\(^1\)H nuclear magnetic resonance) is a method used to characterize the biodegradation of polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"1H NMR is a technique used to study molecular structures and confirm the presence of specific functional groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Proton nuclear magnetic resonance (1H NMR) provides qualitative evidence for PET degradation and oxidation alongside FTIR analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"1H NMR (proton nuclear magnetic resonance) is a spectroscopic technique used to analyze chemical structures and molecular weight distribution of PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"1H NMR (proton nuclear magnetic resonance) confirms oxidation and biodegradation of PP in both insect larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Proton nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy utilized for detailed molecular structure analysis of degraded LDPE."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C-H BONDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C-H bonds are chemical bonds in aromatic structures that are dampened or disappear after PET is processed by T. molitor larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C-O BONDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C-O bonds represent ether aliphatic and aromatic bonds in PET, which are altered during biodegradation by T. molitor larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C=O BONDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C=O bonds are carbonyl groups in PET that show changes after processing by T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"C=O bonds are chemical bonds formed during the breakdown of PS polymer chains."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chemical bonds present in polyester plastics that contribute to the enrichment of fungal colonies in the gut of yellow mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mn represents the number-average molecular weight of polymers, used as a metric in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Number-average molecular weight, a parameter used to assess polymer depolymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Number-average molecular weight, which shows reduction after passage through the digestive tract of larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;Mn, or number-average molecular weight, is a key parameter used to characterize the molecular weight distribution and size of polymer chains in various materials. It represents the average molecular weight of all polymer chains in a sample, calculated based on the number of molecules. Mn serves as an important measure for assessing changes in polymer structure during biodegradation processes, including the degradation of plastics such as polyethylene (PE), polypropylene (PP), polystyrene (PS), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET). These changes can occur due to microbial activity, enzymatic reactions, or other depolymerization mechanisms.

In studies involving biodegradation, Mn has been utilized to evaluate the degree of depolymerization in polymers found in surgical mask layers, PE mulching films, PS residues from antibiotic-treated larvae frass, and PET samples. The number-average molecular weight decreases significantly during these processes, indicating the breakdown of polymer chains. Additionally, Mn has been observed to decrease substantially after mealworm digestion of PVC, further highlighting its role as an indicator of polymer degradation.

While Mn primarily refers to the number-average molecular weight in the context of polymers, it also occasionally denotes manganese content in certain analyses, such as the chemical composition of frass (the excrement of larvae). However, this usage pertains specifically to trace amounts of manganese detected in materials like PS, PU1, and PE, rather than the broader application of Mn as a molecular weight measure.

Overall, Mn is a critical tool in understanding and quantifying the biodegradation of polymers, providing insights into the structural changes that occur during the breakdown of plastic materials by larvae, microbes, or other biological agents.&lt;SEP&gt;"Number-average molecular weight (Mn) provides insights into the size distribution of microplastics during degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MZ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mz (Z-average molecular weight) is a measure used in GPC analysis to assess changes in plastics during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mz (z-average molecular weight) provides additional information about polymer degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mz (z-average molecular weight) provides information about the size distribution of polymers degraded by larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mz indicates the size-average molecular weight, reflecting the highest molecular weight portion of the sample."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mz refers to the z-average molecular weight, showing a reduction during the biodegradation of PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mz represents z-average molecular weight, a measure of polymer size distribution during degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Size-average molecular weight (Mz) represents the largest molecules in a polymer sample."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mz represents the size-averaged molecular weight of PE mulching films, used as a measure of degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mz represents the z-average molecular weight, a measure reflecting the larger-sized molecules in the distribution of PS residues from antibiotic-treated larvae frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mz, or size-averaged molecular weight, provides another measure of polymer chain sizes, emphasizing larger molecules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Size-averaged molecular weight (Mz), representing size-based averages during polymer degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mz is the z-average molecular weight of polymers, utilized to assess changes during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mz is the z-average molecular weight, providing insight into larger polymer chains."&lt;SEP&gt;"Z-average molecular weight, showing substantial percentage reductions after digestion by larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Size-average molecular weight (Mz) indicates the average size of polymer fragments during biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MW DISTRIBUTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MW distribution indicates the range of molecular weights in a polymer sample, shifting towards smaller MW after biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;-CH GROUPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"-CH groups in the main chain of PET break down during biodegradation by T. molitor larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;-CH3 GROUPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"-CH3 groups form as new peaks in residual PET polymers, indicating polymer breakdown."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;-CH2 GROUPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"-CH2 groups appear as new peaks in residual PET polymers, reflecting the depolymerization process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BENZENE RING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Benzene Ring refers to the aromatic ring structure found in polystyrene, which can be destroyed during microbial degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Benzene rings in PS are damaged during the metabolic process, as evidenced by the disappearance of specific vibration bands in FTIR analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"The benzene ring in PET breaks down during degradation, leading to the absence of specific peaks in NMR spectra."&lt;SEP&gt;"The benzene ring is identified in polystyrene through specific absorption bands in the FT-IR spectra."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f102938f307d7404d3f31b7200d93768&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DYSGONOMONADACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dysgonomonadaceae shows increased abundance in the gut microbiota of larvae fed with PET compared to control groups."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPIROPLASMA SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A genus strongly associated with LDPE biodegradation, potentially secreting emulsifying factors enhancing plastic bioavailability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasma is a genus known for its association with plastic degradation and constitutes a dominant portion of the gut microbiota in PET-fed larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasma is a genus of bacteria commonly found in the gut microbiota of T. molitor larvae, associated with plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasma sp. is a bacterial genus observed to be related to PET biodegradation, potentially contributing enzymatic activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasma sp. is a bacterium genus that was found to be the most abundant in T. molitor microbiomes across all feeding variants, including the control, and is generally considered pathogenic in insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasma sp. is generally regarded as a pathogen or male-killing bacterium in insects. Surprisingly, it was absent in the gut microbiome of mealworms in this study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasma sp. is typically considered a pathogen or male-killing bacterium in insects, though it was absent in this study's mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasma sp. is a bacterial genus identified as key in the degradation of different MW PP and lignocellulose."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasma sp. is a genus found predominantly in control-fed larvae, not associated with PET degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasma sp. is one of the dominant bacterial species found in the gut microbiome of mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENTEROCOCCUS SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A genus strongly linked to LDPE biodegradation within the gut microbiomes of T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus reported for plastic-degrading capabilities and significantly enriched in the gut microbiota of PET-fed larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus sp. is another bacterial genus involved in the degradation of PP and lignocellulosic materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus sp. is a bacterial genus found in the gut microbiomes of multiple insect species."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus sp. is a common bacterial species present in the gut microbiome of mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FOURIER-TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (FTIR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"FTIR is a method used to analyze chemical structures, providing evidence of polymer degradation in worm frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is employed to detect chemical changes in PET, offering qualitative evidence of degradation and oxidation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is an analytical technique used to identify chemical compounds, here utilized to detect ethylene glycol presence."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is a spectroscopic technique used to detect chemical changes in polystyrene after digestion by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy identifies chemical bonds and functional groups in organic materials through infrared absorption spectra."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PROTON NUCLEAR MAGNETIC RESONANCE (1H NMR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"1H NMR provides detailed information on the molecular structure of PET, revealing breakdown products and confirming degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACINETOBACTER SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A genus of bacteria associated with PS degradation, specifically isolated as Acinetobacter sp. AnTc-1."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter is a genus enriched in the gut microbiota of PET-fed larvae, potentially contributing to plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter is a genus of bacteria, including NyZ450, capable of degrading plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter sp. is a bacterial genus to which strain NyZ450 belongs, known for its potential in degrading plastic materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter sp. has been associated with LDPE and PS biodegradation in T. molitor and G. mellonella larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter sp. is linked to the degradation of medium molecular weight polypropylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter sp. is a bacterial species strongly associated with polystyrene degradation, isolated from T. castaneum larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 is one of the bacterial strains isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae and involved in polyethylene mulching film degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HAFNIA-OBESUMBACTERIUM SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hafnia-Obesumbacterium is a genus detected in higher abundance in PET-fed larvae, possibly involved in PET degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hafnia-Obesumbacterium sp. is a bacterial species involved in lipid metabolism and PET degradation, showing co-occurrence with Dysgonomonas sp."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CITROBACTER SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Citrobacter is a genus whose relative abundance increases in the gut microbiota of larvae fed with PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter sp. is strongly associated with both PE and PS degradation in T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter sp. is a bacterial species isolated from the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms, associated with the biodegradation of polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter sp. is a bacterial species that was more abundant in plastic-only diets compared to plastic + bran fed diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter sp. is a bacterium associated with the degradation of both PE and PS in the gut of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter sp. is an aerobic bacterium associated with plastic degradation in the gut microbiome of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter sp. is significantly related to polystyrene and polyethylene diets in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter sp. is a microorganism belonging to Enterobacteriaceae, known for its association with PS degradation in T. molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter sp. is a genus of bacteria associated with polypropylene diets in the gut microbiome of Zophobas atratus larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter sp. is a gut microbe associated with the PP-fed microbiome of Z. atratus larvae, potentially playing a role in PP depolymerization."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LACTOCOCCUS SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lactococcus is a genus detected in the gut microbiota of PET-fed larvae, though its role in degradation remains unclear."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lactococcus sp. is a genus found predominantly in control-fed larvae, not associated with PET degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DYSGONOMONAS SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dysgonomonas is a genus significantly enriched in the gut microbiota of larvae ingesting PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dysgonomonas sp. is a bacterial species associated with lipid metabolism and PET degradation, forming positive correlations with specific metabolic pathways."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;16S RRNA GENE AMPLICONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"16S rRNA gene amplicons are used as markers to identify and classify bacterial species in microbiome studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"16S rRNA gene amplicons are used for sequencing and identifying bacterial communities in the gut microbiota of larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"16S rRNA gene amplicons are used to evaluate the gut microbial community responses to PET polymers, revealing shifts in community structure."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANALYSIS OF SIMILARITIES (ANOSIM)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"ANOSIM is a statistical method used to compare microbial communities between different groups, showing significant differences among them."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VENN ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Venn analysis compares overlaps and unique components of bacterial communities across different groups."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OXIDASES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Enzymes that catalyze reactions involving the reduction of oxygen or the oxidation of substrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidases are enzymes that utilize oxygen to transform PET intermediates into further metabolites."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidases, including monooxygenases and dioxygenases, are enzymes over-expressed in PET-fed larvae, contributing to PET oxidation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidases play a role in the biodegradation process by aiding in the oxidation of substrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidases are enzymes that catalyze oxidation reactions, transferring electrons from one molecule to oxygen."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidases, including \(\beta\)-galactosidase and \(\beta\)-glucuronidase, were most active among the studied enzymes but showed no significant differences across experimental variants."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1c9087e78bc6ba94c2b63fd88d2ca66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MONO-(2-HYDROXYETHYL) TEREPHTHALATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
