      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"3-hydroxyanthranilate is an intermediate product resulting from PET degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"A metabolite involved in PET degradation, processed by specific oxidases in the gut microbiome."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PROTOCATECHUIC ACID&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A metabolite derived from terephthalic acid during PET degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Protocatechuic acid is an intermediate compound formed during the aerobic decomposition of PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"Protocatechuic acid is a metabolite derived from the breakdown of aromatic compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Protocatechuic acid is a metabolite derived from the degradation of terephthalic acid during PET biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DECARBOXYLASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme over-represented in PET-fed samples, potentially contributing to PET biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"An enzyme that removes a carboxyl group from a molecule, often producing carbon dioxide."&lt;SEP&gt;"Decarboxylase enzymes remove carboxyl groups from PET intermediates during degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OXIDOREDUCTASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme over-represented in PET-fed samples, potentially contributing to PET biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes that facilitate oxidation-reduction reactions, transferring electrons between molecules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidoreductase enzymes catalyze redox reactions during the breakdown of PET intermediates."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DEHYDROGENASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme over-represented in PET-fed samples, potentially contributing to PET biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dehydrogenase enzymes catalyze the removal of hydrogen atoms from PET intermediates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes that remove hydrogen atoms from a substrate, typically in redox reactions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dehydrogenase contributes to oxidative reactions during the breakdown of complex molecules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dehydrogenase enzymes play a role in metabolic pathways associated with the degradation of polystyrene and lignin."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FLAGELLA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL FEATURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Flagella are structures on bacterial cells that facilitate adhesion by grasping surfaces and aid in electron transport for metabolism."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ELECTRON TRANSPORT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Electron transport is a metabolic process where flagella assist in breaking down organic matter."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBON SOURCE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A carbon source is an essential nutrient required by organisms for energy and growth. Polystyrene can act as a carbon source for certain bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"A carbon source is any organic material used by microorganisms to obtain energy and carbon for growth."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbon source refers to nutrients bacteria seek under low nutrient availability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbon sources are essential nutrients that microorganisms utilize for growth and energy production."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbon source refers to any substance that provides carbon for the growth and metabolism of organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastics like polyethylene serve as carbon sources for certain bacteria during the degradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbon source refers to the material used by organisms for energy and growth; in this study, polystyrene serves as the sole carbon source."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbon source refers to the primary energy-providing substances used by microorganisms, such as polystyrene in this context."&lt;SEP&gt;"A carbon source provides energy and nutrients for microorganisms; in this case, PP serves as a carbon source for larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"The source of carbon derived from PP degradation used by T. molitor larvae for energy and biosynthesis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a8b07ee5dc7a7af59a93d03311efe60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STARVATION CONDITION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Starvation condition is a state where bacterial functions such as adhesion and chemotaxis are highly expressed."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PET DEGRADED PRODUCTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These are small molecules resulting from the breakdown of PET that enter microbial cells via specific transport systems."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13</data>
