      <data key="d1">"A type of plastic degraded by a bacterium isolated from the gut of insect larvae, highlighting bacterial involvement in plastic breakdown."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is a plastic material that can be degraded by T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is a plastic polymer that Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade, with Cl− as a byproduct during mineralization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is a plastic that can be degraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is a thermoplastic polymer widely used in construction due to its durability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyvinyl chloride is a type of plastic that has been shown to degrade by specific bacterial isolates from insect larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is a widely used thermoplastic polymer that can be penetrated by certain insect larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyvinyl chloride is a type of plastic similar to polyethylene, requiring further study for degradation potential in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is a widely produced synthetic plastic polymer, often used in construction due to its durability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyvinyl Chloride is a type of plastic degraded by a bacterial consortium enriched from the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PVC is a widely used plastic known for its durability but also its environmental concerns."</data>
