      <data key="d2">chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACTERIAL STRAINS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROBE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacterial strains are microorganisms isolated from the guts of plastic-eating waxworms capable of degrading polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacterial strains are specific types of bacteria with distinct characteristics and capabilities, such as degrading plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacterial strains such as Bacillus sp., Enterobacter asburiae, Acinetobacter sp., and Exiguobacterium sp. are isolated for their ability to degrade plastics like PS and LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Three potential bacterial strains were identified as candidates involved in the biodegradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Specific bacterial strains isolated from T. molitor guts are capable of degrading bioplastics such as PBSA, PBS, and PCL."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacterial strains from the guts of plastic-eating waxworms are capable of degrading polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacterial strains refer to specific groups of bacteria with unique characteristics, some of which can degrade plastics like polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacterial strains are specific types of bacteria, some of which have been identified for their ability to degrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacterial strains refer to specific genetic variants or subtypes of bacteria, often identified for their unique characteristics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f4c282fe2323dc6e310501af888c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GRAVIMETRIC DETERMINATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gravimetric determination is a method used to assess biodegradation by measuring weight loss of materials over time."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gravimetric determination measures weight loss to assess biodegradation of polystyrene by bacterial strains."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gravimetric determination measures weight loss, an essential indicator of biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d80979bb7e58b10bb558705b5d96fa99</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THERMOGRAVIMETRICAL ANALYSIS (TGA)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"TGA determines the thermal stability of a polymer, indicating biodegradation results."&lt;SEP&gt;"TGA is a technique used to determine the thermal stability of polymers and assess biodegradation based on changes in stability."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DARKLING BEETLES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Darkling beetles (Coleoptera: Tenebrionidae) include species like T. molitor and T. obscurus, known for their potential to degrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Darkling beetles are insects whose gut microorganisms have been studied for their ability to degrade plastics like PS and LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Darkling beetles, including species like Tribolium castaneum, can chew and degrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Darkling beetles are the adult form of mealworms, whose larvae consume and degrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Darkling beetles belong to the family Tenebrionidae and are known for their ability to ingest and degrade plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MOTH LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"INSECT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Moth larvae are insects whose gut microorganisms have also been studied for their plastic-degrading capabilities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LDPE (LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"LDPE is a plastic polymer whose degradation is strongly associated with unclassified Enterobacteriaceae."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE is a type of polyethylene microplastic whose biodegradation depends on its molecular weight."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low-density polyethylene is another type of plastic that can be degraded by specific bacterial strains found in insect guts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACINETOBACTER SP. STRAIN NYZ450&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial isolate from the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, capable of forming a biofilm on polyethylene and contributing to its degradation when in co-culture with Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae and contributes to the biodegradation of polyethylene mulching films."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 is a bacterium capable of co-culture degradation of LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 is part of a co-culture that degrades LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 is a PE-degrading bacterial strain isolated from the intestine of yellow mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae involved in polyethylene mulching film degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 is a bacterial strain capable of degrading polyethylene mulching films in co-culture."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACILLUS SP. STRAIN NYZ451&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial isolate from the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, capable of forming a biofilm on polyethylene and contributing to its degradation when in co-culture with Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. strain NYZ451 is another bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae, working synergistically with Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 to degrade polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451 is a bacterium capable of co-culture degradation of LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451 is part of a co-culture that degrades LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451 is another PE-degrading bacterial strain isolated from the intestine of yellow mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Another bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae, working in co-culture with Acinetobacter sp. strain NyX450 for polyethylene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451 is a bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae that collaborates with Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 in degrading polyethylene mulching films."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451 is another bacterial strain involved in the co-culture degradation of polyethylene mulching films."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163</data>
    </node>
