    <node id="&quot;T. OBSCURUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"T. obscurus is another genus of mealworms with documented plastic-degrading abilities."&lt;SEP&gt;**T. obscurus**, also known as dark mealworms, is a species of beetle larvae within the *Tenebrio* genus that has garnered significant attention for its ability to consume and biodegrade various types of plastics, including polystyrene (PS) and low-density polyethylene (LDPE). These larvae exhibit metabolic adaptations to plastic diets and are capable of degrading plastics through symbiotic reactions with their gut microbiota. Studies have demonstrated that **T. obscurus** demonstrates high efficiency in biodegrading PS, with its gut bacteria playing a crucial role in PS depolymerization.

Compared to *T. molitor*, another well-studied species of mealworm, **T. obscurus** often shows higher survival rates and better PS consumption ability when fed on PS alone or supplemented with co-diets. Additionally, **T. obscurus** larvae have exhibited improved utilization of plastics when supplemented with food additives, indicating potential enhancements in their biodegradation capabilities under specific conditions. The species is also recognized for containing higher cysteine (Cys) content compared to *T. molitor*, which may contribute to its unique metabolic properties.

Research into microbial diversity assessments has highlighted the importance of studying **T. obscurus** alongside *T. molitor* to better understand the mechanisms underlying plastic degradation. While some studies suggest that **T. obscurus** exhibits independence or reduced dependence on gut microbes for LDPE biodegradation, others emphasize the critical role of gut microorganisms in the depolymerization process. Despite these variations, **T. obscurus** consistently demonstrates LDPE depolymerization capabilities similar to or even superior to those of *T. molitor*.

Furthermore, **T. obscurus** larvae are commercially reared for animal feed due to their nutritional value and are actively investigated for their role in breaking down plastic polymers such as PE powders. Their gut microbiome adapts to different diets, including PE microplastics (PE-MPs), further enhancing their potential in plastic waste management.

Overall, **T. obscurus** is a promising candidate for bioremediation efforts targeting plastic pollution, showing similar or even superior capabilities in polymer depolymerization and biodegradation compared to *T. molitor*. This species continues to be studied extensively for its potential applications in environmental sustainability and waste management solutions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45</data>
    </node>
