    <node id="&quot;TENEBRIO MOLITOR LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">**Tenebrio molitor larvae**, commonly referred to as mealworms or yellow mealworms, represent the larval stage of the darkling beetle (*Tenebrio molitor*). These organisms are widely studied for their unique ability to biodegrade various types of plastics, including polystyrene (PS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polypropylene (PP) foam, polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polylactic acid (PLA), cellulose, and aged polyethylene (PE). Their digestive system harbors specific bacterial strains, such as *Acinetobacter* sp. strain NyZ450 and *Bacillus* sp. strain NyZ451, which play a critical role in breaking down plastics like PE. 

The larvae consume these materials and convert them into frass, which contains excreted compounds with new oxygen-containing functional groups, indicating chemical modification of the plastics. Beyond synthetic plastics, **Tenebrio molitor larvae** can also digest natural lignocellulosic polymers, showcasing their versatility in processing both artificial and organic substrates. Physiologically, these larvae weigh approximately 59.6 ± 2.2 mg per larva, with lengths ranging from 2.0 to 2.5 cm, and exhibit metabolic changes while consuming plastics. Additionally, they display developmental plasticity depending on their diet.

Their capacity to degrade plastics is attributed to a combination of host enzymes and gut microbiota, making them an important model organism for isolating PE-degrading bacterial strains and studying biochemical traits and structural changes in their gut microbiome during plastic consumption. The gut microbes of **Tenebrio molitor larvae** play a significant role in the biodegradation of plastics, producing various biodegradation products. Specifically, they are responsible for the biodegradation of PE and PS microplastics, contributing to potential solutions for environmental plastic waste management. 

Overall, **Tenebrio molitor larvae** serve as a crucial model for investigating plastic-degrading macroinvertebrates and understanding the mechanisms behind their biodegradation capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f141d484320c21ec01b892a9b7d56486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3</data>
    </node>
