    <node id="&quot;TENEBRIO MOLITOR LINNAEUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SPECIES"</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrio molitor Linnaeus, commonly known as mealworms or yellow mealworms, is the scientific name for a species of beetle whose larvae have been extensively studied for their unique ability to degrade plastics, particularly polystyrene. The larvae of Tenebrio molitor Linnaeus, referred to as yellow mealworms or simply mealworms, exhibit chewing and eating behavior that allows them to consume materials such as Styrofoam. This consumption contributes to the biodegradation of plastic waste through the action of gut microbiota present in the larvae.

These insects are larger than waxworms and have shown potential in degrading polystyrene efficiently, making them a significant subject in research focused on sustainable waste management solutions. Studies investigating plastic degradation often center around Tenebrio molitor Linnaeus due to its larvae's ability to digest polystyrene, offering promising implications for addressing environmental plastic waste issues. Overall, Tenebrio molitor Linnaeus plays a critical role in the exploration of biological methods for managing plastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff228c7711f94fb27152b187b1fa3eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81b6237c84f002117fbfe7575d9cdd6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WAXWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Waxworms (Galleria mellonella) have gut microbiota that degrade plastics, releasing fatty acids."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are another species of insects known for their ability to degrade plastics using gut microbiota."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are caterpillars of certain moth species, known for their ability to consume and degrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are larvae of Plodia interpunctella, known for chewing and eating PE films, with bacterial strains isolated from their gut capable of degrading PE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are the larvae of certain moth species, some of which have been found to biodegrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms, specifically Galleria mellonella larvae, have been found to biodegrade polyethylene with bacterial strains from their guts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are larvae of certain moth species that have been found to harbor gut microorganisms capable of degrading plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are the larvae of wax moths, notable for harboring gut bacteria capable of degrading plastics like polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms, specifically Galleria mellonella, have demonstrated the ability to biodegrade polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are the larvae of certain moths, whose gut microbiota can degrade polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are larvae of moths, recently studied for their ability to degrade polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are organisms capable of consuming and degrading polyethylene (PE), with their growth influenced by the nutrients in their diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are insect larvae, specifically from the genus Galleria, noted for their gut bacteria capable of degrading plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are larvae of certain moth species that have been shown to degrade polyethylene using specific bacterial strains in their guts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are insects whose gut microbiota includes bacterial strains capable of biodegrading polyethylene."</data>
