    <node id="&quot;ESTERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Compounds detected in the frass, part of the bioactive substances."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esters are bioactive compounds identified in metabolomics studies related to the degradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esters are chemical compounds formed by the reaction between an alcohol and an acid, often found in biological systems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esters are chemical compounds formed from the reaction between alcohols and acids, abundant in beeswax and essential for waxcomb structure."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AMIDES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Amides are bioactive compounds formed during enzymatic activation and amide bond formation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Amides are bioactive compounds found in the polar fraction of frass, indicating enzymatic activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Amides are compounds whose formation correlates with enzymatic activity and bio-catalytic amide bond formation during plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Amides such as oleamide, tetradecanamide, and hexadecanamide are metabolites whose levels vary depending on the diet applied."&lt;SEP&gt;"Compounds identified in the frass, contributing to the bioactive substances."&lt;SEP&gt;"Amides are organic compounds containing a carbonyl group linked to a nitrogen atom, often involved in biochemical processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e4030ea35bee29093c1a8f19bc90774&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Α-GLUCOSIDASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"α-Glucosidase is an enzyme contributing to the breakdown of plastics within the gut microbiome of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"α-Glucosidase is an enzyme that breaks down α-glucosides into glucose."&lt;SEP&gt;"α-glucosidase hydrolyzes α-glucosidic linkages, supporting metabolic pathways."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
    </node>
