      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial species observed in higher growth rates in Diet 2 conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus faecium is a bacterial species that may thrive in water-rich environments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus faecium is a bacterium abundant in the gut microbiome of T. molitor larvae, playing a role in plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LACTOCOCCUS LACTIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial species identified as more abundant in the gut of T. molitor fed with polystyrene (PS)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lactococcus lactis is a bacterial species belonging to the Streptococcaceae family, identified as one of the candidates potentially involved in PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lactococcus lactis is a bacterium present in the gut microbiome of mealworms, contributing to plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lactococcus lactis is a lactic acid bacterium involved in fermentation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"A bacterial species known to form biofilms on polystyrene surfaces, not directly observed in this study but implicated in potential EPS degradation."</data>
