      <data key="d2">chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STYRENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Styrene is a monomer and potential intermediate metabolite in the biodegradation process of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene is a monomer derived from PS degradation, found in the excreted frass of insects."&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene is a chemical compound characterized by its vinyl and aromatic groups, serving as a key monomer in the synthesis of polystyrene (PS). It undergoes both aerobic and anaerobic degradation via various metabolic pathways, making it an important intermediate product during the breakdown of polystyrene. Styrene can be detected through techniques such as FTIR analysis and targeted GC analysis, confirming its role as the primary thermal decomposition product of PS.

In biodegradation processes, styrene serves as a substrate for microbial degradation, involving specific metabolic pathways and enzymes like alkylglycerol monooxygenase, aldehyde dehydrogenase, alcohol dehydrogenase, and peroxidase. Microorganisms oxidize styrene to form compounds such as acetophenone, phenylacetate, and phenylacetyl-CoA, which are integrated into broader metabolic cycles, including the citric acid cycle.

While styrene is observed in frass as a result of PS degradation, its presence is generally weak, indicating the depletion of polystyrene content in the material. Contrary to some observations, styrene was not significantly detected in insect biomass, suggesting that it may not accumulate within the insects themselves. Instead, it is metabolized or excreted during the degradation process. Additionally, styrene oxide is one of the metabolic products formed during its degradation.

Overall, styrene plays a critical role as a precursor monomer for polystyrene, a chemical compound involved in both synthetic manufacturing processes and natural microbial decomposition pathways. Its versatility as both a building block and a degradable compound highlights its significance in both industrial and environmental contexts.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041</data>
    </node>
