      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial genus observed in the gut microbiome of T. molitor when fed with PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Staphylococcus is a bacterial genus noted in the mealworm gut microbiome, playing a role in microbial community composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Staphylococcus exhibits higher abundance in CS-fed larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Staphylococcus shows significant association with gut microbiome assembly factors."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PS BIODEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PS biodegradation is the process by which polystyrene is broken down by microorganisms present in the gut of Tenebrio larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS biodegradation refers to the biological breakdown of polystyrene by microorganisms and enzymes, highlighting an important environmental process."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS biodegradation refers to the breakdown of polystyrene (PS) by microorganisms into simpler substances."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS biodegradation refers to the degradation of polystyrene by microbial enzymatic activities."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process by which polystyrene is broken down by microbial activity in the gut of T. molitor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a8b07ee5dc7a7af59a93d03311efe60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDRATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A physiological state influenced by the presence or absence of water in the mealworms' diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydration affects mealworm survival, growth, and PS biodegradation efficiency."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydration affects metabolic and biochemical activities in PS-containing diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydration enhances gut microbial activity and improves PS consumption yields and insect growth."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FRASS PROTEIN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Protein content measured in the frass of mealworms, reflecting dietary effects."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INSECT BIOMASS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure of the weight and health of mealworms influenced by their diet."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT HEALTH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gut health refers to the overall well-being and functionality of the digestive system, influenced by microbial communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"The overall condition of the digestive tract of T. molitor, influenced by bacterial composition."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FRASS NITROGEN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Frass nitrogen refers to nitrogenous compounds found in insect waste, influenced by diet composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrogen content in the frass provides insights into the nutritional metabolism and biodegradation processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MONOMERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Monomers are breakdown products of PS, observed in varying amounts depending on the diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Smaller molecular units derived from styrene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Monomers are the smaller units produced from the breakdown of polymers during biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SHORT-CHAIN FATTY ACIDS (SCFAS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"SCFAs are produced by gut microbiota and play a role in intestinal homeostasis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STYRENE MONOOXYGENASE (SMO)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"SMO is an enzyme involved in the anaerobic degradation of styrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STYRENE OXIDE ISOMERASE (SOI)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme that converts reactive oxirane into phenylacetaldehyde."&lt;SEP&gt;"SOI catalyzes the conversion of styrene oxide to phenylacetaldehyde."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STYRENE-2,3-DIOXYGENASE (SDO)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"SDO introduces two oxygen atoms into styrene, forming styrene cis-glycol."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHENYLACETALDEHYDE DEHYDROGENASE (PAD)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme converting phenylacetaldehyde into phenylacetic acid."&lt;SEP&gt;"PAD is an enzyme involved in the aerobic degradation pathway of styrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PS RESIDUES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PS residues refer to the remaining polystyrene fragments after partial degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANAEROBIC DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A process occurring in the absence of oxygen, involving the breakdown of organic matter."&lt;SEP&gt;"Anaerobic degradation is the breakdown of styrene in the absence of oxygen."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AEROBIC DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A process requiring oxygen, where organic compounds are broken down into simpler substances."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aerobic degradation is the breakdown of styrene in the presence of oxygen."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STYRENE OXIDE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An intermediate compound formed during the degradation of styrene by certain microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene Oxide is a metabolite involved in the degradation process of styrene by certain microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene oxide is an intermediate product formed during the degradation of styrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene oxide is a reactive metabolite of styrene that has shown positive carcinogenic results in bioassays."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STYRENE-2,3-DIHYDRODIOL DEHYDROGENASE (SDD)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"SDD catalyzes the re-aromatization of styrene cis-glycol to 3-vinylcatechol."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;3-VINYLCATECHOL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"3-vinylcatechol is an intermediate compound in the degradation pathway of styrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"A metabolite formed via the enzymatic action of hydrogenase, which can undergo meta-cleavage breakdown."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VINYLCATECHOL 1,2-DIOXYGENASE (VC12O)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme responsible for the meta-cleavage breakdown of 3-vinylcatechol into 2-vinyl-cis,cis-muconic acid."&lt;SEP&gt;"VC12O performs meta-cleavage breakdown of 3-vinylcatechol into 2-vinyl-cis,cis-muconic acid."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VINYLCATECHOL 2,3-DIOXYGENASE (VC23O)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme involved in the meta-cleavage breakdown of 3-vinylcatechol, producing 2-hydroxy-6-oxoocta-2,4,7-trienoic acid."&lt;SEP&gt;"VC23O performs meta-cleavage breakdown of 3-vinylcatechol into 2-hydroxy-6-oxohepta-2,4-dienedioic acid."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHENYLACETALDEHYDE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A compound produced by SOI isomerization and further converted into phenylacetic acid."&lt;SEP&gt;"Phenylacetaldehyde is an intermediate compound produced during styrene degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STYRENE OXIDE REDUCTASE (SOR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
