      <data key="d2">chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLY(L-LACTIC ACID)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Poly(L-lactic acid) is a biodegradable polyester used in various applications, including medical and packaging industries."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HIGH-MOLECULAR-WEIGHT POLYETHYLENE GLYCOLS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"High-molecular-weight polyethylene glycols are polymers that can be degraded by certain microorganisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THERMOPHILES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Thermophiles are microorganisms capable of thriving at high temperatures, often involved in the degradation of heat-resistant materials."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIODEGRADABLE PLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biodegradable plastics are designed to break down naturally in the environment, potentially reducing plastic pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradable plastics are designed to break down naturally in the environment, reducing long-term environmental impact."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradable plastics are materials designed to break down through microbial activity, with studies focusing on their degradation by phyllosphere yeasts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradable plastics, such as polyactic acid (PLA), are compared to conventional plastics like polyethylene in terms of their degradation rates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradable plastics are materials designed to break down naturally in the environment, offering a potential solution to plastic pollution."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WAX MOTH LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Wax moth larvae (Galleria mellonella) are insects whose gut microbiota includes polystyrene-degrading bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wax moth larvae (Galleria mellonella) are macro-organisms reported to accelerate the biodegradation of polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wax moth larvae possess enzymes capable of breaking down complex polymers found in plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wax moth larvae, specifically from the species Galleria mellonella, exhibit similar behavior to mealworms in degrading plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wax moth larvae are insect larvae that contribute to the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wax moth larvae are macro-organisms investigated for their ability to degrade plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GASIFICATION&quot;">
