      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"1,2,4-TCB is a chlorinated aromatic hydrocarbon often used as a solvent or reagent."&lt;SEP&gt;"1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene (1,2,4-TCB) is a solvent used for extracting PE polymers in analytical procedures."&lt;SEP&gt;"1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene (TCB) is an organochlorine compound used as a solvent and chemical intermediate."&lt;SEP&gt;"1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene is a solvent used in the extraction of LDPE samples for analysis by GPC."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PS MICROPLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PS (polystyrene) microplastics are ingested by mealworms as a sole diet. Their metabolism varies depending on the region of origin of the mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS microplastics are plastic materials ingested by mealworms and metabolized, showing changes in crystalline structure upon digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene microplastics that undergo depolymerization and degradation, leading to changes in molecular weight."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS Microplastics refer to polystyrene microplastics used as a sole carbon source in experiments to test microbial degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS microplastics are small particles of polystyrene used as a carbon source in ex situ microbial cultures for PS degradation experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS microplastics are small particles of polystyrene used as a carbon source in experiments to study microbial degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS microplastics are small polystyrene particles used in microbial cultures to study degradation processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PVC MICROPLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PVC (polyvinyl chloride) microplastics are less likely to be digested by mealworms and can only be metabolized by mealworms from Tai'an and Shenzhen."&lt;SEP&gt;"PVC microplastics are plastic materials ingested by mealworms, with some variations in crystalline structure depending on the mealworm's origin."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyvinyl chloride microplastics that decompose through dechlorination and chain scission processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LDPE MICROPLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"LDPE (low-density polyethylene) microplastics are metabolized by mealworms from all three regions tested."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE microplastics are plastic materials ingested by mealworms, transformed into amorphous polymer after digestion by mealworms from certain regions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low-density polyethylene microplastics that undergo thermal decomposition into alkenes such as ethane, ethene, propane, propene, butane, and butene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
