    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SYNERGISTIC DRIVING FORCES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Synergistic driving forces refer to combined effects of larval digestive systems and microbial activities in biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PE-BIODEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PE-biodegradation refers to the degradation of polyethylene through biological means, potentially accelerated by larval digestive systems and microbial activities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TENEBRIO GENUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Tenebrio genus includes T. molitor and other species involved in studies of plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio genus includes species such as mealworms that have been studied for their ability to degrade plastics like PS and LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Tenebrio genus includes beetles such as mealworms, whose larvae have shown potential in biodegrading plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ZOPHOBAS ATRATUS LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Another type of insect larvae being studied for their potential in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms whose gut microbiota may also contribute to polyethylene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus larvae, also known as superworms, are insects whose gut microbes have been shown to digest plastics such as polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus larvae, also known as superworms, exhibit different depolymerization patterns for EPS and LDPE depending on the strain (G or M)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus larvae are known to biodegrade plastics like polystyrene and low-density polyethylene, causing fragmentation and shifts in gut microbiota."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus larvae, also known as superworms, have shown potential in biodegrading LDPE foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus larvae, also known as superworms, are insects used in experiments for biodegradation of PP foam. They weigh 611.0 ± 12.7 mg/larva and are 3.0–4.0 cm in length."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus larvae are insect larvae known for their ability to degrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus larvae are plastic-degrading insect larvae proposed for future studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OXYTETRACYCLINE ANTIBIOTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ANTIBIOTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Oxytetracycline antibiotics were used in a suppression test to determine if gut microbes contribute to the depolymerization of EPS in land snails."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;COPROCOCCUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Coprococcus is a genus of anaerobic gut cocci whose abundances increased significantly in larvae fed with PS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MORGANELLA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Morganella is a bacterial genus found in the gut of yellow mealworms and superworms on PP diet, strongly correlating with the inner layer mask diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Morganella is a genus of gut microbes whose abundances decreased in larvae fed with PE and PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Morganella is another bacterial genus that showed increased relative abundance in the gut microbial community after feeding on mask layers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Morganella is a genus of bacteria whose relative abundance varies depending on the diet association."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CROSSLINKING REACTIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Crosslinking reactions involve the formation of covalent bonds between polymer chains, potentially affecting biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Crosslinking reactions occur during depolymerization/biodegradation, potentially causing an increase in Mw."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NMDS ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Non-metric multidimensional scaling (NMDS) analysis evaluates the similarity of samples based on their community structure."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DIETS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Diets refer to the food consumed by T. molitor larvae, including bran, PE (polyethylene), and PS (polystyrene)."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DYELLA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dyella is a genus of bacteria that significantly increases in T. molitor larvae fed with PS and may be involved in the degradation of PS intermediates."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LYSOBACTER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lysobacter is a genus of bacteria significantly associated with the microbiome fed with PS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LEPTOTHRIX&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Leptothrix is a genus of bacteria significantly associated with the microbiome fed with PS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KOSAKONIA SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Kosakonia sp. is a bacterial species that was more abundant in plastic-only diets compared to plastic + bran fed diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kosakonia sp. is a facultative anaerobic bacterium linked to plastic degradation within the mealworm gut microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kosakonia sp. is another bacterial species identified in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms, playing a role in the biodegradation of polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kosakonia sp. is strongly associated with both PE and PS degradation in T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kosakonia sp. is another bacterium strongly linked to the degradation of PE and PS in the gut of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kosakonia sp. is associated with polystyrene and polyethylene diets in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kosakonia sp. is a microorganism within Enterobacteriaceae, linked to PS degradation in T. molitor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACILLUS SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacillus is a genus known for its involvement in the biodegradation of PP."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus is a genus of bacteria, including NyZ451, known for its versatile metabolic capabilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. increases in T. molitor larvae fed with PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. is a bacterial genus to which strain NyZ451 belongs, known for its potential in degrading plastic materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus is a genus of bacteria, some species of which are involved in the biodeterioration of polypropylene microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. is a genus of rod-shaped, Gram-positive bacteria, including the strain PELW2042 capable of degrading HDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. refers to bacteria within the Bacillus genus that participate in the enzymatic degradation of polystyrene by forming biofilms and causing material deterioration."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. is a genus identified in low MW PP-fed T. molitor larvae, potentially involved in PP degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. plays a role in the metabolism of low molecular weight polypropylene alongside other bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. is a bacterial genus isolated from insect guts that contributes to the breakdown of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. refers to a bacterial genus involved in the gut microbiome of Galleria mellonella."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. is a genus of rod-shaped, Gram-positive bacteria known for its ability to degrade polypropylene microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. is a genus of rod-shaped, Gram-positive bacteria involved in the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451 is another bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae and plays a role in the degradation of polyethylene mulching films."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. represents bacteria from the Bacillus genus involved in polystyrene degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PENG ET AL.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"RESEARCH REFERENCE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A research study observing the community shift in T. obscurus fed PS versus bran."&lt;SEP&gt;"Researchers who isolated bacterial strains from the intestinal flora of yellow mealworms capable of degrading plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"A team of researchers studying the biodegradation of various plastics, including PS, PVC, and PUR, by T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Peng et al. contributed analytical evidence supporting the findings on EPS degradation by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Peng et al. investigated the role of gut bacteria in EPS biodegradation and analyzed fecula molecular weights using GPC."&lt;SEP&gt;"Peng et al. conducted studies on the biodegradation of various polymers, identifying specific functional groups and chemical shifts via spectroscopic techniques."&lt;SEP&gt;"Peng et al. investigated the biodegradation of plastics by Z. atratus larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Peng et al. provided insights into the FTIR analysis and ¹H NMR spectra during LDPE biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e</data>
