    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DIGESTIVE ENZYMES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Digestive enzymes activated by larval gut functions facilitate the degradation of polyolefins like PP."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestive enzymes are proteins produced by the host organism to aid in the breakdown of polypropylene during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestive enzymes in the gut of plastic-degrading macroinvertebrates enable the biodegradation of petroleum-based plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestive enzymes are proteins produced by organisms such as Zophobas aratus larvae, involved in breaking down polymers during plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestive enzymes produced by the lesser waxworms may play a role in the biodegradation of polyethylene, though this requires further investigation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestive enzymes are necessary for breaking down food and were affected by nutrient exhaustion in mealworms fed microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestive enzymes are proteins produced by organisms to break down complex molecules into simpler forms for absorption."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestive enzymes contribute to the breakdown of plastics within the mealworm digestive system."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC DEGRADATION-RELATED BACTERIAL ENZYMES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Specific bacterial enzymes up-regulated in the transcriptome of Tenebrio molitor contribute to the biodegradation of polypropylene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ULOMA SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Uloma sp. is a genus of insect larvae reported to degrade polystyrene while completing its life cycle."&lt;SEP&gt;"Uloma sp. is another insect species involved in the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Uloma sp. larvae have been reported to degrade polyethylene wastes, completing their life cycle while consuming it."&lt;SEP&gt;"Uloma sp. refers to a genus of honey bee pests whose larvae can degrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Uloma sp. refers to a genus of darkling beetles with confirmed ability to degrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Uloma sp. refers to a genus of organisms capable of degrading plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Uloma sp. represents a group of insect species that could be tested for their plastic degradation capabilities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748</data>
    </node>
