      <data key="d1">"Cronobacter sp. is a genus found predominantly in control-fed larvae, not associated with PET degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cronobacter sp. is a genus involved in plastic degradation and nitrogen fixation in T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cronobacter sp. is another major bacterial species identified in the mealworm gut microbiome."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MIXTA SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mixta sp. is a genus found in significant abundance in T. molitor larvae fed medium MW PP."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PEDIOCOCCUS SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Pediococcus sp. is a bacterial genus associated with the production of organic acids from water-soluble carbohydrates during fermentation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pediococcus sp. is a genus that may utilize water-soluble carbohydrates during fermentation and is associated with high MW PP degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLOSTRIDIALES FAMILY XIV. INCERTAE SEDIS UNCLASSIFIED&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This unclassified genus within Clostridiales Family XIV was enriched in the gut of T. molitor larvae fed with low MW PP."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LACHNOSPIRACEAE UNCLASSIFIED&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An unclassified genus within Lachnospiraceae, found in low MW PP-fed T. molitor larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VIRGIBACILLUS SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Virgibacillus sp. is a genus with low abundance but potential involvement in PP degradation in T. molitor larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PCA (PRINCIPAL COMPONENT ANALYSIS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PCA is a statistical method used to analyze and visualize variations in gut microbiota composition based on genera."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCA is a statistical method used to analyze differences in gut fungal communities across different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCA is a statistical technique applied to visualize differences in gut bacterial communities between bran-fed and plastics-fed groups."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EUCLIDEAN DISTANCES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Euclidean distances are used in heatmaps to represent similarities or differences in gut microbiota compositions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LDPE (LOW DENSITY POLYETHYLENE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"LDPE is a type of plastic where Acinetobacter sp. has been strongly associated with its biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PP FOAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PP Foam is a polypropylene-based material used as feedstock in the experiment. It has Mn of 356.2 kDa, Mw of 109.8 kDa, and Ms of 765.0 kDa, with a density of 0.02 g/cm³."&lt;SEP&gt;"PP Foam is a type of polypropylene product that serves as both a control sample and diet for larvae in this study."&lt;SEP&gt;"PP Foam refers to polypropylene foam with a relatively high molecular weight, degraded by Kluyvera sp. in previous studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"PP foam refers to a polypropylene-based product with a relatively high molecular weight, used in the study for comparison."&lt;SEP&gt;"PP foam is ingested by insect worms, correlating with increased Enterobacteriaceae abundance."&lt;SEP&gt;"PP foam refers to the polypropylene material used in experiments, serving as the substrate for microbial biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WHOLE-PLANT CORN SILAGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Whole-plant corn silage is a feedstuff that supports microbial fermentation and organic acid production."&lt;SEP&gt;"Whole-plant corn silage is a nutrient source where Pediococcus sp. is involved in organic acid production during fermentation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PP-H&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PP-H is a polypropylene variant with a molecular weight of 750.00 kDa, used as a reference in this study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WATER-SOLUBLE CARBOHYDRATES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Water-soluble carbohydrates serve as substrates for fermentation processes leading to the production of organic acids."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;16S RRNA DATA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"16S rRNA data provides insights into bacterial diversity and their roles in polypropylene degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACINETOBACTER NYZ450&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Acinetobacter NyZ450 is a bacterium involved in synergistic biodegradation of polyethylene mulching films."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACILLUS SPP. NYZ451&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacillus spp. NyZ451 collaborates with Acinetobacter NyZ450 for the biodegradation of polyethylene mulching films."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CUTINASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cutinase is an enzyme involved in the degradation of cutin and related synthetic polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cutinase is an enzyme with homologous activity towards polyethylene terephthalate (PET) degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cutinase is an enzyme that acts on ester bonds in polymers such as PET, aiding in their degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cutinase is an enzyme that can degrade PET under specific conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METABOLIC PATHWAYS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Metabolic pathways describe the biochemical reactions involved in the degradation of plastics by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolic pathways describe the series of chemical reactions occurring within a cell or organism, relevant to understanding plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolic pathways represent the most enriched functional category among KEGG pathways, indicating active metabolism in PP-fed larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"The biochemical routes through which degraded PP molecules are processed within microbial cells."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ABC TRANSPORTER PATHWAYS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"ABC transporter pathways facilitate the intracellular importation of intermediate products following extracellular depolymerization of PP."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHEMOTAXIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chemotaxis is the movement of microorganisms toward chemical gradients, observed in gut bacteria attracted to PET metabolic intermediates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chemotaxis-related pathways enable bacterial navigation toward PP, aiding in community colonization and motility."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NITROGEN METABOLISM PATHWAY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nitrogen metabolism pathway (KO00910) indicates nitrogen fixation capabilities in PP-fed larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ATP-BINDING CASSETTE (ABC) TRANSPORTER PATHWAYS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These pathways facilitate the importation of intermediate products from PP degradation into bacterial cells."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TWO-COMPONENT SYSTEM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL FUNCTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A signaling mechanism activated in larval gut flora to respond to dietary signals and promote PE biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The two-component system is a chemotaxis-related pathway aiding in bacterial community coordination during PP degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACTERIAL CHEMOTAXIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacterial chemotaxis allows bacteria to move toward PP, supporting their role in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacterial chemotaxis is the movement of bacteria towards or away from chemical stimuli in their environment."&lt;SEP&gt;"The movement of bacteria towards or away from chemical stimuli, influencing their interactions with the environment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FLAGELLAR ASSEMBLY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Flagellar assembly contributes to bacterial motility, enabling navigation toward PP for degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SECONDARY METABOLITES BIOSYNTHESIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This pathway is up-regulated in larvae fed with PP, indicating involvement in plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROBIAL METABOLISM IN DIVERSE ENVIRONMENTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This pathway reflects the adaptability of gut microbiota to degrade various substrates, including PP."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AMINO ACID METABOLISM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Amino acid metabolism is up-regulated in PP-fed larvae, suggesting its role in plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HOST TRANSCRIPTOME RESPONSE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The host transcriptome response reflects gene expression changes in \(T\). molitor larvae during PP degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DIFFERENTIALLY EXPRESSED GENES (DEGS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENETIC CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"DEGs are genes whose expression levels change significantly under different conditions, such as during plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"DEGs indicate specific genetic responses in \(T\). molitor larvae when fed different MW PP polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MONOXYGENASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A class of oxidases involved in the degradation of PP polymers by introducing oxygen into the polymer structure, facilitating chain breaking."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PEROXIDASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme that uses hydrogen peroxide to oxidize various substrates, aiding in the breakdown of PP polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Peroxidase catalyzes the breakdown of lignin and other aromatic compounds during the degradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Peroxidase is an enzyme involved in the degradation of PS derivatives through oxidation reactions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Peroxidase is an enzyme involved in the degradation of oxidized polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MRPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROTEIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Multidrug resistance proteins (MRPs) activated by the host larvae to manage plastic efflux and reduce metabolic pressure."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOEMULSIFYING FACTORS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bioemulsifying factors produced by gut microbes accelerate plastic degradation by enhancing the interaction between enzymes and substrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Substances secreted by both the host and gut microbiota that assist in the attachment of enzymes to the surface of PP particles."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRANSMEMBRANE PROTEINS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROTEIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Proteins responsible for transporting degraded PP molecules into microbial cells for further metabolism."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROXYLATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A chemical process where a hydroxyl group (-OH) is added to the PP polymer backbone, aiding in its biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxylation is a secondary enzymatic modification involving the addition of hydroxyl groups to degradation intermediates."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBOXYLATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A process that introduces carboxyl groups (-COOH) into the PP polymer structure, enhancing its susceptibility to further degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHEWING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL FUNCTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chewing is the mechanical process by which larvae break down LDPE into smaller fragments, increasing its surface area for microbial and enzymatic activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"The mechanical action performed by T. molitor larvae to defragment PP particles into smaller pieces for enzymatic attack."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INNATE DIGESTIVE ENZYMES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Endogenous enzymes secreted by T. molitor larvae involved in the digestion of food and plastic substrates."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC DEGRADATION-RELATED ENZYMES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Specialized enzymes produced by T. molitor larvae and gut microbiota to break down plastic polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SIGNAL TRANSDUCTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL FUNCTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The cellular communication process activated in response to nutrient-deficient conditions caused by PP diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process by which extracellular signals are converted into intracellular responses, guiding cellular behavior."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DIETARY ADAPTIVE REGULATORY MECHANISM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL FUNCTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The mechanism by which T. molitor larvae adapt their metabolism to survive on nutrient-restricted PP diets."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENVIRONMENTAL IMPLICATIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Environmental implications describe the potential ecological impacts of plastic degradation by macroinvertebrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"The broader ecological significance of T. molitor larvae's ability to biodegrade commercial PP products."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ΔΔ¹³C&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure used to assess the extent of PP biodegradation by comparing isotopic compositions before and after degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMER MW&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
