    <node id="&quot;NPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"NPS refers to a compound or powder utilized in experiments for assessing bacterial degradation capabilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"NPS refers to nano-polystyrene, a type of synthetic polymer that serves as a substrate for microbial degradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"NPS refers to nanoplastics, which are degraded through microbial action, particularly by Achromobacter xylosidans M9."&lt;SEP&gt;"NPS refers to the nanoplastics or microplastics derived from polystyrene that are being analyzed for their degradation and interaction with bacterial strains."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nano-polystyrene (NPS) is a carbon source used in the degradation study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nano-sized polystyrene particles used in biodegradation experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics (NPs) are ultra-small plastic particles smaller than 1 μm, with increased reactivity and potential to induce severe physiological stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics, even smaller plastic particles resulting from further fragmentation of microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"NPs refer to nanoplastics, smaller plastic particles that pose significant environmental risks."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHTHALIC ACIDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Phthalic acids are chemical compounds that were tested for presence in the plastic materials, with concentrations below detection limits."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C: H: O: N RATIO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This ratio represents the elemental composition of wheat bran, analyzed using an elemental analyzer."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPECIFIC PLASTIC CONSUMPTION RATE (SPCR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"SPCR measures the rate at which larvae consume different types of microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AVERAGE WEIGHTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Average weights reflect the growth or reduction in size of larvae depending on their diet."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FAT AND CRUDE FAT CONTENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Measures indicating fat content changes in larvae due to diet variations."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PROTEIN AND CRUDE PROTEIN CONTENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Indicators of protein levels in larval biomass influenced by dietary intake."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MOLECULAR WEIGHT (MN, MW, MZ)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Molecular weight measurements provide critical information about the size and structure of PVC, PS, and PLA polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight measures (Mn, Mw, Mz) indicate changes in polypropylene structure during depolymerization, showing reductions or increases depending on the species."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RAPID GROWTH PERIOD&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The rapid growth period refers to the developmental stage of T. molitor larvae during which they exhibit significant physiological changes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ELEMENTAL ANALYZER (VARIO EL CUBE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An elemental analyzer used to determine the chemical composition of wheat bran, providing precise C: H: O: N ratios."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KAPLAN-MEIER SURVIVAL ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A statistical method used to analyze survival rates of T. molitor larvae over the 6-week period."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHLORINATED INTERMEDIATES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TOXIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Byproducts of PVC degradation contributing to toxicity and oxidative stress in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chlorinated intermediates are byproducts generated from PVC degradation that may negatively impact larval health."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chlorinated intermediates are toxic compounds released during the biodegradation of PVC."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chlorinated intermediates are toxic substances produced during the depolymerization of PVC, potentially harmful to mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STORED BIOMASS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Stored biomass refers to the energy reserves within T. molitor larvae utilized for survival when no exogenous food is available."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CO-DIET&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Co-diet refers to the addition of bran to the diet of larvae, which reduces oxidative stress and lipid peroxidation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Co-diet refers to the inclusion of wheat bran alongside microplastics, enhancing larval growth and survival."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BINZHOU MEALWORM CO.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A company supplying T. molitor larvae and wheat bran for the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SHANDONG, CHINA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The location where Binzhou Mealworm Co. is based, indicating the origin of study materials."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROLYSIS REACTIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydrolysis reactions are chemical processes that involve breaking bonds in polymers using water, contributing to the degradation of plastics within the gut microenvironment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DIGESTIVE PROCESSES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Digestive processes refer to the mechanisms by which larvae metabolize and process plastics and other nutrients in their gut."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMER DEGRADABILITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polymer degradability refers to the ability of plastics to be broken down into simpler forms, influencing the growth and physiological responses of T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polymer degradability refers to the ease with which a polymer can be broken down by biological or chemical means."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EXOGENOUS FOOD SOURCE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Exogenous food source refers to external nutrients like bran that supplement the diet of larvae consuming plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LONG-TERM GROWTH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Long-term growth is a measure of the sustained development of T. molitor larvae influenced by their diet and environmental factors."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHYSIOLOGICAL FUNCTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL FUNCTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Physiological function encompasses the normal biological processes and activities of T. molitor larvae, affected by their diet and exposure to plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOMASS NUTRIENT CONTENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biomass nutrient content refers to the levels of key nutrients such as total sugar, fat, and protein in the larvae, reflecting their nutritional status."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENERGY RESERVES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Energy reserves refer to the stored energy in the form of carbohydrates and fats within the larvae, utilized during periods of insufficient nutrition."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHLORINATED ORGANICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TOXIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chlorinated organics are toxic compounds resulting from the incomplete biodegradation of PVC in the larvae, contributing to their malnutrition."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROPHOBIC PLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydrophobic plastics, such as PS and PVC, have low water affinity, affecting their degradation and impact on larval hydration."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NEGATIVE PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Negative physiological responses include dehydration, malnutrition, and reduced growth in larvae exposed to certain plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RADAR MAP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Radar map is a graphical representation used to evaluate the overall nutrient content and physiological status of larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC REMOVAL EFFICIENCIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic removal efficiencies refer to the percentage of plastics successfully removed or degraded by larvae over a specific period."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ROS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Reactive Oxygen Species (ROS) is a type of unstable molecule that contains oxygen and easily reacts with other molecules in a cell, leading to oxidative stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive Oxygen Species (ROS) is a type of unstable molecule that contains oxygen and reacts with other molecules in cells, serving as an important biological marker for oxidative stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are free radicals produced during oxidative stress, causing damage to cellular structures if not neutralized."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are produced at high nanoplastic concentrations, causing damage to bacterial cell membranes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive oxygen species (ROS) levels were measured to assess oxidative stress in mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PVC + BRAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A diet consisting of Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC) combined with bran, used to study its effects on ROS levels in larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PS + BRAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A diet consisting of Polystyrene (PS) combined with bran, used to study its effects on ROS levels in larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLA + BRAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A diet consisting of Polylactic Acid (PLA) combined with bran, used to study its effects on ROS levels in larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FLUORESCENCE INTENSITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Fluorescence intensity is a quantitative measure used to assess the level of ROS in biological samples, providing insights into oxidative stress."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC-DEGRADING ABILITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL FUNCTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The ability of organisms to degrade plastics is a crucial factor in understanding their role in mitigating plastic pollution."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBOXYLESTERASES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carboxylesterases are enzymes involved in the degradation of PLA polymers via enzymatic hydrolysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROGEN CHLORIDE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TOXIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydrogen chloride is a byproduct released during the biodegradation of PVC via oxidative depolymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrogen chloride is a toxic byproduct of PVC depolymerization that may impair intestinal health."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OXIDATIVE DEPOLYMERIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Oxidative depolymerization is the process by which PVC and PS are broken down, releasing harmful byproducts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENZYMATIC HYDROLYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Enzymatic hydrolysis is the process by which PLA polymers are degraded by specific enzymes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymatic hydrolysis refers to the chemical breakdown of PET using enzymes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROGEN PEROXIDE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydrogen Peroxide is a chemical compound used to test catalase activity in bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) is a product of SOD activity and is further broken down by CAT into water and oxygen."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MEMBRANE LIPIDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Membrane lipids are components of cell membranes that can be damaged by free radicals if antioxidant defenses fail."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLY(LACTIC ACID) (PLA)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Poly(lactic acid) (PLA) is a biodegradable polymer tested for its impact on Tenebrio molitor larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANTIOXIDANT ENZYMATIC ACTIVITIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Antioxidant Enzymatic Activities encompass the actions of enzymes like SOD, GST, and CAT that counteract oxidative stress."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLY(BUTYLENE ADIPATE-CO-TEREPHTHALATE) (PBAT)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PBAT is a biodegradable polyester that mealworms can metabolize."&lt;SEP&gt;"PBAT is a biodegradable polymer that may be fragmented into smaller sizes by earthworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Poly(butylene adipate-co-terephthalate) (PBAT) is a biodegradable polymer proposed for future studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SUPERWORMS (ZOPHOBAS ATRATUS LARVAE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Superworms are organisms where reactive oxygen species trigger oxidative degradation of polyethylene in their gut."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACILLUS THURINGIENSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROBE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacillus thuringiensis is a bacterium used as a basis for creating green nanocatalysts with enhanced photocatalytic activity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RUTHENIUM/NICKEL CO-DOPED ZINC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ruthenium/nickel co-doped zinc is a material with potential applications in efficient H2 production."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MALACHITE GREEN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL COMPOUND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Malachite Green is a chemical compound used as a textile dye or antimicrobial, featuring a styrene core structure."&lt;SEP&gt;"Malachite Green is a toxic compound that can be biodegraded by Kocuria rosea MTCC 1532."&lt;SEP&gt;"Malachite green is a chemical compound with a styrene core structure, used as a textile dye or antimicrobial in aquacultures."&lt;SEP&gt;"Malachite green is a toxin that can be removed using novel nanocomposites."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GO@ZNO-NIFE2O4-AL2O3 NANOCOMPOSITES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These nanocomposites are innovative materials designed for adsorptive removal of toxins."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ADVERSE OUTCOME PATHWAYS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Adverse outcome pathways describe mechanisms through which micro- and nanoplastics induce oxidative stress and inflammation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pathways describing the progression of toxicity from molecular initiating events to adverse health outcomes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INFLAMMATORY RESPONSE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"IMMUNE RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Inflammatory response is part of the immune system's reaction to harmful stimuli, linked here to plastic exposure."&lt;SEP&gt;"Inflammatory response refers to the body’s reaction to harmful stimuli, such as exposure to microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ALTERNATIVE PROTEIN SOURCE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mealworms are considered an alternative protein source for monogastric animals, highlighting their nutritional value."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHOTOCATALYTIC ACTIVITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Photocatalytic activity describes the ability of materials like ruthenium/nickel co-doped zinc to accelerate chemical reactions under light."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SODIUM BOROHYDRIDE METHANOLYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Sodium borohydride methanolysis is a chemical reaction used in hydrogen production processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DIGESTIVE FRAGMENTATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Digestive fragmentation is the process by which organisms break down microplastics during digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestive fragmentation is the process by which organisms like Antarctic krill break down microplastics into smaller particles."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MEALWORM (TENEBRIO MOLITOR LARVAE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mealworms, specifically the larvae of Tenebrio molitor, are explored as an alternative protein source for monogastric animals."</data>
