    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PROTEINASE K&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e</data>
      <data key="d1">"Proteinase K was used in enzymatic lysis for genomic DNA extraction."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MFR INVESTIGATION&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
      <data key="d1">"MFR investigation shows that recycled polystyrene has increased molecular weight, affecting its accessibility to mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT MICROBIOMES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gut microbiomes of Tenebrio molitor play a synergistic role in the biodegradation of PET intermediates and provide nitrogen sources."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut microbiomes refer to the microbial communities within the digestive tracts of superworms and yellow mealworms, which play a role in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut microbiomes refer to the microbial community in the digestive tract of organisms, which respond to dietary changes such as PP ingestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut microbiomes of larvae are investigated for their role in microplastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MOLECULAR SIZE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The molecular size of the polymer influences the rate of PET degradation by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HIGH-CRYSTALLINITY PET&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"High-crystallinity PET is a form of polyethylene terephthalate with a high degree of crystallinity, making it more challenging to degrade."&lt;SEP&gt;"PET with high crystallinity, indicating a more challenging substrate for biodegradation compared to low-crystallinity PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STABLE CARBON ISOTOPES (\(\DELTA^{13} \MATHRM{C}\))&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Stable carbon isotopes are used as a measure to confirm the biodegradation of high-crystallinity PET by testing changes in \(\delta^{13} \mathrm{C}\)."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ATP BINDING CASSETTE (ABC)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROTEIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"ATP binding cassette proteins are involved in various transport mechanisms and may play a role in the metabolism of depolymerized PET intermediates."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBOHYDRATE-ACTIVE ENZYMES (CAZY)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carbohydrate-active enzymes are involved in the degradation of complex carbohydrates and could potentially interact with PET intermediates during biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KYOTO ENCYCLOPEDIA OF GENES AND GENOMES (KEGG)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENETIC CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"KEGG is a database that helps in understanding the functional interactions of genes and metabolites involved in PET degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MONOHYDROXYETHYL TEREPHTHALATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Monohydroxyethyl terephthalate (MHET) is a breakdown product of PET during its degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACTINOMYCETOTA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYLUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Actinomycetota is a phylum of bacteria that includes species capable of degrading PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PSEUDOMONADOTA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYLUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Pseudomonadota is a phylum of bacteria with members involved in PET degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACTERIODOTA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYLUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacteriodota is another bacterial phylum containing species that contribute to PET hydrolysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SURFACE HYDROPHOBICITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Surface hydrophobicity affects the biodegradability of plastics, as it influences microbial attachment and enzymatic activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Surface hydrophobicity describes the water-repellent characteristics of a material's surface, often measured by water contact angle (WCA)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Surface hydrophobicity refers to the extent to which a polymer resists wetting by water, impacting biodegradation rates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Surface hydrophobicity measures the tendency of a surface to repel water, which affects enzymatic activity on polymer surfaces."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BROAD EXTENT DEPOLYMERIZATION (BD)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Broad extent depolymerization involves significant breakdown of polymers into smaller units, observed in the biodegradation of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LIMITED EXTENT DEPOLYMERIZATION (LD)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Limited extent depolymerization occurs when high molecular weight polymers degrade slowly, resulting in accumulation or minimal breakdown."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FT-IR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"FT-IR (Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy) is a technique used to identify oxidized groups during the biodegradation of polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) is a technique used to analyze chemical bonds and properties of residual PET polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Δ¹³C&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Stable isotope signature (δ¹³C) is used to analyze the isotopic composition of residual plastics, indicating oxidation during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"δ¹³C (stable carbon isotope ratio) is used as an indication of PET biodegradation, reflecting isotopic exchange during microbial activity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ISOTOPIC EXCHANGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Isotopic exchange involves the replacement of \(^{12}C\) with \(^{13}C\) during biodegradation, enriching \(^{13}C\) in residual PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NITROGEN SOURCES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nitrogen sources are organic nutrients that supplement the digestion of ingested plastics by gut microbiota."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROCARBON VAPOR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydrocarbon vapor is a type of organic compound that undergoes biodegradation through microbial activity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;REDUCTIVE DECHLORINATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Reductive dechlorination is a process where chlorinated compounds are reduced to non-chlorinated forms during biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PE, PP, PS, PVC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These are various types of polymers studied for their biodegradation characteristics using FT-IR and \(^1\)H NMR techniques."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STABLE CARBON ISOTOPE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Stable carbon isotopes, such as \(^{13}C\), are used to track the biodegradation process through isotopic enrichment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HOST TRANSCRIPTOME&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENETIC CONCEPT"</data>
