    <node id="&quot;ACHROMOBACTER XYLOSOXIDANS M9&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterium isolated from Tenebrio molitor capable of biodegrading polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9 is a Gram-negative bacterium isolated from the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae. It shows significant abilities in degrading nanoplastics, reducing nanoplastic particle size by 92.3% within 6 days."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestines of yellow mealworms, known for its ability to degrade petroleum hydrocarbons and plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9 is a bacterial strain isolated from yellow mealworms intestines that contributes to polystyrene degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE FILM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An additive-free styrene polymer film with a thickness of 0.05 mm provided by GoodFellow."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene Film is a thin layer of polystyrene used as a substrate for studying microbial degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene film is a form of polystyrene that was degraded by Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9, resulting in a 7% weight loss over 30 days."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene film is a material whose morphological properties are altered by the bacterial strain Achromobacter xylosidoxans OR859752."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene film represents a solid form of polystyrene with slower degradation rates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene Film is used as a substrate for bacterial attachment and degradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene film is a material dissolved in chloroform to prepare EPS solution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene film is a material used in experiments to test the degradation activity of microorganisms. Its surface appearance changes when exposed to certain bacteria."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa64e5cd2c08637934509262e56ab363&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a32ea55d79e87933e26b4165db47b1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA DSM 50071&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Pseudomonas aeruginosa DSM 50071 is a bacterial strain isolated from Zophobas atratus that degrades polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas aeruginosa DSM 50071 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestine of the superworm (Zophobas atratus) that can degrade PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas aeruginosa DSM 50071 is a bacterium known for its ability to degrade high-density polyethylene (HDPE)."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d</data>
    </node>
