      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polystyrene Foam Density refers to the density variations of polystyrene foams, which influence their degradation rates in mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;14C-LABELED POLYSTYRENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"14C-Labeled Polystyrene is a form of polystyrene used in studies to track its biodegradation through radiolabeling techniques."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHOTOLYTIC OR THERMOLYTIC CLEAVAGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Photolytic or Thermolytic Cleavage refers to processes where chemical bonds in polystyrene are broken using light or heat, respectively."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOLOGICAL MECHANISM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biological Mechanism describes the natural processes by which organisms like mealworms can break down complex materials such as polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biological mechanism refers to the underlying processes by which bacteria degrade polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-126ff1ea4b1c89f82a750386be59f926</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EDIBLE PROTEIN SOURCE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Edible Protein Source refers to mealworms as a sustainable alternative to traditional animal protein sources for human consumption."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOREGENERATIVE LIFE SUPPORT SYSTEMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bioregenerative Life Support Systems involve recycling resources within closed-loop environments, with mealworms proposed for astronaut food supply."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANOBIIDAE FAMILY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Anobiidae Family includes beetles known for damaging packaging materials, but without detailed investigation into their plastic-eating behavior."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Anobiidae family is a taxonomic group of beetles, mentioned alongside the Tenebrionidae and Dermestidae families in studies related to plastic consumption."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DERMESTIDAE FAMILY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dermestidae Family comprises beetles also noted for their ability to damage packaging materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Dermestidae family is a taxonomic group of beetles, studied similarly to the Tenebrionidae family for potential plastic degradation capabilities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EPS FOAMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"EPS foams (expanded polystyrene) are a type of plastic material that has been consumed by mealworms in various studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;XPS FOOD CONTAINERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"XPS food containers (extruded polystyrene) are another form of plastic material that mealworms have been observed consuming."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EPS FOAM PLATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"EPS foam plate is a specific type of expanded polystyrene material used in tests evaluating the impacts of added nutrients and temperature on PS degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYETHYLENE STORAGE CONTAINERS&quot;">
