      <data key="d1">"PS-degrading microbial activities refer to the processes by which microbes in the gut of larvae degrade polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MATURATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Maturation is the developmental process leading to the readiness for reproduction."&lt;SEP&gt;"Maturation refers to the biological process of larvae reaching their mature stages, potentially affecting PS consumption rates."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THF EXTRACTABLE RATIO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"THF extractable ratio (TER) is a biochemical measure that reflects the residual PS polymer content in larval frass."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROPHILIC PROPERTY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydrophilic property indicates the increased affinity for water observed in degraded polystyrene samples."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VOLATILE ORGANICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Volatile organics are byproducts detected during thermal analysis, likely originating from gut secretions and biodegradation processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOLOGICAL WASTES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biological wastes are residues produced during the degradation of polystyrene, identified through thermal analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBOXYLIC ACID COMPOUNDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carboxylic acid compounds are intermediates formed during the biodegradation of polystyrene, contributing to volatile organics."&lt;SEP&gt;"These are degradation products formed during the biodegradation of polymers such as polypropylene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;N2 AMBIENCE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL FEATURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"N2 ambience refers to an inert nitrogen environment used during TGA to study thermal decomposition of materials."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MASS LOSS RATIO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mass loss ratio quantifies the extent of decomposition at specific temperature ranges during TGA analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mass loss ratio quantifies the extent of polystyrene degradation in the frass of T. obscurus and T. molitor larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROBIAL COMMUNITY DIVERSITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microbial community diversity describes the variety and abundance of bacterial species within the guts of T. obscurus and T. molitor larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC-DEGRADING ECOSYSTEM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An ecosystem involving microorganisms capable of breaking down plastics, specifically found in the gut of Tenebrio larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLUSTER I&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TAXONOMIC GROUPING"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A taxonomic cluster where the nifH genes of Klebsiella isolates were classified."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cluster I includes Dark-11 and Yellow-11 samples, showing high similarity in microbial community structure after feeding on PS for 11 days."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLUSTER II&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cluster II includes Yellow-1 and Yellow-6 samples, representing a distinct grouping of microbial communities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLUSTER III&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cluster III includes the Dark-6 sample, separated from other clusters, indicating differences in microbial community structure."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLUSTER IV&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cluster IV includes the Dark-1 sample, further verifying shifts in gut microbial structure due to PS ingestion."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SYNERGISTIC RELATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Synergistic relation describes the cooperative interactions between key functional microorganisms involved in PS depolymerization."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f</data>
    </node>
