      <data key="d2">chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLADOSPORIUM SP. P7&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cladosporium sp. P7 is a fungal strain with the capacity to biodegrade polyester polyurethane through specific metabolic pathways."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ALCYCLIPHILUS SP. BQB&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Alcycliphilus sp. BQB is a bacterial strain studied for its ability to degrade adipate/phthalate polyester polyurethanes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AMIDEASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Amidease is an enzyme that works synergistically with esterase to enhance the degradation of thermoplastic polyurethanes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ADIPATE/PHTHALATE POLYESTER POLYURETHANES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Adipate/phthalate polyester polyurethanes are commercial-type plastics that can be degraded by certain bacterial strains."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SUPERWORM (ZOPHOBAS ARATUS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Superworms (Zophobas aratus) exhibit different plastic ingestion preferences and efficiencies compared to yellow mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DEGRADATION CAPACITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Degradation capacity refers to the ability of organisms or enzymes to break down complex materials such as plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THERMOPLASTIC POLYURETHANES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Thermoplastic polyurethanes are materials that can be enzymatically recycled through synergistic enzyme action."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BUILDING BLOCKS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Building blocks refer to the monomers recovered from the enzymatic recycling of polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HIGH PURITY MICROPLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"High purity microplastics are used in experiments to study the effects of physical-chemical properties on plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"High purity microplastics are used to study the impact of physical-chemical properties on depolymerization and biodegradation in mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHYSICAL-CHEMICAL PROPERTIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Physical-chemical properties of materials influence their susceptibility to biodegradation processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EVA DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dygonomonas was specific to EVA-fed groups, indicating its unique role in EVA degradation."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYOLEFIN DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
      <data key="d1">"Acinetobacter was dominant in polyolefin-fed groups, suggesting its role in polyolefin degradation."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BASELINE DIET&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hyphopicha is dominant in bran-fed groups but significantly decreases in plastic-fed groups."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT BACTERIAL COMMUNITIES&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hierarchical cluster analysis elucidates relationships and similarities among gut bacterial communities based on diet."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC POLLUTANTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENVIRONMENTAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic pollutants refer to non-biodegradable plastic waste that harms ecosystems, with polypropylene being a major contributor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;IPP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"iPP is a form of PP where the methyl groups are arranged on one side of the carbon chain, making it commercially significant."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;APP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"aPP refers to an atactic form of PP with irregular methyl group arrangement, present in small amounts in commercial products."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"sPP is a form of PP characterized by alternating arrangements of methyl groups along the polymer backbone."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METHYL GROUP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Methyl groups (\(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\)) play a critical role in determining the structure and properties of PP."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;P. STUTZERI&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"P. stutzeri is another bacterial species contributing to the biodegradation of iPP."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SULFATE REDUCING BACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Sulfate reducing bacteria contribute to the biodegradation of PP in selective enrichment cultures."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ISOPARAFFIN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Isoparaffin consists of side chains and is more difficult to biodegrade compared to linear n-paraffin."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OXYGEN ABSORPTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Oxygen absorption is part of the initial degradation mechanism for PP, leading to chain scission and formation of oxygenated functional groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxygen absorption refers to the initial degradation mechanism of PP, where oxygen reacts with the polymer backbone."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METHYL KETONES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Methyl ketones are products formed as part of the degradation pathway of PP."&lt;SEP&gt;"Methyl ketones are products formed during the biodegradation of PP through oxidation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SELECTIVE ENRICHMENT CULTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Selective enrichment culture involves growing microorganisms under specific conditions to enhance their ability to degrade PP."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GRAFTING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Grafting involves attaching biodegradable polymers like lignin to PP to improve its biodegradability."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BLENDING STARCH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Blending starch with PP can enhance its biodegradability by introducing biodegradable components."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PP FILMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PP Films are polypropylene films that were pre-treated with UV and incubated with Bacillus and Pseudomonas strains to observe their biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMER BACKBONE ATTACK&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polymer backbone attack describes the chemical interaction between oxygen and the polymer structure during PP degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC MPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic microplastics (MPs) are small particles detected in various invertebrates, indicating environmental contamination."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;UV PRE-TREATMENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"UV pre-treatment refers to the process of exposing PP films to ultraviolet light before incubation with microbes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC PACKAGE MATERIALS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic package materials refer to the types of plastics used in packaging that are damaged by certain insects."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MPS INGESTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MPs ingestion refers to the process by which microplastics are consumed and retained by invertebrates."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Z. ATRATUS LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SPECIES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Z. atratus larvae specifically refer to the larvae of Zophobas atratus used in biodegradation experiments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT MICROBE-DEPENDENT PS DEPOLYMERIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gut microbe-dependent PS depolymerization describes the role of gut microbes in breaking down polystyrene in insect larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LDPE DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"LDPE degradation refers to the breakdown of low-density polyethylene by insect larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPECIFIC PP CONSUMPTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Specific PP consumption measures the amount of polypropylene consumed by larvae per unit weight or number over time."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PET FOOD FACTORY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A supplier of Tenebrio molitor larvae located in Luoyang, Henan, China."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SUPERWORMS HOUSE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A supplier of Zophobas atratus larvae and wheat bran located in Yangjiang, Guangdong, China."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KUNSHAN BALKLINE COMPANY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A supplier of PP foam located in Suzhou, China."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MS (Z-AVERAGE MOLECULAR WEIGHT)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure of the average molecular weight based on scattering intensity, used to describe PP foam properties."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ARTIFICIAL CLIMATE INCUBATOR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EQUIPMENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An incubator from Shanghai Shuli, used to maintain controlled environmental conditions for larval rearing."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPECIFIC PP CONSUMPTION RATES (SPPCR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure of the amount of PP consumed per larva or per group of larvae per day."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANTIBIOTIC SUPPRESSION TEST&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Antibiotic suppression test is a method used to determine the dependency of biodegradation on gut microbes by suppressing them with antibiotics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Antibiotic suppression test is a procedure involving the use of antibiotics to suppress gut microbes to study their role in PP biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Antibiotic suppression test is used to confirm the dependence of plastic biodegradation on gut microbes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MILLI Q WATER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Milli Q water is ultra-pure water used in the decontamination process of larvae before gut analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;80% ETHANOL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"80% ethanol is used for decontaminating the larvae prior to gut microbial analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPECIFIC PP CONSUMPTION RATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Specific PP consumption rate measures the efficiency of PP consumption by the larvae under different dietary conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Specific PP consumption rate measures the efficiency of PP consumption per unit weight of larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WCA ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"WCA analysis (water contact angle analysis) measures the hydrophobicity or hydrophilicity of PP foam and frass samples."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INITIAL WEIGHT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL CHARACTERISTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Initial weight refers to the starting weight of larvae before feeding experiments begin."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CUMULATIVE PP CONSUMED&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cumulative PP consumed quantifies the total amount of polypropylene consumed by larvae over time."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;&lt;SUP&gt;1&lt;/SUP&gt;H NMR ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt;H NMR Analysis is a technique used to determine the structure and composition of organic compounds by analyzing hydrogen atoms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BRUKER AVANCE NEO 600 SPECTROMETER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A high-resolution spectrometer used for analyzing 1H NMR spectra of dissolved samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bruker Avance NEO 600 Spectrometer is a device used for performing &lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt;H NMR analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BRUKER CORPORATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bruker Corporation is the manufacturer of the Bruker Avance NEO 600 Spectrometer."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CAMBRIDGE ISOTOPE LABORATORIES, INC.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cambridge Isotope Laboratories, Inc. is the supplier of Chloroform-D solvent."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MP BIOMEDICALS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MP Biomedicals provides the Fast DNA SPIN extraction kits used for DNA extraction."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DIGESTIVE REAGENTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Digestive reagents are substances produced by larvae to facilitate the digestion process of PP."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CANNIBALISM RATES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cannibalism Rates indicate the frequency of larvae consuming each other due to nutritional limitations."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BODY BIOMASS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Body Biomass refers to the organic material within the larvae consumed when essential nutrients are lacking."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MOLTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Molts refer to the discarded exoskeletons of larvae, consumed under nutritional stress."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;YOUNG LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Young larvae represent an earlier developmental stage of Z. atratus with different survival characteristics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MATURE LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mature larvae indicate a later developmental stage of Z. atratus showing aggressive cannibalism behavior."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPPCR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"SPPCR represents specific polypropylene consumption rates, which are calculated based on larval numbers and weights."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ht represents the distribution of accumulated molecular number portion versus molecular weight, indicating shifts in polymer structure."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NUTRITION LIMITATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nutrition limitation refers to the condition where insufficient nutrients lead to behavioral changes such as increased cannibalism."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIODEGRADED INTERMEDIATES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biodegraded intermediates are compounds formed during the breakdown of plastics that may affect larval health."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NON-EXTRACTABLE FRACTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Non-extractable fraction contains solid intermediates and undigested residues from the larval digestive system."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C-C BONDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carbon-carbon bonds are covalent bonds between carbon atoms in polymers that can be broken during biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMER-DEGRADING ENZYMES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These enzymes are specialized proteins that facilitate the breakdown of polymers into simpler compounds."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VIRGIN PP FOAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The original polypropylene foam used as a control in experiments to compare with biodegraded samples."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PP BIODEGRADATION RESIDUES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Residual products resulting from the partial biodegradation of polypropylene within the larval gut."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C–H ALKYL BEND&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C–H alkyl bend refers to a chemical bond vibration observed in FTIR spectra, indicating specific molecular structures within PP foam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C–H RING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C–H ring is a chemical bond associated with aromatic structures in FTIR analysis, found in the initial PP foam but absent in frass samples."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;-C=O CARBONYL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"-C=O carbonyl is a functional group formed during the oxidation process of PP polymers, confirmed by FTIR analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;-C-O STRETCH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"-C-O stretch represents a functional group identified in the biodegradation process of PP, indicating oxygen incorporation into the polymer chain."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;O–H OF THE HYDROXYL BAND&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"O–H of the hydroxyl band signifies the presence of hydroxyl groups in the biodegraded PP polymer, suggesting increased hydrophilicity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AUTA ET AL.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"RESEARCH TEAM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Auta et al. refers to a research team that contributed findings on the biodegradation of PP foam and identified key chemical changes in frass samples."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PIRES ET AL.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"RESEARCH TEAM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Pires et al. investigated enzymatic additives in soil for iPP degradation, providing insights into carbonyl group formation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SKARIYACHAN ET AL.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"RESEARCH REFERENCE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Skariyachan et al. described the breakdown of long-chain polymers into CH₂ moieties during biodegradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Skariyachan et al. documented the formation of CH₃ moieties due to polymer breakdown during biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ZAINAL ET AL.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"RESEARCH REFERENCE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Zainal et al. identified metabolites or residues like amino acids at specific peaks in frass samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zainal et al. identified metabolites such as amino acids formed during the biodegradation of polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KUNDUNGAL ET AL.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"RESEARCH REFERENCE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Kundungal et al. documented chemical modifications in polyolefin plastics due to biodegradation, specifically highlighting new peaks at 2.34 ppm."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kundungal et al. reported observations on chemical modifications during HDPE biodegradation by A. grisella and Uloma larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CH₂ MOIETY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"CH₂ moiety refers to the formation of methylene groups observed during the breakdown of long-chain polymers in biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ALDEHYDE MOIETY (-CHO)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Aldehyde moiety (-CHO) is a functional group identified in T. molitor frass extracts, indicating further oxidation of PP polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C=C-H (ALKENE BONDS)&quot;">
