    <node id="&quot;A. HYDROPHILA MA32&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterium isolated from the intestine of mealworms, with potential involvement in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"A. hydrophila MA32 exhibits similar surface appearance to the polystyrene film without microorganism inoculation, showing no degrading activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"A. hydrophila MA32 is a bacterial strain that also does not grow well on polystyrene micro-sprayed plates."&lt;SEP&gt;"A. hydrophila MA32 is a bacterial strain with reduced adhesion to polystyrene film."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Y. REGENSBURGEI MA19&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterium isolated from the intestine of mealworms, potentially involved in polystyrene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Y. regensburgei MA19 consistently exhibits polystyrene-degrading activity through cell adhesion, growth, and decomposition processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Y. regensburgei MA19 is a bacterial strain capable of adhering to polystyrene film."&lt;SEP&gt;"Y. regensburgei MA19 is a bacterial strain involved in the degradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Y. regensburgei MA19 is a bacterial strain that reduces the size of polystyrene particles and decreases their edge sharpness."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-126ff1ea4b1c89f82a750386be59f926&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;K. GRIMONTII MA76&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterium whose growth is affected by salt concentration, isolated from the intestine of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"K. grimontii MA76 consistently exhibits polystyrene-degrading activity through cell adhesion, growth, and decomposition processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"K. grimontii MA76 is a bacterial strain capable of adhering to polystyrene film."&lt;SEP&gt;"K. grimontii MA76 shows changes in polystyrene particles, indicating potential degradation ability."</data>
