    <edge source="&quot;BIODEGRADATION&quot;" target="&quot;DEPOLYMERIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d3">208.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Biodegradation includes depolymerization as a key step, where polymer chains are broken into smaller molecules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation involves depolymerization, where polymers break down into smaller units, affecting PS and PE."&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation and depolymerization are interconnected processes that play a crucial role in the breakdown of polymers into simpler compounds. Biodegradation involves the chemical reaction of depolymerization, where complex polymers such as polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and polypropylene (PP) are broken down into smaller units or monomers. This process is particularly significant in environments like the mealworm's digestive system, where enzymatic activities of microorganisms facilitate the breakdown of these polymers.

Depolymerization serves as a critical step within the broader biological process of biodegradation. It initiates the breakdown of polymer chains by reducing their molecular weight, enabling further degradation through oxidation and mineralization. Enzymatic activity plays a key role in this process, breaking down the macromolecule backbones of polymers into simpler substances. This allows for the complete biodegradation of plastics, including PS, PE, HDPE, LDPE, and PP, making depolymerization an essential initial step in the overall biodegradation mechanism. 

In summary, biodegradation relies heavily on the depolymerization process to transform complex polymers into smaller, more manageable units, ultimately facilitating their assimilation and decomposition in natural environments.</data>
