    <edge source="&quot;BIODEGRADATION&quot;" target="&quot;MINERALIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d3">112.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Biodegradation and mineralization are sequential processes in the breakdown of organic materials, with the former leading to the latter."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation is followed by mineralization, where organic PET derivatives are converted into inorganic compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation leads to mineralization, where organic materials are transformed into inorganic compounds, completing the nutrient cycle."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mineralization is part of the biodegradation process, where PS is converted into simpler inorganic compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation often leads to mineralization, where organic polymers are broken down into simpler inorganic compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation processes eventually lead to mineralization, breaking down complex plastics into simpler inorganic components."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation leads to mineralization, where organic matter like PS is converted into inorganic compounds such as CO2."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mineralization is a key aspect of biodegradation, where polystyrene is broken down into simpler inorganic compounds by gut bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation often leads to mineralization, where complex organic materials are broken down into simpler inorganic forms, completing the cycle of material transformation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation leads to mineralization, where polystyrene is converted into simpler compounds like CO2."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation leads to mineralization, where organic materials are converted into inorganic compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation often leads to mineralization, showing a sequential relationship between these processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mineralization is a key step in the biodegradation process, where organic material is converted into inorganic compounds."</data>
