    <edge source="&quot;GUT MICROBIOME&quot;" target="&quot;TENEBRIO MOLITOR&quot;">
      <data key="d3">153.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor (commonly known as mealworms) plays a crucial role in enabling the insect larvae to biodegrade various types of waste materials, including plastics such as polyurethane foam, polystyrene, polyethylene, and PET (polyethylene terephthalate), as well as cellulose. This symbiotic relationship between the host digestive system and its microbial community is essential for the effective degradation of complex materials, highlighting an interdependence between the insect and its gut microbiome.

Research indicates that the gut microbiome exhibits adaptability in response to dietary changes, with plastic-fed groups showing higher microbial diversity compared to those fed on conventional diets like bran. This adaptability allows the microbial community to adjust its ecological niche width and diversity when exposed to different polymers, influencing the extent of depolymerization depending on the specific conditions and type of polymer. The gut microbiome undergoes notable changes during the biodegradation process of substrates such as cellulose, polyethylene, and polystyrene, which not only aids in breaking down these materials but also impacts the overall health and function of the larvae.

Furthermore, the synergy between the host digestive system and the gut microbiome is particularly evident in processes such as PET biodegradation, where the microbial community contributes significantly to the breakdown of the plastic. These findings underscore the critical role of the gut microbiome in facilitating the insect's ability to degrade a wide range of substrates, from plastics to natural fibers, thereby positioning Tenebrio molitor as a potential solution for addressing plastic waste management challenges.</data>
