    <edge source="&quot;POLYSTYRENE (PS)&quot;" target="&quot;TENEBRIO MOLITOR&quot;">
      <data key="d3">85.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) possess the intrinsic ability to biodegrade polystyrene through their eating behaviors and gut microbial activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor larvae are capable of modifying and oxidizing polystyrene during digestion, indicating a biological interaction."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor participates in the biodegradation of polystyrene, indicating its potential role in managing plastic pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor larvae degrade polystyrene through their gut microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor larvae consume polystyrene, using it as a carbon source, though it is not nutritionally sufficient for biomass gain or prolonged survival."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor larvae have been observed to degrade polystyrene, demonstrating their versatility in plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yellow mealworms prefer polystyrene due to its low density, enhancing ingestion rates compared to harder plastics like PVC and PE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene is degraded within the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, demonstrating a biological mechanism for breaking down this persistent polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor has been proven to degrade polystyrene, though less efficiently than T. obscurus."&lt;SEP&gt;"The larvae of Tenebrio molitor efficiently degrade polystyrene, demonstrating their potential for plastic waste management."</data>
