    <edge source="&quot;POLYSTYRENE (PS)&quot;" target="&quot;MEALWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d3">141.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Mealworms also degrade polystyrene, though at a slower rate than polyethylene, highlighting their adaptability to different plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume Styrofoam, breaking it down through their digestive processes, establishing a relationship between them."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume and metabolize polystyrene foam, demonstrating their unique capability to degrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms' gut microbiome is responsible for the biodegradation of polystyrene, indicating their role in plastic waste management."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms metabolize Polystyrene effectively, indicating their ability to break it down into smaller components."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms degrade and mineralize polystyrene, demonstrating effective biodegradation capabilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms efficiently degrade Polystyrene, breaking down its long-chain molecules into depolymerized metabolites."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms fed exclusively on polystyrene showed survival but no significant biomass gain, indicating the material's limited nutritional value."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms digest Polystyrene, showing evidence of successful pupation despite lower fat content, paralleling their interaction with PU Foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consumed PS plates, which were processed through extrusion and injection moulding techniques."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume polystyrene at the highest rate among the tested plastics, indicating their preference and efficiency in degrading this material."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume and degrade PS, leading to its depolymerization and modification in the digestive system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume polystyrene, breaking it down into smaller molecular weight components and excreting it as frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foams are fed to mealworms, whose gut and frass specimens are sources for isolating PS-degrading bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms biodegrade polystyrene polymers, altering their gut microbiome and metabolic pathways."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms, the larval stage of Tenebrio molitor, actively consume and degrade polystyrene, indicating an effective mechanism for waste treatment."</data>
      <data key="d5">"biodegradation, consumption"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, gut health"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, insect digestion"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, metabolic activity"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, plastic metabolism"&lt;SEP&gt;"biological function, environmental impact"&lt;SEP&gt;"biological function, waste management"&lt;SEP&gt;"consumption, degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"consumption, processing"&lt;SEP&gt;"degradation, metabolism"&lt;SEP&gt;"digestion, assimilation"&lt;SEP&gt;"metabolism, biodegradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"nutritional deficiency, survival"&lt;SEP&gt;"plastic degradation, metabolic activity"&lt;SEP&gt;"substrate, host interaction"</data>
