    <edge source="&quot;T. MOLITOR&quot;" target="&quot;LDPE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">93.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"LDPE is also consumed by T. molitor larvae, but exhibits different depolymerization patterns compared to EPS."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae fed with LDPE showed significant increases in MP polymer content and differences in carbohydrate content compared to other diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE is metabolized by T. molitor larvae, indicated by differences in Mn and Mw between virgin samples and frass extracts."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae significantly degrade LDPE through broad depolymerization, reducing molecular weight and crystallinity degree."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae significantly reduce the molecular weights of LDPE, indicating broad depolymerization patterns."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae exhibit both broad and limited extent depolymerization patterns for LDPE biodegradation, indicating their ability to degrade this polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae are used to study the biodegradation of LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae consume LDPE, contributing to its biodegradation and producing frass as a byproduct."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae consume LDPE, which affects their gut microbial communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae consumed LDPE, demonstrating higher specific consumption rates and maintaining survival activities."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae perform oxidation and biodegradation of LDPE, leading to changes in its chemical structure."</data>
