    <edge source="&quot;PE&quot;" target="&quot;MEALWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d3">74.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Mealworms also consume PE in addition to PS in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms also consume and degrade PE more efficiently than PS, showing versatility in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume PE at higher rates than polyether-PU, highlighting their adaptability to different plastic substrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume PE, demonstrating their ability to digest multiple types of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms change the surface of PE by introducing wrinkles and bubble-like protrusions, highlighting their ability to biologically degrade this plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consumed PE, and their capacity to degrade it was assessed similarly to PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE undergoes significant depolymerization and chemical modification within the gut of mealworms, suggesting their ability to degrade this plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms ingest PE microplastics, leading to changes in PE's molecular structure through biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The ingestion of PE microplastics influenced the survival rates and body weights of mealworms."</data>
