    <edge source="&quot;PS&quot;" target="&quot;MEALWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d3">171.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Mealworms consume PS as part of their diet during the experimental setup."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume and degrade PS through their gut microbiota, demonstrating their role in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms demonstrate higher consumption rates of PS compared to polyether-PU, indicating differences in their metabolic capabilities for various plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms mineralize PS into biomass, demonstrating their ability to degrade certain plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS serves as a feedstock for mealworms, influencing their survival and degradation capabilities depending on dietary supplements like bran."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume and digest PS, leading to significant changes in its surface structure, indicating biological degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms from all three regions metabolize PS, leading to changes in its crystalline structure and thermal degradation characteristics."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS is a plastic material less available for mealworm consumption due to its high gloss and hardness."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms degrade PS through enzymatic and microbial processes, leading to changes in molecular weights and chemical structures of PS residues."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms were fed PS, and their ability to degrade it was measured through PGF values and other biochemical analyses."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS also experiences significant depolymerization and chemical modification in the gut of mealworms, consistent with observations for PE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms degrade PS through enzymatic activities in their gut microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS showed increasing consumption by mealworms over time, with less negative effects on their mass compared to other plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume and degrade PS materials, demonstrating their ability to survive on PS alone and convert it into frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume and degrade PS, with their efficiency influenced by nutrient supplements and environmental conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms demonstrated the ability to consume all six tested PS waste materials, indicating their potential for biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume PS microplastics, causing alterations in PS's molecular weight as characterized by GPC."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS microplastics similarly affected mealworm survival and growth as observed with PE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume PS as part of their diet, demonstrating their ability to degrade this material."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS impacts the survival, growth, and reproduction of mealworm larvae by altering their energy dynamics."</data>
      <data key="d5">"availability, consumption"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, consumption"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, environmental impact"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, mineralization"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, nutrient utilization"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, organism activity"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, plastic consumption"&lt;SEP&gt;"consumption, viability"&lt;SEP&gt;"dietary consumption, plastic degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"enzymatic degradation, biodegradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"feeding behavior, biodegradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"larval digestion, polymer breakdown"&lt;SEP&gt;"metabolism, degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"molecular weight change, characterization"&lt;SEP&gt;"nutrition, degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"nutritional impact, developmental effects"&lt;SEP&gt;"plastic consumption, insect behavior"&lt;SEP&gt;"plastic consumption, metabolism"&lt;SEP&gt;"plastic degradation, biological function"&lt;SEP&gt;"survival, negative growth"</data>
