    <edge source="&quot;MEALWORMS&quot;" target="&quot;LDPE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">70.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"LDPE caused mealworms to lose less mass than HDPE during the 10-day interval but resulted in greater mass loss at 30 days and less mass gain at 20 days."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms affect LDPE by altering its molecular weight and introducing new chemical groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms were fed LDPE to evaluate its degradability and impact on their growth status."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms from Tai'an and Shenzhen metabolize LDPE, transforming it from a crystalline to an amorphous polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms also degrade LDPE, but at lower rates compared to greater wax moth larvae, suggesting differences in efficiency."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms make holes in LDPE substrates and cause mass loss, though their effectiveness varies depending on experimental conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume LDPE as part of their diet during the experimental setup."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE undergoes limited-extent depolymerization in the digestive system of mealworms, leading to increased molecular weights."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms similarly process LDPE, breaking it down effectively within their digestive systems."</data>
      <data key="d5">"biodegradation, digestion"&lt;SEP&gt;"degradation rate, species comparison"&lt;SEP&gt;"degradation, nutritional impact"&lt;SEP&gt;"dietary consumption, plastic degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"digestion, mass change"&lt;SEP&gt;"digestive efficiency, plastic consumption"&lt;SEP&gt;"metabolism, transformation"&lt;SEP&gt;"oxidation, polymer modification"&lt;SEP&gt;"substrate interaction, degradation"</data>
