    <edge source="&quot;MEALWORMS&quot;" target="&quot;POLYSTYRENE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">273.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Mealworms can degrade and metabolize polystyrene, indicating a clear interaction between the two entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms ingest and biodegrade polystyrene, generating microplastics through biofragmentation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms ingest and degrade polystyrene, harboring bacteria capable of breaking it down."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms, particularly the larvae of Tenebrio molitor, interact with polystyrene through biodegradation processes, breaking it down into simpler forms."&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms, specifically the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor*, have been extensively studied for their unique ability to biodegrade and mineralize polystyrene, a type of plastic waste. This process is facilitated by the gut microbiota present in mealworms, which enables them to break down polystyrene through enzymatic processes. The interaction between mealworms and polystyrene demonstrates a biological relationship where polystyrene serves as a food source for the larvae, allowing them to survive despite its low nutritional value. However, prolonged consumption of polystyrene can lead to high mortality rates in mealworms due to insufficient nutrients.

Research has shown that mealworms degrade polystyrene more effectively when supplemented with additional nutrients such as sucrose or bran. This indicates that while mealworms are capable of utilizing polystyrene as a food source, their physiological health may be compromised without proper dietary support. Furthermore, mealworms exhibit greater physiological changes when fed polystyrene compared to other similar species like superworms, suggesting a distinct effect on their metabolism.

The capability of mealworms to break down polystyrene holds significant promise for addressing plastic waste pollution. By consuming and degrading polystyrene through the action of their gut microorganisms, mealworms contribute to the biodegradation and mineralization of this otherwise persistent material. This highlights their potential role in plastic waste management and offers a biological solution to the growing environmental challenge posed by polystyrene and other plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene is degraded by mealworms, indicating a biological process where the plastic is mineralized in the digestive system of the larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene is degraded by symbiotic bacteria found in the gut of mealworms, indicating their role in solving environmental pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms have the ability to biodegrade polystyrene, demonstrating their role in plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d5">"biodegradation, biofragmentation"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, digestion"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, ecological interaction"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, ecological significance"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, environmental impact"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, environmental remediation"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, environmental solution"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, enzymatic activity"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, insect activity"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, metabolism"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, microorganism activity"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, mineralization"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, nutrient enhancement"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, survival mechanism"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, symbiosis"&lt;SEP&gt;"biological digestion, plastic consumption"&lt;SEP&gt;"dietary impact"&lt;SEP&gt;"nutritional dependency, biodegradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"physiological response, nutrient impact"&lt;SEP&gt;"plastic degradation, enzymatic activity"&lt;SEP&gt;"plastic degradation, symbiosis"</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf28f07f3e46b29e447aae784667c29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a0e22d97687eaef3d31a60f7da6e668&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db0003a7244c51697d0129eb84b8e28e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b</data>
