    <edge source="&quot;ZOPHOBAS ATRATUS&quot;" target="&quot;POLYSTYRENE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">166.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Polystyrene is degraded by Zophobas atratus larvae into microplastics, demonstrating the larva's biodegradation capabilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms (Zophobas atratus) exhibit significant biodegradation capabilities for polystyrene, enhancing plastic waste reduction efforts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms degrade polystyrene but not as effectively as yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus, another insect species, also exhibits the capacity to biodegrade polystyrene effectively."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas Atratus is capable of mineralizing polystyrene, indicating a direct interaction between the organism and the polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas Atratus larvae have demonstrated an ability to biodegrade polystyrene effectively."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus larvae exhibit the ability to degrade low-density polystyrene and polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus larvae contribute to the biodegradation of Polystyrene through enzymatic activities within their gut microbiota."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus larvae have demonstrated the ability to biodegrade polystyrene through enzymatic processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus larvae possess the ability to biodegrade polystyrene through their gut microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus contains gut microorganisms capable of degrading polystyrene, indicating potential for biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus larvae demonstrate the ability to degrade polystyrene, contributing to plastic waste management solutions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus larvae are capable of degrading polystyrene, demonstrating their role in managing plastic waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus larvae exhibit the ability to biodegrade polystyrene, showing a direct interaction between the insect and the polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus larvae exhibit the strongest ability to degrade polystyrene, converting it into low-molecular-weight substances with high efficiency."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus, or superworms, exhibit the capability to biodegrade and mineralize polystyrene, highlighting their role in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus larvae degrade polystyrene through the action of reactive oxygen species and extracellular oxidase from gut microbes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus larvae degrade foam plastics, including polystyrene, altering gut enzyme activities and microbiome composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas atratus exhibits the ability to biodegrade and mineralize polystyrene, demonstrating a direct interaction."</data>
      <data key="d5">"biodegradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, depolymerization"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, efficiency"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, enzymatic activity"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, enzymatic efficiency"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, gut microbiome"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, metabolism"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, mineralization"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, plastic degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, plastic digestion"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, plastic waste"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, plastic-eating capability"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, symbiosis"&lt;SEP&gt;"degradation, insect contribution"&lt;SEP&gt;"plastic degradation, environmental sustainability"&lt;SEP&gt;"plastic degradation, enzymatic activity"</data>
